{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md | sed -n '260,340p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md | sed -n '130,190p'\"","aggregated_output":"   130\t\n   131\t**AC-1.2:** On `session_start` that resolves to an existing thread (via `--continue`, `--session`, or a picker selection), the extension initializes against that thread by resolving it through the registry. No second thread is created for an already-resolved thread.\n   132\t\n   133\t**AC-1.3:** Across all hooks, the extension holds only plain data (thread reference, file path, told-the-user flags) between events. It retains no PI context or session-manager object across hook boundaries; each handler uses the fresh context PI provides. A session replacement (new/resume/fork) that invalidates prior context objects does not break capture.\n   134\t\n   135\t**AC-1.4:** On `session_shutdown`, the extension disposes the LHC instance with flush and cleanup. A subsequent run that resolves the same thread finds it complete through the last event recorded before shutdown — no trailing loss.\n   136\t\n   137\t**AC-1.5:** On reload (extension torn down and re-initialized while the same session continues), the extension reconstructs the thread reference from durable state (the resolved thread id), not from retained in-memory objects, and capture continues on the same thread.\n   138\t\n   139\t**AC-1.6:** Launch resolves the thread by mode: no flag creates a new thread; `--session <id>` resolves a named thread by full or partial id; `--continue`/`-c` resolves the most recently created thread; an ambiguous or unresolvable id fails with an actionable message rather than silently creating a new thread.\n   140\t\n   141\t**AC-1.7:** `--resume`/`-r` lists threads scoped to the current working directory, each shown with its title and creation time, and resolves the operator's selection. With no threads for the cwd, the picker reports an empty list rather than failing.\n   142\t\n   143\t---\n   144\t\n   145\t## Flow 2: Event Capture and Turn Derivation\n   146\t\n   147\tFrom the first prompt on, the extension maps each finalized PI message to LHC intake events and records them in order. PI fires a turn boundary per agent step; an LHC turn spans a whole exchange (one user prompt and everything the agent did in response). The converter derives LHC turn boundaries from PI traffic rather than copying PI's. Capture is duplicate-safe; writable-thread failures become durable gaps, store-unavailable failures become visible extension diagnostics, and neither breaks the session.\n   148\t\n   149\t```mermaid\n   150\tsequenceDiagram\n   151\t  participant PI as PI session\n   152\t  participant E as converter\n   153\t  participant L as LHC intake\n   154\t  PI->>E: message_end(user)\n   155\t  E->>L: user_prompt event (opens LHC turn)\n   156\t  PI->>E: message_end(assistant [thinking,text,toolCall×N])\n   157\t  E->>L: assistant_thinking, assistant_text, tool_call×N\n   158\t  PI->>E: message_end(toolResult) ×N\n   159\t  E->>L: tool_result×N (correlated by toolCallId)\n   160\t  Note over PI,E: PI fires turn_end per agent step — NOT mapped 1:1\n   161\t  PI->>E: agent run completes (no further tool calls)\n   162\t  E->>L: turn_end event (closes the LHC turn)\n   163\t```\n   164\t\n   165\t**AC-2.1:** Every PI message finalized through `message_end` — user, assistant, tool result — is mapped to LHC intake events and recorded in source order. An assistant message fans out to per-content-part events in thinking → text → tool-call order: `assistant_thinking`, `assistant_text`, and one `tool_call` per call.\n   166\t\n   167\t**AC-2.2:** LHC turn boundaries are derived from PI traffic, not from PI's per-agent-step `turn_end`. One LHC turn spans a user prompt and all subsequent agent activity until the next user prompt or the end of the agent run. The converter emits exactly one LHC `turn_end` event per LHC turn, at the agent run's completion — never one per PI `turn_end`, and never keyed off PI's per-agent-run `turnIndex` as a session counter.\n   168\t\n   169\t**AC-2.3:** Parallel tool calls are captured with correct correlation: when one assistant message carries multiple `tool_call` parts and their results complete out of arrival order, each `tool_result` event is matched to its call by `toolCallId`, not by arrival order.\n   170\t\n   171\t**AC-2.4:** A tool result carrying an error is captured as a `tool_result` event with its error content and an error flag set. No event is dropped because a tool failed.\n   172\t\n   173\t**AC-2.5:** A graceful interrupt — a complete turn PI marks aborted, with partial assistant content preserved — is captured whole: the partial content is recorded and the aborted disposition is carried through. The interrupted content is not discarded.\n   174\t\n   175\t**AC-2.6:** Capture is duplicate-safe. Re-delivered events (reload, crash-replay) are recognized by idempotency key and skipped rather than recorded twice. On normal resume PI re-delivers no historical events, so duplication only arises on the reload and replay paths.\n   176\t\n   177\t**AC-2.7:** A capture failure does not break the PI session, and does not vanish silently. When the thread is writable, a malformed or unmappable event records a durable, queryable gap that surfaces in thread health. When the thread store itself is unavailable, the failure surfaces as an extension diagnostic / health signal rather than a durable gap (the record cannot be written), and no exception propagates into the PI hook. Either way the session continues, and capture adds no perceptible latency to interactive use.\n   178\t\n   179\t**AC-2.8:** Runtime changes that PI fires only in-stream — model selection (`model_select`) and thinking-level selection (`thinking_level_select`) — are captured in order as `runtime_note` events carrying the change (the new model or level, and the previous one). These are recorded at the moment they fire because no durable record holds them otherwise; nothing in this epic consumes them, and their presence in the thread is what lets later epics attribute a turn to the model that produced it.\n   180\t\n   181\t---\n   182\t\n   183\t## Flow 3: Fork as New Thread\n   184\t\n   185\tA user forks a PI session. PI creates a new session file and leaves the original untouched. A fork produces a new LHC thread, seeded by replaying the source thread's recorded events up to the fork point. The source thread is never written. Derived forms carry over when their provenance proves the reuse safe; otherwise they requeue on the new thread.\n   186\t\n   187\t**AC-3.1:** Forking a PI session creates a new LHC thread. The source thread receives no writes — it is unchanged by the fork.\n   188\t\n   189\t**AC-3.2:** The new thread is seeded by replaying the source thread's recorded events up to the fork point. The seeded thread's read-back (events, messages, turns) matches the source's read-back through that point.\n   190\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md | sed -n '260,340p'\"","aggregated_output":"   260\tThe converter emits LHC `MessageEventInput` events. Each carries `eventKind`, `idempotencyKey`, `actor`, `harness`, and a kind-fixed `payload`. The seven kinds and their payloads:\n   261\t\n   262\t| Event kind | Payload |\n   263\t|------------|---------|\n   264\t| `user_prompt` | `{ text }` |\n   265\t| `assistant_text` | `{ text }` |\n   266\t| `assistant_thinking` | `{ text }` |\n   267\t| `tool_call` | `{ toolCallId, toolName, arguments }` |\n   268\t| `tool_result` | `{ toolCallId, content, isError? }` |\n   269\t| `runtime_note` | `{ text }` |\n   270\t| `turn_end` | `{}` (empty) |\n   271\t\n   272\t### PI message → LHC event mapping\n   273\t\n   274\t| PI message (`message_end`) | LHC events emitted (in order) |\n   275\t|----------------------------|-------------------------------|\n   276\t| user `[text]` | one `user_prompt` |\n   277\t| assistant `[thinking?, text?, toolCall×N]` | `assistant_thinking` (if present), then `assistant_text` (if present), then one `tool_call` per call |\n   278\t| toolResult `[text]`, `isError?` | one `tool_result` (correlated by `toolCallId`) |\n   279\t| (agent run completes) | one `turn_end` — see turn derivation below |\n   280\t\n   281\tImage and file-reference content parts: LHC intake payloads are text today. Handling (placeholder vs. payload-schema extension) is an M0 decision (A-7), not a tech design question; until that decision lands, the converter must not silently drop a content part without recording the omission.\n   282\t\n   283\tRuntime-change events: PI's `model_select` and `thinking_level_select` map to `runtime_note` events (AC-2.8). The note text is structured enough to recover the change — the new value and the previous one — but it rides the existing `runtime_note` kind, adding no new intake kind.\n   284\t\n   285\t| PI event | LHC event |\n   286\t|----------|-----------|\n   287\t| `model_select` `{model, previousModel}` | one `runtime_note` recording the model change |\n   288\t| `thinking_level_select` `{level, previousLevel}` | one `runtime_note` recording the level change |\n   289\t\n   290\t### Turn derivation\n   291\t\n   292\tPI fires `turn_start`/`turn_end` per agent step, and resets `turnIndex` to 0 at the start of each agent run. An LHC turn is a whole exchange. The converter derives LHC turns from PI traffic:\n   293\t\n   294\t- An LHC turn **opens** on a `user_prompt` event.\n   295\t- It stays open across every agent step — each PI `turn_end` within the run is **ignored as an LHC turn boundary**.\n   296\t- It **closes** with one LHC `turn_end` event when the agent run completes (no further tool calls; PI's `agent_end`), or implicitly when the next `user_prompt` arrives.\n   297\t\n   298\t**Worked example.** A user asks one question that triggers two tool calls, then gets a final answer. PI emits:\n   299\t\n   300\t```\n   301\tturn_start(0)\n   302\t  message_end(user [text])\n   303\t  message_end(assistant [thinking, toolCall, toolCall])\n   304\t  message_end(toolResult) × 2\n   305\tturn_end(0)\n   306\tturn_start(1)\n   307\t  message_end(assistant [text])\n   308\tturn_end(1)\n   309\tagent_end\n   310\t```\n   311\t\n   312\tThe converter emits **one** LHC turn:\n   313\t\n   314\t```\n   315\tuser_prompt, assistant_thinking, tool_call, tool_call, tool_result, tool_result,\n   316\tassistant_text, turn_end\n   317\t```\n   318\t\n   319\tPI's `turn_end(0)` and `turn_end(1)` produce no LHC `turn_end`; the single LHC `turn_end` is emitted at `agent_end`. Session order comes from source-event order through the converter, never from PI's per-agent-run `turnIndex`.\n   320\t\n   321\t### Abort and crash shapes\n   322\t\n   323\t| Shape | PI signal | Converter behavior |\n   324\t|-------|-----------|--------------------|\n   325\t| Graceful interrupt | assistant `message_end` with partial content and `stopReason: aborted`; `turn_end` `aborted`; `agent_end` fires | Capture the partial content; carry the aborted disposition; close the LHC turn normally (a complete-but-aborted turn) |\n   326\t| Hard kill | user `message_end` only; no assistant close, no `turn_end`, no `agent_end` | On reattach, find a dangling open turn with a trailing user prompt; tolerate it (LHC `turn_end`-with-no-open-turn is a no-op); resume capture without duplicating the recorded prefix |\n   327\t\n   328\t### Idempotency key\n   329\t\n   330\tEach emitted event carries a stable idempotency key so re-delivery (reload, crash-replay) is recognized and skipped. Construction precedence (from the POC mapper, carried forward): PI entry id (`pi:<session>:entry:<entryId>`) → provider response id / tool-call id → content fingerprint as last resort. The exact construction is tech design; the contract is that the key is stable across re-delivery of the same logical event and unique across distinct events.\n   331\t\n   332\t### Thread resolution and fork lineage\n   333\t\n   334\tThe thread is resolved at launch through the registry catalog. The four launch modes and their registry operations:\n   335\t\n   336\t| Launch | Registry operation |\n   337\t|--------|--------------------|\n   338\t| no flag | `newThread({ filePath, cwd, title })` — new thread, registered |\n   339\t| `--session <id>` | `resolve({ threadId })`, full or partial-id match |\n   340\t| `--continue` / `-c` | resolve the most recently created thread |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
