.message_end(b.ctx, makeMessageEnd(makeUserMessage(\"second after resume\")));\npackages/pi-lhc/test/capture/reattach-idempotency.test.ts:73:    await a.connector.handlers.message_end(a.ctx, makeMessageEnd(makeUserMessage(\"first\")));\npackages/pi-lhc/test/capture/reattach-idempotency.test.ts:79:    await b.connector.handlers.message_end(b.ctx, makeMessageEnd(makeUserMessage(\"second after reload\")));\npackages/pi-lhc/test/capture/reattach-idempotency.test.ts:91:    await a.connector.handlers.message_end(a.ctx, makeMessageEnd(makeUserMessage(\"first\")));\npackages/pi-lhc/test/capture/reattach-idempotency.test.ts:92:    await a.connector.handlers.message_end(a.ctx, makeMessageEnd(makeAssistantMessage({ text: \"answer one\" })));\npackages/pi-lhc/test/capture/reattach-idempotency.test.ts:100:    await b.connector.handlers.message_end(b.ctx, makeMessageEnd(makeUserMessage(\"second\")));\npackages/pi-lhc/test/capture/reattach-idempotency.test.ts:101:    await b.connector.handlers.message_end(b.ctx, makeMessageEnd(makeAssistantMessage({ text: \"answer two\" })));\npackages/pi-lhc/test/capture/reattach-idempotency.test.ts:133:    const userEvents = mapMessage(makeUserMessage(\"hello\"), { piSessionId: \"sess\", entryId: \"s0\" });\npackages/pi-lhc/src/capture/map-message.ts:11:import { eventKey } from \"./idempotency.js\";\npackages/pi-lhc/src/capture/map-message.ts:24:  entryId?: string;\npackages/pi-lhc/src/capture/map-message.ts:73:    entryId: ctx.entryId,\npackages/pi-lhc/src/capture/map-message.ts:90:  return { eventKind: kind, idempotencyKey: key, actor, harness: HARNESS, payload: { text } };\npackages/pi-lhc/src/capture/map-message.ts:101:    idempotencyKey: key,\npackages/pi-lhc/src/capture/map-message.ts:116:    idempotencyKey: key,\npackages/pi-lhc/src/capture/converter.ts:3:import { fingerprint } from \"./idempotency.js\";\npackages/pi-lhc/src/capture/converter.ts:52:    idempotencyKey: `pi-lhc:gap:${fingerprint(JSON.stringify(events), reason)}`,\npackages/pi-lhc/test/capture/abort.test.ts:34:    await connector.handlers.message_end(ctx, makeMessageEnd(makeUserMessage(\"write a long essay\")));\npackages/pi-lhc/test/capture/abort.test.ts:35:    await connector.handlers.message_end(\npackages/pi-lhc/src/capture/turn-accumulator.ts:2:import { eventKey } from \"./idempotency.js\";\npackages/pi-lhc/src/capture/turn-accumulator.ts:29:  // A monotonic counter giving each emitted `turn_end` a distinct idempotency\npackages/pi-lhc/src/capture/turn-accumulator.ts:58:      entryId: `turn:${this.closedCount}`,\npackages/pi-lhc/src/capture/turn-accumulator.ts:66:        idempotencyKey: key,\npackages/pi-lhc/test/smoke/extension-load.test.ts:86:    await connector.handlers.message_end(syntheticCtx(\"ctx-2\"), makeMessageEnd(makeUserMessage(\"hi\")));\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:30:| I-5 | TDQ-5 | Converter structure — how it consumes `message_end` and emits batched `MessageEventInput[]`. | **Resolved.** The converter is two pure stages plus one stateful accumulator: `mapMessage` (one PI `AgentMessage` → ordered `MessageEventInput[]`, no I/O) and `deriveTurnClose` (decides when to emit `turn_end`), driven by a per-session `TurnAccumulator` that tracks the open LHC turn across `message_end`/`agent_end`. Batches flush through `intakeStream.messageEvents(threadRef, events)`. See Flow 2 design. |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:39:The shape of the work is set by two systems that already exist and are not being changed. PI fires lifecycle hooks (`session_start`, `message_end`, `turn_end`, `agent_end`, `session_shutdown`, fork/switch) and an extension subscribes to them; this is a fixed, versioned contract verified against v0.79.2 by live recon. The LHC SDK is constructed once per session (`initLhc`, the agreed rename of the current `createSdk`), exposes operation surfaces (`intakeStream`, `threads`, `inspect`, the background work scheduler), and persists one SQLite file per thread. The connector is the tissue between them: it translates PI's events into LHC intake operations, and translates LHC's need for inference back into PI's authenticated model registry. It owns no context state of its own beyond a thread reference and a few told-the-user flags, because PI tears down and replaces its session objects on every new/resume/fork transition, and a cached reference to one of those objects is a latent crash.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:45:The connector also inherits a body of prior art. A POC extension (`src/context-steward/pi/`, ~3,900 lines across the mapper, the parallel-event intake, and the extension shell) already solved much of the hard mapping — idempotency-key construction, parallel-tool correlation, unsupported-part degradation — against an older LHC storage API. This epic productionizes that logic against the current, leaner SDK surface (plain-string `actor`/`harness`, per-event `idempotencyKey`, the seven-kind `MessageEventInput` union), so the converter is a rewrite informed by the POC, not a port of it. The POC's stale-context handling, by contrast, dies here: the tech arch's plain-data-only rule replaces it.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:56:    H[\"hook surface\\nsession_start, message_end,\\nturn_end, agent_end, shutdown, fork\"]\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:93:| `message_end` | `message: AgentMessage` (user / assistant / toolResult) | Map to `MessageEventInput[]`, batch into intake |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:98:| `session_before_fork` | `entryId`, `position` | Capture the fork point for replay-seeding |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:108:| `intakeStream` | `messageEvents(threadRef, MessageEventInput[])` → `BatchResult` | Record capture; dedup by idempotency key |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:159:      idempotency.ts               NEW  pure: stable per-event key construction\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:189:| `capture/map-message.ts` | NEW | Pure map of one `AgentMessage` to ordered events; fan-out; parallel-tool correlation; error-result; abort; unsupported→runtime_note | idempotency | AC-2.1, AC-2.3, AC-2.4, AC-2.5 |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:192:| `capture/idempotency.ts` | NEW | Construct a stable key per event so re-delivery dedups | — | AC-2.6 |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:213:  PI->>IDX: message_end / turn_end / agent_end\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:227:Each flow below opens with the context that makes its mechanics make sense, then a sequence diagram annotated with the ACs it satisfies, then a skeleton-requirements table and the TC approaches. The depth is uneven on purpose: turn derivation, idempotency, reattach, the inference adapter, and fork-replay carry the design's risk and get the most room; thread creation and config loading are mechanical and get less.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:283:  message_end(user [text])               assistant_thinking\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:284:  message_end(assistant                  tool_call (id=a)\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:286:  message_end(toolResult b)              tool_result (id=b)\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:287:  message_end(toolResult a)              tool_result (id=a)\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:290:  message_end(assistant [text])\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:303:  PI->>CV: message_end(user)\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:307:  PI->>CV: message_end(assistant [thinking,toolCall×2])\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:309:  PI->>CV: message_end(toolResult) ×2 (out of order)\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:317:  Note over CV,IS: AC-2.6 dedup by idempotency key — AC-2.7 failure to gap\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:326:| Idempotency key | `capture/idempotency.ts` | `eventKey(parts): string` | pure; deterministic |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:329:The idempotency key gets real depth because dedup correctness (AC-2.6) and crash-replay both depend on it. Construction precedence, carried from the POC and adapted to the SDK's per-event-key field: PI entry id when present (`pi:<session>:<entryId>:<block>:<kind>`), else provider `responseId` (assistant) or `toolCallId` (tool result), else a `role:timestamp:content-hash` fallback. The contract is that the key is identical across re-delivery of the same logical event and distinct across different events; the SDK's `messageEvents` returns `skipped` with `skipReason: duplicate_idempotency_key` for a re-delivered key, which is the dedup mechanism the test asserts against.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:333:A fork produces a new LHC thread seeded by replay, and never writes the source. Detection does not depend on a `session_start` fork reason: recon found print-mode `--fork` reports `reason=startup`. The fork point comes from the `session_before_fork` hook (`entryId`, `position`) when present. In Epic 1, PI still runs its own session, so PI's session tree (the `parentId` chain) is available as additional fork evidence when the hook is absent. This is an Epic-1 convenience, not the permanent design: the long-term home for fork lineage is LHC thread metadata (Feature 2+, once PI's session file goes vestigial), so the design must not harden PI's session tree into a permanent dependency.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:459:export interface MapCtx { piSessionId: string; entryId?: string; }\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:479:// capture/idempotency.ts — AC-2.6\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:481:  piSessionId: string; entryId?: string; responseId?: string;\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:542:| Idempotency / Retry | `capture/idempotency.test.ts` | Replaying the same corpus twice produces no duplicate events; re-delivered keys come back `skipped` | TC checks dedup once; the hazard is re-delivery under reload/replay being safe |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:580:Idempotency-key construction is deterministic but its golden cases live with the idempotency risk test rather than here.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:594:   /          \\ Module tests: map-message, turn-accumulator, idempotency,\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:656:**Scope:** the converter, fan-out, turn derivation, runtime metadata capture, parallel correlation, error/abort capture, dedup, failure isolation. **ACs:** 2.1–2.8. **Relevant sections:** Flow 2, `capture/*`, Idempotency + Atomicity risks, Deterministic Boundaries (turn derivation). **Architecture-risk tests:** idempotency double-replay, mid-batch failure isolation. **Risk shape:** the highest-risk chunk; turn derivation is the load-bearing mechanic; capture failure must isolate.\npackages/pi-lhc/src/capture/runtime-changes.ts:3:import { eventKey } from \"./idempotency.js\";\npackages/pi-lhc/src/capture/runtime-changes.ts:41:    idempotencyKey: eventKey({\npackages/pi-lhc/src/capture/runtime-changes.ts:43:      entryId: ctx.entryId,\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/artifacts/02-event-capture-and-turn-derivation/004-verify.json:1:{\"command\":\"story-verify\",\"version\":1,\"status\":\"ok\",\"outcome\":\"revise\",\"result\":{\"resultId\":\"dcbc4543-77eb-48ca-ae16-47342b85f1a1\",\"role\":\"story_verifier\",\"provider\":\"codex\",\"model\":\"gpt-5.5\",\"sessionId\":\"019ec517-957e-7520-99ad-8c58c7c12cc1\",\"continuation\":{\"provider\":\"codex\",\"sessionId\":\"019ec517-957e-7520-99ad-8c58c7c12cc1\",\"storyId\":\"02-event-capture-and-turn-derivation\"},\"mode\":\"initial\",\"story\":{\"id\":