s:284,357) and turns.requeue (src/domains/turns/index.ts:284,383) with already_queued noop, version-scoped idempotent item ids; (2) reason-class persistence — retryable flag flows through work-queue terminal failure path (src/tech-utils/work-queue/index.ts:383-394, scheduler.ts:148-174, handlers); FC-0.4 (passing since Story 0) proves distinguishable transient/permanent classes on read-back via production drains. No Epic 02 patch needed.\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/team-impl-log.md:46:- Story Title: Story 0: Foundation\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/team-impl-log.md:156:- Story 3 hard gate (cross-epic): before 03-readiness-sweep starts, verify (1) Epic 02 requeue patch landed (live-work-only queue rows), (2) terminal-failure write path persists a classifiable reason class. If opaque, halt story and surface the named Epic 02 patch (stamp provider `retryable` at exhaustion). FC-0.4 in Story 0 is the fixture-side proof.\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/team-impl-log.md:165:5. **storyRunSelection/baseline mismatch.** `validate` reported `baselineBeforeCurrentStory: 365` (workspace-wide test-file count), while the story-lead final package reported baseline 240→255 (lhc package test count). Two different baseline definitions under one name; skill/CLI should pin which one impl-lead compares.\\\\n./packages/lhc/test/fixtures.test.ts:112:// ── Story 0 (Epic 02): provider double, shared types, fixture builders ──\\\\n./packages/lhc/test/fixtures.test.ts:384:  it(\\\\\\\"FC-0.6: the damaged-source thread reads back the corruption it claims (Epic 01 definition)\\\\\\\", async () => {\\\\n./packages/lhc/test/work-execution.test.ts:207:    // …and the burst coalesced: one initial pass plus exactly one follow-up,\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/05-derivation-work-queueing.md:132:### Definition of Done\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/05-derivation-work-queueing.md:133:<!-- Jira: Definition of Done or Acceptance Criteria footer -->\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/epic.md:204:- **AC-4.6**: A derivation whose handler finds source damage (per Epic 01's corruption definitions) lands `blocked` with a reason naming the damage; drain continues past it; re-queue requests for a `blocked` form are refused with that reason.\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/epic.md:253:Delete removes what should not be in the readable record. It is projection-level: the message (or turn) drops from reads and membership while the source events remain in the event log. This is the one sanctioned exception to Epic 01's frozen-membership rule, and an exception for the readable record only — source event membership stays auditable through event read-back, and membership is only ever shrunk, never re-cut. Deleting a message that initiates a turn is refused toward the turn delete: a turn is a prompt and what came back for it, so removing the prompt removes the turn, explicitly, through `turns`. Cascades are bounded by structure: one turn, one chunk; boundaries never move.\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/epic.md:256:2. Validation: target exists, turn closed; message delete additionally checks the target does not initiate a turn\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/epic.md:267:- **AC-6.3**: Deleting a message that initiates a turn is refused with an error naming the turn and the turn-delete path; nothing changes.\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/epic.md:278:- **TC-6.3** (AC-6.3): Delete a turn-initiating prompt → refused; error names the turn id and turns-delete; full read-back unchanged.\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/epic.md:353:| delete message | `messages` SDK + CLI | message id | exists; turn closed; not turn-initiating | removed id, cleared forms, queued work |\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/epic.md:361:| `message_initiates_turn` | caller_error | message delete targeting a turn-initiating prompt |\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/epic.md:419:### Story 0: Foundation\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/epic.md:425:**Prerequisite:** Story 0\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/tech-design.md:25:| Transient classification basis | AC-3.3, epic TD-Q9 | By **reason-code classification table** (per TD-Q9), owned by `sweep.ts`: rate-limit / timeout / provider-unavailable classes ⇒ transient; content-refusal / validation / `unknown_work_kind` ⇒ permanent; **unclassified codes ⇒ permanent** (reported in the receipt as unclassified, never requeued — the conservative default; the other way re-spends on a permanent failure at every compact). Attempt count distinguishes only retrying-vs-exhausted, never repairability. Dependency: exhausted forms must persist the final failure's reason class, not a bare retry-exhausted marker — the Story 0 fixture proves the two failed states carry distinguishable codes; if Epic 02's persisted reason is opaque, that is an Epic 02 patch named at the Story 3 gate. | Resolved — clarified |\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/tech-design.md:115:Boundary rules, one addition: `thread-view` imports `messages`/`turns` through their `index.ts` surfaces only, never internals; nothing imports `thread-view/internal`; **and `intake-stream` imports the `thread-view` surface** (the advance registration, Flow 4) — sanctioned by the surface-call rule, named here so Story 0's boundary gate expects it. The check-boundaries script gains both lines: thread-view's allowed imports, and intake-stream's added thread-view-surface line. Note for the script: this creates the domain graph's first surface-level cycle (intake → thread-view → messages ← intake) — runtime-safe (registration-then-flush, no import-time execution), but if the script models a DAG it needs a sanctioned-cycle annotation, not a silent pass.\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/tech-design.md:295:## Interface Definitions\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/tech-design.md:257:**Flows 5/6 — Mutations.** One transaction (`runInTransaction`): validate (exists via deleted-filtered read; turn closed; for message-delete: not turn-initiating — else `message_initiates_turn` naming the turn and the turns-delete path), apply record change (content+blocks+token re-stamp for edit; `deleted_at` stamp for deletes), run cascade (DD-8): bump source version + `pending` on cleared forms, drop forms whose subject is deleted (state rows removed — dropped, not failed, AC-6.6), supersede-delete still-queued old-version items (issue 1), enqueue replacements with target source version, register poke. Claimed old-version items may still finish; DD-3 discards their stale results because their source version no longer matches. Result reports changed/cleared/dropped/queued/superseded. Refusals: `turn_open`, `message_not_found`/`turn_not_found`, second delete of the same id → `message_not_found` by the filtered read (AC-6.7's \\\\\\\"refusal, not silent success\\\\\\\").\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/tech-design.md:395://  message_initiates_turn   caller_error      delete refused toward turns.delete\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/tech-design.md:479:| 6 Delete | deleted_at stamps, filtered reads everywhere, initiating-prompt refusal, turn delete, empty chunk | TC-6.1 events-survive-delete; TC-6.5 bounded cascade; TC-6.7 triple-refusal |\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/tech-design.md:490:| E02-FIX2 | AC-3.4 spec-compliance restoration (not a deviation) | `composeRenderingInput` groups maximal runs of consecutive tool activity into one `RenderingPart` + one tool-run receipt with an outcome-explicit run account (tools, call count, per-call outcomes; mixed outcomes stay explicit). Per-message mechanical stamping is untouched. | The initial build shimmed one part/receipt per tool message; AC-3.4 and story §Anti-Shim require grouped run accounts. Restores spec compliance. | Done (fix-batch-002) |\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/00-foundation.md:1:# Story 0: Package Foundations\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/00-foundation.md:74:| Smoke tests | `test/smoke.test.ts`, `test/fixtures.test.ts` (names inferred; Chunk 0 scope) |\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/00-foundation.md:94:| FC-0.3 | `test/smoke.test.ts` | help, unknown command, stub failure shape |\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/00-foundation.md:96:| FC-0.5 | `test/smoke.test.ts` | golden token counts, repeated runs identical |\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/00-foundation.md:129:- Targeted: `pnpm test -- test/smoke.test.ts test/fixtures.test.ts`\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/00-foundation.md:137:### Definition of Done\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/00-foundation.md:138:<!-- Jira: Definition of Done or Acceptance Criteria footer -->\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/stories/01-queue-execution-drain.md:18:- Dispatch through the Story 0 handler map; unknown kind lands `failed_terminal` with stable reason, drain continues\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/stories/01-queue-execution-drain.md:26:**Dependencies:** Story 0 (handler map, migrated queue fields, double, fixtures).\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/stories/01-queue-execution-drain.md:164:### Definition of Done\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/stories/01-queue-execution-drain.md:165:<!-- Jira: Definition of Done or Acceptance Criteria footer -->\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/02-event-recording-validation-idempotency.md:66:Strictness falls out of schema construction, not remembered rules: every level (envelope, event, payload) is a closed Effect Schema struct, so unknown-field rejection is a property of the definitions. Server-generated fields are denied by name with their own reason string — the old MVP's silent-root-field-drop bug class gets named when it appears, not just rejected.\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/02-event-recording-validation-idempotency.md:134:- Order counter initializes from `MAX(event_order)` at transaction start; only recorded events increment it.\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/02-event-recording-validation-idempotency.md:146:- Registration/default path: CLI router routes to the real surface, replacing Story 0 stubs; SDK exports the operation.\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/02-event-recording-validation-idempotency.md:159:### Definition of Done\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/02-event-recording-validation-idempotency.md:160:<!-- Jira: Definition of Done or Acceptance Criteria footer -->\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/stories/06-message-turn-delete.md:13:**Objective:** The record's removal mutations ship on the cascade machinery Story 5 proved. Message delete drops the message from reads and membership (source event retained), cascades like an edit minus the message's own forms (dropped, not rebuilt). Deleting a turn's initiating prompt is refused with a pointed error naming the turn — `turns.delete` is the operation for that intent, removing the turn and its messages from reads, dropping its forms, shrinking its chunk. Membership only ever shrinks; boundaries never re-cut.\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/stories/06-message-turn-delete.md:16:- `messages.delete` (SDK + CLI): closed-turn non-initiating messages; message drops from reads and turn membership; source event remains in event read-back; the message's own forms drop (no rebuild of deleted content); upward cascade re-queues turn + chunk forms (minus-one composition)\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/stories/06-message-turn-delete.md:17:- Prompt protection: deleting a turn's initiating user prompt is refused with an error naming the turn and the right operation (`turns.delete`)\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/stories/06-message-turn-delete.md:35:- **AC-6.3**: Deleting a message that initiates a turn is refused with an error naming the turn and the turn-delete path; nothing changes.\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/stories/06-message-turn-delete.md:36:  - **TC-6.3** (AC-6.3): Delete a turn-initiating prompt → refused; error names the turn id and turns-delete; full read-back unchanged.\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/stories/06-message-turn-delete.md:55:Delete's cascade differs from edit's in one rule: the deleted subject's own forms **drop** (state rows removed — nothing to rebuild from a deleted source) while everything upward re-queues for minus-one composition. The prompt-protection refusal (`message_initiates_turn`, naming the turn and the turns-delete path) routes whole-exchange intent to `turns.deleteTurn` — a turn is a prompt and what came back for it; no prompt, no turn. Membership only ever shrinks; boundaries never re-cut (the sanctioned exception to Epic 01's frozen membership, and it shrinks in place).\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/stories/06-message-turn-delete.md:77:| Storage | `deleted_at` columns (landed in Story 0's migration; first writes here) |\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/stories/06-message-turn-delete.md:96:| TC-6.3 | `test/mutations.test.ts` | prompt delete refused `message_initiates_turn`, names turn + turns-delete path |\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/stories/06-message-turn-delete.md:153:### Definition of Done\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/stories/06-message-turn-delete.md:154:<!-- Jira: Definition of Done or Acceptance Criteria footer -->\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/04-inspection/artifacts/02-inspect-domain-overview-and-health/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-003.md:110:- [tech-design.md §Interface Definitions](/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/tech-design.md:151), lines 151-213\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/04-inspection/artifacts/02-inspect-domain-overview-and-health/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-003.md:162:### Definition of Done\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/04-inspection/artifacts/02-inspect-domain-overview-and-health/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-003.md:163:<!-- Jira: Definition of Done or Acceptance Criteria footer -->\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/04-inspection/artifacts/02-inspect-domain-overview-and-health/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-003.md:194:Fixture: Epic 03's tool-heavy fixture reused; extended in place (Story 2) with a mutation-in-flight variant — one edit applied, drain *not* settled, so cleared-pending states are real production states, not hand-written rows (FC-fidelity rule carried from Epic 03 Story 0: states reached through intake → mutate → partial drain, never direct `derived_form` writes).\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/04-inspection/artifacts/02-inspect-domain-overview-and-health/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-003.md:283:    mode: \\\\\\\"initial\\\\\\\"\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/04-inspection/artifacts/02-inspect-domain-overview-and-health/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-003.md:334:        - \\\\\\\"Definition of Done: SDK exports inspect, CLI routes lhc inspect overview/health, and a focused dist CLI probe verified overview/health JSON equals SDK results.\\\\\\\"\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/04-inspection/artifacts/02-inspect-domain-overview-and-health/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-003.md:335:        - \\\\\\\"Production path audit: no material fake adapter, mock path, shim, fallback, or placeholder was found in inspect.overview or inspect.health runtime paths; the inspect.view not_implemented skeleton is explicitly Story 3 scope.\\\\\\\"\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/04-inspection/artifacts/02-inspect-domain-overview-and-health/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-003.md:361:      - \\\\\\\"Story 3 explicitly owns threadView.describe, inspect.view, and lhc inspect view; the current inspect.view structured not_implemented result matches the existing surface-skeleton convention and is outside Story 2 readiness.\\\\\\\"\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/04-inspection/artifacts/02-inspect-domain-overview-and-health/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-003.md:402:    planSummary: \\\\\\\"Created the inspect domain as a pure surface consumer: shared report shapes in src/shared/inspect.ts; overview composing threads.info + listEvents + listMessages({includeDeleted}) + listTurns/listChunks + both owners' reports + status/pull.meta into the full InspectOverview (absent pieces as zeros/nulls, one composition path, no shape branches); health composing messages.report + turns.report into owner/kind/state counts, failure detail (reason/attempts/lastError from form metadata or queue join), repairPreview (= failed-not-blocked, reported never executed), and queue counts from the same entries' live joins (consistent by construction with pending+retrying). inspect.view ships as the structured not_implemented stub (Story 3). CLI lhc inspect overview|health wired as thin argv-to-SDK wrappers; sdk.ts gains the seam-scoped inspect namespace and shared-type exports. check-boundaries gains inspect's pinned acyclic edge set plus the Epic 04 source check forbidding sqlite/raw-SQL in domains/inspect/**. Fixture extended in place with mutationInFlightVariant (compact + one production edit, drain not settled) and mixedStateVariantThread (ready/failed-transient/failed-permanent/blocked/pending all via intake + scripted provider failures + sanctioned corruption + partial drain). Shared read-only delta helper (observableState/expectReadOnly) lands in test/fixtures and wraps the new ops plus Story 1's list/show retroactively. Smoke-tested the CLI end to end on a fresh thread.\\\\\\\"\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/04-inspection/artifacts/02-inspect-domain-overview-and-health/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-003.md:406:        reason: \\\\\\\"New: InspectOverview, ViewContentsReport, HealthReport load-bearing shapes (tech design §Interface Definitions)\\\\\\\"\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/04-inspection/artifacts/02-inspect-domain-overview-and-health/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-003.md:409:        reason: \\\\\\\"New inspect surface: overview/health real (touch-suppressed pure reads), view as structured not_implemented stub until Story 3\\\\\\\"\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/04-inspection/artifacts/02-inspect-domain-overview-and-health/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-003.md:483:        - \\\\\\\"threads.info initially returned a state_corruption result under a new error-code semantic for the unreachable lost-metadata-row branch; changed to the existing storageFailure so the story adds no error codes (story contract: 'Error codes: no additions')\\\\\\\"\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/04-inspection/artifacts/02-inspect-domain-overview-and-health/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-003.md:484:        - \\\\\\\"Compacted-overview test initially used default compact (sweep on), which would requeue the transiently-failed form and move the derivation counts; pinned to compact({ sweep: false }) so the two manufactured failed states stay failed and counts assert exactly\\\\\\\"\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/04-inspection/artifacts/02-inspect-domain-overview-and-health/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-003.md:494:      - \\\\\\\"inspect.view stub is routed in the CLI (returns the structured not_implemented result) but deliberately omitted from --help until Story 3 lands the real report.\\\\\\\"\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/04-inspection/artifacts/02-inspect-domain-overview-and-health/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-003.md:670:State: initialized\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/04-inspection/stories/03-view-contents-report.md:74:- [tech-design.md §Interface Definitions](/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/tech-design.md:151), lines 151-213\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/04-inspection/stories/03-view-contents-report.md:127:### Definition of Done\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/04-inspection/stories/03-view-contents-report.md:128:<!-- Jira: Definition of Done or Acceptance Criteria footer -->\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/stories/02-message-level-derivation.md:26:**Dependencies:** Story 1 (drain executes the queued work). Story 0 (double, state types, `tool_call_summary` kind registered).\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/stories/02-message-level-derivation.md:151:| 2026-06-11 | Actual sanctioned-amendment edits (the planned tool_call sweep): `test/work-queue.test.ts` restart-survival rows 3→4 (`messageWork` 2→3, list gains `w-m2-tool_call_summary-v1`) + TC-2.9 kind-gate comment; `test/work-execution.test.ts` TC-1.1 (4 items, 5 forms) and maxItems leg (remaining 2→3); `test/cli-process-work.test.ts` TC-1.3 (4 seeded items, 3-row after-kill detail, 5 final forms) — the latter two are Epic 02 Story 0/1 files caught by the same sweep rule. Red manifest regenerated for all three plus the new suite | Implemented; behavior-preserving count/id updates only |\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/stories/02-message-level-derivation.md:152:| 2026-06-11 | `test/work-queue.test.ts` handler-map assembly test (Story 0) amended: `expect(sdk.workHandlers).toEqual({})` cannot survive Story 2 registering the real messages table — now asserts exactly the three message kinds and probes the miss with `turn_derivation` (unregistered until Story 3) | Necessary consequence of the story's registration step, not in the pre-listed sweep; flagged for verifier attention |\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/stories/02-message-level-derivation.md:155:| 2026-06-11 | `tool_result_summary` forms also carry `metadata.outcome` (stamped from the result's own `isError` via `outcome.ts`) — the epic's tool-activity outcome contract and the Story 0 multi-state fixture already claim this shape; the story text names only the call summary | Additive, consistent with AC-2.4's mechanism |\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/stories/02-message-level-derivation.md:156:| 2026-06-11 | \\\\\\\"Form read-back on message reads\\\\\\\" (Implementation Targets, Surface row) initially deferred to Story 4; verifier finding 02F-001 ruled it in-scope. Implemented: `listMessages` attaches each message's stored `derived_form` rows as `MessageRecord.forms` (one grouped query, stored state verbatim — no read-time derivation; messages with no rows carry no key, preserving AC-2.7). TC-2.1 amended to prove readability through this production path; red manifest regenerated for the suite | Resolved per 02F-001 |\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/stories/02-message-level-derivation.md:158:### Definition of Done\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/stories/02-message-level-derivation.md:159:<!-- Jira: Definition of Done or Acceptance Criteria footer -->\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:17:| 5 | Registry location and initialization undefined (epic TD question 6) | Resolved | Explicit `registryPath` parameter on registry-touching operations, defaulting to `~/.lhc/registry.sqlite`. Lazy creation on first write; reads against an absent registry return empty list / `thread_not_found` without creating it |\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:18:| 6 | AC-1.6 \\\\\\\"every thread-scoped operation accepts id or path\\\\\\\" is over-broad read literally (`new-thread` has no id yet; `resolve` is id-by-definition) | Resolved — clarified | Thread-scoped means operations on an existing thread's content: `message-events` and all read-backs. Creation and resolution are registry-scoped by nature |\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:82:        registry.ts         // registry open/init, row operations\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:118:Import boundaries are enforced from Story 0 by a zero-dependency check script (`scripts/check-boundaries.mjs`, run inside `verify`): no import may reach another domain's `internal/`, and `tech-utils`/`shared` may not import from `domains/`. The ergonomic path for cross-domain calls is the domain's `index.ts` surface, which is also the only path that compiles cleanly under the check.\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:151:**Thread creation spans two databases and cannot be one transaction.** Order: create and initialize the thread file first, then insert the registry row; if the row insert fails, delete the file (compensation) and return the error. The invariant \\\\\\\"no registry row without its file\\\\\\\" holds absolutely (row is written second). The residual crash window — process death between file creation and row insert — leaves an orphan file with no row; it is harmless (nothing references it), detectable (a later registry-refresh feature can adopt or report it), and re-creation at the same path correctly fails `path_exists`, which an operator resolves by deleting the orphan. Documented behavior, not a bug to engineer away at v1 scale.\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:181:### 6. Registry location and initialization\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:224:Creation's only interesting problem is that it spans two databases — the new thread file and the registry — and SQLite transactions do not cross files. Design Decision 2 resolves it by ordering plus compensation, and the diagram below is that decision made visible. Resolution and listing are ordinary registry reads; their one subtlety is lazy initialization (Design Decision 6): reads against a registry that has never been written return empty results without creating the database.\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:247:The existence check and file creation are not atomic against a concurrent creator racing the same path — accepted under the single-writer assumption (A1); the loser of such a race gets a `system_error` from SQLite rather than silent corruption. Thread-file schema creation runs inside the new file's own transaction, so a half-initialized file cannot result from a crash mid-create: the file either has its full schema and metadata row or fails compensation cleanup, and an orphan from the crash window between the two databases is the documented harmless case.\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:257:| TC-1.5 | Listing | Registry scan, lazy-init empty case |\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:264:The walk is where most of this epic's behavior lives. Each recorded event takes its order number from a counter initialized at `MAX(event_order)` when the transaction begins — skipped duplicates do not consume numbers, so the stream stays dense and AC-2.1's continuity holds across batches and across skips. Projection happens in the same iteration as recording: the message row, its blocks, the token estimate, and the actor/harness carry are written while the event is in hand, and the message id is the event's order number with an `m` prefix (Design Decision 7), so projection cannot drift from its source.\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:340:Strictness is the entire design: every schema is closed (`Schema.Struct` with no index signature, decoded under `onExcessProperty: \\\\\\\"error\\\\\\\"`), so unknown-field rejection at all three levels falls out of the schema definitions rather than being a rule someone must remember to apply. The decoded result is a typed value the pipeline trusts completely — no defensive re-checking inside the transaction.\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:597:**Exit criteria:** `verify-all` passes with zero behavior tests; smoke tests prove the rail (help, unknown command, stub failure shape), fixture validity (builders satisfy the Flow 4 schemas once they exist — initially golden-shaped), tokenizer determinism (golden counts for known strings), and boundary script failure on a deliberate violation (test-only sabotage file, then removed).\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:602:**Risk shape:** cross-database compensation; lazy initialization.\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:650:- Story 0 maps to Chunk 0 verbatim; Stories 1–5 map to Chunks 1–5. The epic's story skeletons carry the governing ideas; chunks above add the technical exit criteria.\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/stories/coverage.md:26:- **Story 0 fixtures are golden-shaped until consumed.** The multi-state fixture asserts states that only exist for real once Stories 2–4 produce them; the damaged-source fixture is validated against Story 4's blocked path (TC-4.6). Debt: Stories 2/4 confirm the fixtures' claims; Story 0 proves only read-back of what the builders wrote.\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/stories/coverage.md:46:- **1:1 flow-to-story mapping plus foundation.** Stories 1–6 own Flows 1–6 exactly as the epic's Recommended Story Breakdown cut them; no AC moved between flows during sharding. Story 0 owns the cross-cutting substrate (provider seam, state types, kinds, migration, fixtures) that the epic's breakdown lists as story 0.\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/stories/coverage.md:51:- **Story 0 owns no behavior** and proves the gates (FC-numbered, same pattern as Epic 01's Story 0). Its largest real risk is the migration (FC-0.5: Epic 01 suite green on the migrated schema) — that gate is the story's point.\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/04-inspection/stories/02-inspect-domain-overview-and-health.md:89:- [tech-design.md §Interface Definitions](/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/tech-design.md:151), lines 151-213\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/04-inspection/stories/02-inspect-domain-overview-and-health.md:141:### Definition of Done\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/04-inspection/stories/02-inspect-domain-overview-and-health.md:142:<!-- Jira: Definition of Done or Acceptance Criteria footer -->\\\\n./packages/lhc/test/fixtures/view-thread.ts:1:// The Epic 03 derived-thread fixture (Story 0, FC-0.3/0.4/0.5): one recorded\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/stories/02-inference-seam-and-model-assignment.md:86:- [tech-design.md §Interface Definitions](/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/05-derivation-inference/tech-design.md:210), lines 210-242\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/stories/02-inference-seam-and-model-assignment.md:194:### Definition of Done\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/stories/02-inference-seam-and-model-assignment.md:195:<!-- Jira: Definition of Done or Acceptance Criteria footer -->\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/stories/04-derivation-state-report-repair.md:20:- Blocked semantics: a handler reading damaged source (per Epic 01's corruption definitions) lands the form `blocked` with a reason naming the damage; the drain continues past it; re-queue requests for a `blocked` form are refused with that reason\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/stories/04-derivation-state-report-repair.md:25:**Dependencies:** Stories 2–3 (real forms in every state to report and repair). Story 0 (damaged-source fixture).\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/stories/04-derivation-state-report-repair.md:40:- **AC-4.6**: A derivation whose handler finds source damage (per Epic 01's corruption definitions) lands `blocked` with a reason naming the damage; drain continues past it; re-queue requests for a `blocked` form are refused with that reason.\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/stories/04-derivation-state-report-repair.md:151:### Definition of Done\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/stories/04-derivation-state-report-repair.md:152:<!-- Jira: Definition of Done or Acceptance Criteria footer -->\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/stories/06-turn-end-boundary-advance.md:96:- [tech-design.md §Interface Definitions](/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/05-derivation-inference/tech-design.md:272), lines 272-275\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/stories/06-turn-end-boundary-advance.md:197:### Definition of Done\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/stories/06-turn-end-boundary-advance.md:198:<!-- Jira: Definition of Done or Acceptance Criteria footer -->\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/stories/05-message-edit-cascade.md:50:The **source-version check** is the story's architectural heart and TC-5.4 is the epic's named architecture-risk test: a pre-edit item that completes after the edit writes through `forms.ts`'s version check, mismatches, and discards as `stale_discarded` (row deleted, outcome reported) — the post-edit rebuild stands regardless of completion order (AC-5.4). The rule in one line: a background result must not overwrite a derived form if the source changed since the job was queued. The check was built in Story 0, enforced from Story 2's first form write; this story is where it's finally *provoked*. Same machinery serves Story 6's deletes — the cascade module has two callers by design.\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/stories/05-message-edit-cascade.md:149:### Definition of Done\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/stories/05-message-edit-cascade.md:150:<!-- Jira: Definition of Done or Acceptance Criteria footer -->\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/stories/04-tool-result-visibility.md:43:  - **TC-4.6** (AC-4.9): Inject a failure at the advance seam (test seam from Story 0); intake a batch that crosses max → intake reports success and all messages committed; boundary unchanged; status read shows the zone sum over max. Clear the injection; intake another batch → the advance lands at target.\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/stories/04-tool-result-visibility.md:51:The boundary is the view's one self-acting piece — everything else changes by explicit call. Its writers are exactly two: this story's post-commit advance and Story 2's compact reset (already landed; this story proves the reset end-to-end). The advance rides the same `ctx.onCommit` seam as Epic 02's queue poke, registered by intake-stream — which makes this the story that introduces the sanctioned intake→thread-view surface import and the domain graph's first cycle (both pre-cleared in the design and the check-boundaries script per Story 0's DoD).\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/stories/04-tool-result-visibility.md:98:No host-called advance surface may exist — if a test needs an advance, it intakes a batch (the seam is the only trigger; a test-only `advance()` export would be the forgettable-nudge pattern reborn). The G1 golden's expected positions are hand-derived from the design rules before implementation, same discipline as Story 2's goldens. TC-4.6's injection uses the Story 0 facility's named post-commit point — not a mock of `boundary.ts`.\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/stories/04-tool-result-visibility.md:112:### Definition of Done\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/stories/04-tool-result-visibility.md:113:<!-- Jira: Definition of Done or Acceptance Criteria footer -->\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/04-inspection/artifacts/02-inspect-domain-overview-and-health/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-001.md:110:- [tech-design.md §Interface Definitions](/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/tech-design.md:151), lines 151-213\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/04-inspection/artifacts/02-inspect-domain-overview-and-health/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-001.md:162:### Definition of Done\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/04-inspection/artifacts/02-inspect-domain-overview-and-health/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-001.md:163:<!-- Jira: Definition of Done or Acceptance Criteria footer -->\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/04-inspection/artifacts/02-inspect-domain-overview-and-health/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-001.md:194:Fixture: Epic 03's tool-heavy fixture reused; extended in place (Story 2) with a mutation-in-flight variant — one edit applied, drain *not* settled, so cleared-pending states are real production states, not hand-written rows (FC-fidelity rule carried from Epic 03 Story 0: states reached through intake → mutate → partial drain, never direct `derived_form` writes).\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/04-inspection/artifacts/02-inspect-domain-overview-and-health/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-001.md:254:- current_summary: Story orchestration started and durable state has been initialized.\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/04-inspection/artifacts/02-inspect-domain-overview-and-health/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-001.md:282:currentSummary: \\\\\\\"Story orchestration started and durable state has been initialized.\\\\\\\"\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/04-inspection/artifacts/02-inspect-domain-overview-and-health/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-001.md:336:State: initialized\\\\n./packages/lhc/test/fixtures/view-seam.ts:1:// Epic 03 Story 0 view-domain test seams. Lives in fixtures/ — the one\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/04-inspection/stories/04-lifecycle-exercise.md:130:### Definition of Done\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/04-inspection/stories/04-lifecycle-exercise.md:131:<!-- Jira: Definition of Done or Acceptance Criteria footer -->\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/stories/02-smart-compact.md:120:Compact through `createSdk().threadView.compact` with real profiles resolved from real `SdkConfig.view`; crash injection through the Story 0 facility's named compact-write point, not a monkeypatch.\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/stories/02-smart-compact.md:130:### Definition of Done\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/stories/02-smart-compact.md:131:<!-- Jira: Definition of Done or Acceptance Criteria footer -->\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/stories/03-the-adapter-and-the-seven-prompts.md:92:- [tech-design.md §Interface Definitions](/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/05-derivation-inference/tech-design.md:244), lines 244-270\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/stories/03-the-adapter-and-the-seven-prompts.md:191:### Definition of Done\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/stories/03-the-adapter-and-the-seven-prompts.md:192:<!-- Jira: Definition of Done or Acceptance Criteria footer -->\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/stories/00-foundation.md:1:# Story 0: Foundation\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/stories/00-foundation.md:21:- Fixture builders: multi-state thread (forms in every state), damaged-source fixture (per Epic 01 corruption definitions), twin SDK/CLI fixtures for parity tests\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/stories/00-foundation.md:30:Story 0 owns no epic ACs. Its acceptance is foundation criteria, proven by focused invariant tests:\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/stories/00-foundation.md:45:Story 0 is the tech design's Chunk 0: the substrate every later story dispatches through. Three seams land inert-correct here. The **provider seam** (DD-7) is dependency injection at SDK construction — the deterministic double implements the same `DerivationProvider` interface production adapters will, so tests exercise the production seam, not a mock of internals. The **handler map** (DD-6) is the only join between the queue's opaque kinds and domain code; it assembles at `createSdk` from per-domain `workHandlers` tables. The **enqueue wrapper** (DD-5) makes \\\\\\\"queueing is what schedules processing\\\\\\\" structural: `enqueue` writes the work row *and* creates-or-resets the form's `pending` state row in the ambient transaction (enqueue is the only place form rows are created — completion is UPDATE-only), and registers the scheduler poke on `ctx.onCommit` — row, state, and poke all commit or all vanish together.\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/stories/00-foundation.md:102:| Enqueue atomicity | `test/work-queue.test.ts` | induced rollback after `enqueue` → no work row, no `pending` form row, no poke fired | Story 0 owns no epic TCs; this invariant underlies every later queue site |\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/stories/00-foundation.md:137:**Cross-story debt** (coverage.md): the multi-state and damaged-source fixtures are golden-shaped until consumed — Story 0 proves read-back of what the builders wrote; Stories 2/4 confirm the states arise for real.\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/stories/00-foundation.md:163:| 2026-06-10 | Work-item ids gain the version suffix (`w-<sourceId>-<kind>-v<sourceVersion>`), changing Epic 01 exact-id assertions — sanctioned amendment, see test plan §Sanctioned Amendments (Story 0 entry); red manifest regenerates as a story step | Planned amendment; record actual edits here during implementation |\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/stories/00-foundation.md:165:### Definition of Done\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/stories/00-foundation.md:166:<!-- Jira: Definition of Done or Acceptance Criteria footer -->\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/04-inspection/stories/01-message-read-surface.md:70:- [tech-design.md §Interface Definitions](/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/tech-design.md:189), lines 189-213\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/04-inspection/stories/01-message-read-surface.md:120:### Definition of Done\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/04-inspection/stories/01-message-read-surface.md:121:<!-- Jira: Definition of Done or Acceptance Criteria footer -->\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/stories/coverage.md:50:TC ownership count: Story 1 owns TC-1.1, TC-1.2, TC-1.4, TC-2.5 (4); Story 2 owns TC-1.3, TC-1.5, TC-2.1, TC-2.2, TC-2.3, TC-2.4, TC-2.6, TC-2.7 (8); Story 3 owns TC-3.1–TC-3.4 (4); Story 4 owns TC-4.1–TC-4.6 (6); Story 5 owns TC-5.1–TC-5.5 (5). Total 27/27. Story 0 owns FC-0.1–0.6 only.\\\\n./packages/lhc/test/fixtures/threads.ts:4:// and handlers land in Stories 1–3) — Story 0 proves read-back of what the\\\\n./packages/lhc/test/fixtures/threads.ts:301:// ── damaged-source thread (Epic 01 corruption definition) ────────\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/stories/03-turn-composition-chunk-formation.md:25:**Dependencies:** Story 2 (message forms to compose from). Story 1 (drain). Story 0 (double's compose/project/summarize operations, chunk kinds registered).\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/stories/03-turn-composition-chunk-formation.md:149:- **Tool-run grouping (Fix 2, fix-batch-002) — spec-compliance restoration, not a deviation.** `composeRenderingInput` folds maximal runs of consecutive tool activity into one `RenderingPart` and one tool-run receipt, each carrying an outcome-explicit run account (tools used, call count, per-call mechanical outcomes; mixed outcomes stay explicit, never collapsed into a vague success), as AC-3.4 and §Anti-Shim Requirements require. The initial build shimmed one part/receipt per tool message; this restores the grouped-run contract. Per-message mechanical stamping (AC-2.4) is untouched; gaps stay per-message.\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/stories/03-turn-composition-chunk-formation.md:152:### Definition of Done\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/stories/03-turn-composition-chunk-formation.md:153:<!-- Jira: Definition of Done or Acceptance Criteria footer -->\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/stories/04-failure-classification.md:80:- [tech-design.md §Interface Definitions](/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/05-derivation-inference/tech-design.md:252), lines 252-258\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/stories/04-failure-classification.md:160:### Definition of Done\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/stories/04-failure-classification.md:161:<!-- Jira: Definition of Done or Acceptance Criteria footer -->\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/stories/01-pull-and-status.md:15:- `thread-view` surface skeleton (structured-result stubs per tech design §Interface Definitions) with `pull` and `status` implemented\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/stories/01-pull-and-status.md:21:**Dependencies:** Story 0 (fixture, migration, profiles).\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/stories/01-pull-and-status.md:46:Types first (`shared/view.ts` landed in Story 0 — consume, don't redefine), then the surface skeleton with all five stubs (Red asserts each returns `{ ok: false, reason: \\\\\\\"not implemented: <op>\\\\\\\" }`), then Green: `render.ts` tail formatting kind-by-kind against the mapping table, then `pull` assembly, then `status`. The mapping table is seven rows — implement and test them as seven legs, not one blob assertion, so a single kind's drift fails one named test.\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/stories/01-pull-and-status.md:75:| TC-1.4 | `view-pull.test.ts` | boundary seeded mid-tail (Story 0 helper): short behind, full ahead, prompts/text full everywhere |\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/stories/01-pull-and-status.md:92:All reads through the real SDK surface (`createSdk().threadView.pull/status`) against real temp thread files from the Story 0 fixture. No internal-function shortcuts in TC tests (golden/unit tests of `render.ts` legs may call internals; the TC rows go through the surface).\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/stories/01-pull-and-status.md:102:### Definition of Done\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/stories/01-pull-and-status.md:103:<!-- Jira: Definition of Done or Acceptance Criteria footer -->\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/04-inspection/tech-design.md:151:## Interface Definitions\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/stories/05-real-inference-suite-and-capstone.md:180:### Definition of Done\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/stories/05-real-inference-suite-and-capstone.md:181:<!-- Jira: Definition of Done or Acceptance Criteria footer -->\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/04-inspection/artifacts/epic/streams/002-epic-reverify.stdout.log:1:{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"system\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"subtype\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"init\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"cwd\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"session_id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"77af4b8d-fd36-430f-8eea-47bd404bad4c\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"tools\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\"Task\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"AskUserQuestion\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"Bash\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"CronCreate\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"CronDelete\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"CronList\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"DesignSync\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"Edit\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"EnterPlanMode\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"EnterWorktree\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"ExitPlanMode\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"ExitWorktree\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"Monitor\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"NotebookEdit\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"PushNotification\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"Read\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"RemoteTrigger\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"ScheduleWakeup\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"Skill\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"TaskCreate\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"TaskGet\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"TaskList\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"TaskOutput\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"TaskStop\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"TaskUpdate\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"ToolSearch\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"WebFetch\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"WebSearch\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"Workflow\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"Write\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\"mcp_servers\\\\\\\":[],\\\\\\\"model\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"claude-fable-5\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"permissionMode\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"bypassPermissions\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"slash_commands\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\"deep-research\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"design-sync\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"update-config\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"verify\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"debug\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"code-review\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"simplify\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"batch\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"fewer-permission-prompts\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"loop\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"schedule\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"claude-api\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"run\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"run-skill-generator\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"clear\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"compact\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"context\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"heapdump\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"init\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"reload-skills\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"review\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"security-review\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"usage-credits\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"extra-usage\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"usage\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"insights\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"goal\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"team-onboarding\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\"apiKeySource\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"none\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"claude_code_version\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"2.1.175\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"output_style\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"default\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"agents\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\"claude\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"Explore\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"general-purpose\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"Plan\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"statusline-setup\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\"skills\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\"deep-research\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"design-sync\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"update-config\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"verify\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"debug\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"code-review\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"simplify\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"batch\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"fewer-permission-prompts\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"loop\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"schedule\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"claude-api\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"run\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"run-skill-generator\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\"plugins\\\\\\\":[],\\\\\\\"analytics_disabled\\\\\\\":false,\\\\\\\"product_feedback_disabled\\\\\\\":false,\\\\\\\"uuid\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"9fb3384e-afe9-4f46-98c1-5b9ba592abbc\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"memory_paths\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"auto\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"/Users/leemoore/.claude/projects/-Users-leemoore-code-pi-long-horizon-liminal-context/memory/\\\\\\\"},\\\\\\\"fast_mode_state\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"off\\\\\\\"}\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/04-inspection/artifacts/epic/streams/002-epic-reverify.stdout.log:31:{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"user\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"message\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"role\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"user\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"content\\\\\\\":[{\\\\\\\"tool_use_id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"toolu_01GiVtLqABvhqfqwupkLkzf8\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"tool_result\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"content\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"1\\\\\\\\t{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"epic-review\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"version\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"blocked\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"outcome\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"block\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"result\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"outcome\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"block\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"canonicalReview\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"outcome\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"block\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"reviewerLabels\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"epic-reviewer-1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"epic-reviewer-2\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"reconciliationSummary\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Both reviewers agree on nearly the whole epic: gates green (independently re-run by this canonical pass: pnpm verify-all exit 0, 366/366 tests, 44 files, process suite ran), composition discipline structural, read-only enforced at two layers, approved rulings (011 Story 3 loadCost/sourceState, 011 Story 4 threadId-normalized file equality, 023 strict-read-purity) implemented exactly, no fake or placeholder production paths. The one material disagreement is the health queue section. epic-reviewer-1 treated the listQueuedWork→report-entry-join substitution as a surface-respecting design drift ('arguably stronger for AC-4.5', observation only); epic-reviewer-2 blocked on it with a reproduced divergence (queued:5 vs 4 live work items). Independent verification confirms reviewer-2 on every link: health.ts:92-95 increments queue once per FormReportEntry with a queue join and never calls listQueuedWork; turns/internal/forms.ts:266,296 ('Both turn forms map to the one turn_derivation kind') makes one live turn_derivation work item join onto two form rows (turn_rendering + lower_band_projection); and the shipped test inspect-health.test.ts:174 itself pins queued:5 for the post-edit cascade whose owners' queue detail holds exactly 4 work items (prompt_smoothing, turn_derivation, chunk_summary_brief, chunk_summary_detailed) — so the divergence is pinned by the default suite, not edge-case-only. Epic AC-4.5's governing text ('queued and claimed work counts from the owners' queue detail') and the epic NFR ('where a report describes another surface's output ... equality is contractual') are not satisfied as written, no ruling covers the substitution (impl-log rulings 011/011/023 cover other contracts), and the tech design is internally ambiguous (Flow 4 names listQueuedWork as an input yet says 'queue = counts from the live-item joins'). Per the conservative rule, the blocker is retained, not downgraded: EV-04-001 stands as the single blocking finding (absorbing the health half of E04-ER1-002), with human-ruling scope because both fix paths alter a spec'd contract — either count work items from queue detail (breaking the queued+claimed = pending+retrying identity TC-4.1 leans on) or ratify form-entry semantics and amend AC-4.5/tech-design wording. Reviewer-1's doc-lag finding (E04-ER1-001) and reviewer-2's (EV-04-002) are the same issue and are merged into one minor finding, fully verified against epic.md:149/156, test-plan.md:38, coverage.md:48, tech-design.md:94 (DD-4 stored-sum wording vs view-report.ts:67 measured tokens), and stories/03-view-contents-report.md ('Spec Deviations: None' despite approved ruling-011). Reviewer-1's remaining observations (overview boundary via pull.meta because ViewStatus at shared/view.ts:128-135 exposes no boundaryPosition; stale stub-era comment at cli/index.ts:473-475) were independently confirmed and retained as non-blocking. No reviewer finding was discarded as unsupported.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"crossStoryFindings\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Story handoffs completed as tracked: the Story 2 interim view-summary source (pull().meta) was swapped to threadView.describe in Story 3 (overview.ts:121-131), and the Story 2 inspect.view stub was replaced by the real composeViewReport — receipt-tracked risks are closed in code, not just in the log.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Shared vocabulary discipline holds: report shapes live in src/shared/inspect.ts and Story 3 consumes them without redefinition; messages.show reuses the owner's report(messageId) read (DD-2); one lifecycle script (test/fixtures/lifecycle.ts) drives all four TC-5 legs including the process-suite leg (DD-8).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Confirmed cross-story semantic drift (the blocker): Flow 4's health.queue counts form-report entries with live queue joins, while Epic 02's queue-detail surfaces (listQueuedWork) count work items — a turn_derivation work item owning both turn forms is counted twice by health (EV-04-001).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Cumulative test baseline never regressed across the four accepted stories (290 → 300 → 313 → 320 → 328 default tests); epic gate re-run for this canonical review: 366/366 tests across 44 files with the process suite executed.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"All four story receipts in team-impl-log.md show accepted status with verifier PASS and disposition of every finding; approved rulings are reflected accurately in shipped code — but the health-queue substitution was never the subject of any ruling.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"architectureFindings\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Composition discipline is structural: domains/inspect imports only other domains' index surfaces, check-boundaries.mjs carries the inspect allowlist plus the Epic 04 source check rejecting raw storage access from domains/inspect/**, and the gate runs it; the domain-graph addition is acyclic as designed.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Read-only is enforced at two layers per DD-6: every new operation runs under touch suppression, and tests assert absence-of-delta over observable state including the Story 3 storedView field added to the snapshot.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Tech design Flow 4 is internally ambiguous and the ambiguity was resolved silently in code: the flow names listQueuedWork (both owners) as a health input and the External Contracts table lists it as 'Used by ... health', yet the same paragraph says 'queue = queued/claimed counts from the live-item joins'. health.ts implemented the latter reading and never calls listQueuedWork; no design deviation was recorded and no ruling was sought (feeds EV-04-001).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"DD-5 ('overview takes boundary via status') is impossible as written: ViewStatus (src/shared/view.ts:128-135) exposes no boundaryPosition, so overview.ts:121-128 reads it from pull.meta — the only surface exposing it — at the cost of a full pull render per overview call. Surface-respecting, but unrecorded in the tech design (E04-ER1-002).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Approved public-contract deviations are implemented exactly as ruled: loadCost.bandTokens measured from pull-served band messages (view-report.ts:67, ruling-011 Story 3), sourceState/meta nullable for never-compacted threads, threadId-normalized materialized-file equality with literal pull-output hash equality (lifecycle.test.ts:17-22, ruling-011 Story 4).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Error model and CLI conventions inherited cleanly: one additive caller_error code (invalid_bounds, approved); CLI commands are thin argv-to-SDK wrappers so parity including refusals is structural.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"epicCoverageAssessment\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"22 of 23 ACs and 16 of 17 TCs are implemented, traceable, and green: test describe blocks name their TC/AC pairs across inspect-overview, inspect-view, messages-read, inspect-health, lifecycle, and cli-process-inspect test files; epic gate re-run by this canonical review passed (pnpm verify-all, exit 0, 366/366 tests, 44 files, process suite ran).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"AC-4.5 is not conclusively covered as written: TC-4.1's and TC-4.3's shipped assertions (inspect-health.test.ts:63, :174) pin the form-entry semantics — queue equals pending+retrying entry counts — not 'work counts from the owners' queue detail'; on the post-edit cascade the pinned value (queued:5) diverges from the queue-detail work-item count (4).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"AC-5.3/AC-5.4 ship as threadId-normalized file equality per approved Story 4 ruling-011 (the thread id is the design's one random value; everything else byte-identical, Date frozen so pull-output hash equality stays literal); the relaxation is principled and recorded in the story spec, but epic-level docs still carry the superseded literal wording (EV-04-002).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Zero-provider NFR verified structurally for the whole epic: inspect/list/show/describe run green under a throwing provider; the lifecycle's only provider is the registry's deterministic one.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Closeout items still owed: the M3/M4 quality-half transfer to the inference epic must be recorded, and the sanctioned PRD/tech-arch search-deferral backfill exists only as uncommitted working-tree changes (see unresolvedItems).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"productionPathFindings\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"No fake adapters, shims, or placeholder execution paths remain on any production path: the not_implemented code survives only as vocabulary in shared/errors.ts, the Story 2 inspect.view stub was replaced by the real implementation in Story 3, and the lifecycle exercise runs the real production SDK through the production registry.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Provider posture is honest by construction: the registry contains exactly one provider (deterministic — the spec'd v1 state), there is no default provider, and the CLI fails with provider_not_configured rather than silently substituting.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Confirmed material production-path issue (EV-04-001): inspect.health's queue section reports per-form-entry counts, not live work-item counts; on the standard mutation-in-flight cascade it reports queued:5 while the owners' listQueuedWork surfaces hold 4 work items, so the report disagrees with the queue-detail surface an operator would drill into.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Cosmetic only: src/cli/index.ts:473-475 still carries the stub-era comment describing 'inspect view' as not-implemented; the route is real and in the help text (E04-ER1-003).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"blockingFindings\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"EV-04-001\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"severity\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"major\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"title\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Health queue counts are form-entry counts, not the queue-detail work counts AC-4.5 specifies — confirmed divergence, pinned by the shipped tests\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"evidence\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Epic AC-4.5 requires 'queued and claimed work counts from the owners' queue detail'; tech-design Flow 4 and its External Contracts table name listQueuedWork (both owners) as a health input. composeHealth (src/domains/inspect/internal/health.ts:40-106) reads only messages.report + turns.report and increments queue once per FormReportEntry carrying a queue join (lines 89-95); it never calls listQueuedWork. The divergence is structural: the turns report join maps BOTH turn forms (turn_rendering, lower_band_projection) to the one turn_derivation work-item kind (src/domains/turns/internal/forms.ts:266,294-301), so one live turn-derivation item is counted twice. The shipped default suite pins the divergent value: inspect-health.test.ts:174 asserts queue {queued:5, claimed:0} for the post-edit cascade whose owners' queue detail holds exactly 4 work items (prompt_smoothing, turn_derivation, chunk_summary_brief, chunk_summary_detailed) — matching epic-reviewer-2's independent reproduction. This violates AC-4.5's source clause and the epic NFR 'where a report describes another surface's output ... equality is contractual', and no approved ruling covers the substitution. Human ruling required because both fix paths alter spec'd contract text: counting work items from queue detail breaks the queued+claimed = pending+retrying identity the current TC-4.1 assertion leans on, while ratifying form-entry semantics requires amending AC-4.5, tech-design Flow 4/External Contracts, and the test-plan TC-4.1 spine.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"affectedFiles\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/src/domains/inspect/internal/health.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/test/inspect-health.test.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/test/lifecycle.test.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/epic.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/tech-design.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/test-plan.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"requirementIds\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"AC-4.5\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"TC-4.1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tech-design Flow 4: Health\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"recommendedFixScope\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"human-ruling\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"blocking\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":true}],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"nonBlockingFindings\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"EV-04-002\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"severity\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"minor\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"title\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Epic-level artifacts not updated for approved ruling-011 deviations (threadId-normalized file equality; measured loadCost.bandTokens)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"evidence\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Merged from E04-ER1-001 and EV-04-002; all citations verified. Story 4 ruling-011 (approved) relaxed materialized-file equality to substitute only the random thread id (implemented in lifecycle.test.ts:17-22 and the file-equality assertions), but epic.md AC-5.3/TC-5.2 (lines 149, 156), test-plan.md TC-5.2 (line 38), and stories/coverage.md (line 48) still say byte-identical/hash-equal with no qualifier. Story 3 ruling-011 (approved) made loadCost.bandTokens measured from pull-served band messages (view-report.ts:67), but tech-design.md DD-4 (line 94) still says 'sum of stored band counts', and stories/03-view-contents-report.md's Spec Deviations section still says 'None.' despite that approved public-contract deviation. Code and tests implement the ruled contracts correctly; only the docs lag.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"affectedFiles\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/epic.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/test-plan.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/stories/coverage.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/tech-design.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/stories/03-view-contents-report.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"requirementIds\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"AC-2.3\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"AC-2.5\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"AC-5.3\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"AC-5.4\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"TC-5.2\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"recommendedFixScope\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"quick-fix\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"blocking\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":false},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"E04-ER1-002\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"severity\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"observation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"title\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Tech design DD-5 not backfilled: overview reads the visibility boundary from pull.meta because ViewStatus exposes no boundaryPosition\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"evidence\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tech-design.md DD-5 says overview composes boundary/zone 'via status', but ViewStatus (src/shared/view.ts:128-135) has no boundaryPosition field — overview.ts:121-128 reads it from pull.meta, the only surface exposing it, at the cost of a full pull render per overview call. Surface-respecting and AC-1.1-satisfying, but unrecorded as a design deviation. (The health half of the original finding — the listQueuedWork substitution — is escalated into blocking finding EV-04-001 and not duplicated here.)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"affectedFiles\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/tech-design.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/src/domains/inspect/internal/overview.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"requirementIds\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"AC-1.1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"recommendedFixScope\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"quick-fix\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"blocking\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":false},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"E04-ER1-003\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"severity\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"observation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"title\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Stale stub-era comment on the 'inspect view' CLI route\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"evidence\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"src/cli/index.ts:473-475 still says the route 'returns the structured not-implemented result; Story 3 makes it real (and adds it to the help text)' — Story 3 landed, runInspectView is the real implementation, and the command is in the help text. Comment is false; code is correct.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"affectedFiles\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/src/cli/index.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"requirementIds\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"recommendedFixScope\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"quick-fix\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"blocking\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":false}],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"unresolvedItems\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Human ruling required for EV-04-001: decide whether HealthReport.queue counts live work items (per epic AC-4.5's 'work counts from the owners' queue detail' — requires reworking health.ts to consume listQueuedWork and re-stating TC-4.1's consistency clause, since queued+claimed would no longer equal pending+retrying when a turn derivation is in flight) or pending/retrying form entries (per the shipped implementation and tests — requires amending AC-4.5, tech-design Flow 4 and External Contracts, and the test-plan TC-4.1 spine to ratify the deviation).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Uncommitted working-tree changes carry the sanctioned PRD AC-4.3 search-deferral backfill (packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/00-prd.md, 01-tech-arch.md) plus impl-log bookkeeping — epic-mandated doc changes that must be committed as part of epic closeout.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Epic closeout must record the M3/M4 quality-half transfer to the inference epic explicitly (epic.md Assumptions); not yet written — this review is upstream of that closeout step.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"gateResult\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"pass\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"verifierResults\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"resultId\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"6b026f0b-72fd-4f16-88b6-28f3f56e2b90\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"outcome\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"pass\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"provider\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"claude-code\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"model\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"claude-fable-5\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"reviewerLabel\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"epic-reviewer-1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"crossStoryFindings\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Story 2 → Story 3 handoff completed as tracked: overview's interim view-summary source (pull().meta, Story 2 approved deviation) was swapped to threadView.describe in Story 3 (overview.ts), and the Story 2 inspect.view stub was replaced with the real composeViewReport. The receipt-tracked risk is closed in code, not just in the log.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Shared vocabulary discipline holds across stories: report shapes live in src/shared/inspect.ts (Story 2) and Story 3 consumes them without redefinition; messages.show reuses the owner's reportMessageForms read (DD-2) rather than new join machinery; the lifecycle fixture (test/fixtures/lifecycle.ts) is one script driven by all four TC-5 legs including the process-suite leg (DD-8), so replay/teardown/CLI runs exercise the same sequence.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The read-only delta helper (test/fixtures/read-only-delta.ts, Story 2) wraps Story 1's ops retroactively as the test plan required: inspect-overview/view/health tests use expectReadOnly, messages-read uses the same observableState snapshot directly, and the Story 3 storedView field was added to the snapshot so a forgotten view touch is caught even when pull meta would not show it.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Cumulative baseline never regressed: 290 → 300 → 313 → 320 → 328 default tests across the four accepted stories; epic gate run for this review shows 366 tests / 44 files with the process suite included.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"All four story receipts in team-impl-log.md show accepted status with verifier PASS, impl-lead-run gates, and disposition of every finding (SV-01-001..005, SV-03-001, SV-04-001); approved rulings (023 strict-read-purity, 011 Story 3 loadCost/sourceState, 011 Story 4 threadId-normalized file equality) are all reflected accurately in the shipped code.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"architectureFindings\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Composition discipline is structural, not aspirational: domains/inspect imports only the five other domains' index surfaces (verified in source), check-boundaries.mjs carries the inspect dependency allowlist plus the Epic 04 source check rejecting raw storage access from domains/inspect/** (scripts/check-boundaries.mjs:77-115), and the gate runs it. The domain graph addition is acyclic as designed.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Read-only is enforced at two layers as DD-6 demanded: every new operation (inspect.overview/view/health, threadView.describe, messages.listMessages/show) runs under runWithThreadTouchSuppressed so a background scheduler can never hang a catch-up drain off a read, and tests assert absence-of-delta over observable state including raw derived_form rows.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Minor drift from tech-design mechanics, surface-respecting in both cases: (1) DD-5 says overview takes boundary 'via status' but ViewStatus does not expose boundaryPosition — the implementation reads it from pull.meta (overview.ts:125-129, commented), which is the only surface exposing it; side effect is that every overview call performs a full pull render. (2) Tech design Flow 4 names listQueuedWork as a health input, but health.ts derives queue counts from the report entries' queue joins instead — consistent-by-construction with the same report's state counts, which is arguably stronger for AC-4.5, but the tech design was not updated to record either substitution.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Approved public-contract deviations are implemented exactly as ruled: loadCost.bandTokens measured from pull-served band messages rather than stored sums (ruling-011 Story 3; AC-2.3's equality is the governing contract and the shared/inspect.ts comment documents why stored sums structurally cannot match), and sourceState/meta nullable for never-compacted threads.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Error model and CLI conventions inherited cleanly: one additive caller_error code (invalid_bounds, Story 1 approved ruling, Epic 03 precedent), no other additions; CLI commands are thin argv-to-SDK wrappers with semantic validation kept in the SDK so parity including refusals is structural (cli/inspect.ts, cli/messages-read.ts).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"epicCoverageAssessment\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"All 23 ACs and 17 TCs are implemented and traceable: test describe blocks name their TC/AC pairs explicitly across inspect-overview.test.ts (TC-1.1–1.3), inspect-view.test.ts (TC-2.1–2.3 + describe purity legs), messages-read.test.ts (TC-3.1–3.3 + three architecture-risk suites), inspect-health.test.ts (TC-4.1–4.3 + provider-free leg), lifecycle.test.ts (TC-5.1–5.3 with all four AC-5.2 checkpoint assertions including the receipt-vs-health cross-check), and cli-process-inspect.test.ts (TC-3.4, TC-5.4, process suite).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"AC-5.3/AC-5.4 materialized-file equality ships as threadId-normalized equality per approved Story 4 ruling-011: thread id is the design's one random value, the test substitutes only it and requires every other byte identical, with Date frozen so pull-output hash equality stays literal with zero normalization. The relaxation is principled, documented in the test header, and recorded in the story spec.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Zero-provider NFR is verified structurally for the whole epic: inspect/list/show/describe run green under a throwing provider, and the lifecycle's only provider is the registry's deterministic one (zero network, zero real-provider calls — AC-5.1).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Epic gate run by this reviewer: pnpm verify-all PASS, exit 0, 366/366 tests across 44 files with the process suite executed (LHC_PROCESS_SUITE=1 is baked into verify-all), build/typecheck/lint/boundaries all green.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Closeout items still owed per the epic's own assumptions: the M3/M4 quality-half transfer to the inference epic must be recorded at closeout, and the search-deferral PRD/tech-arch backfill exists but is uncommitted (see unresolvedItems).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"productionPathFindings\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"No fake adapters, shims, or placeholder execution paths remain on any production path. Grep for stub/placeholder/not-implemented across src/ finds only SQL parameter placeholders, comments, and the not_implemented code's vocabulary entry in shared/errors.ts — no operation returns it anymore; the Story 2 inspect.view stub was replaced by the real implementation in Story 3.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Provider posture is honest by construction: the registry (src/providers/registry.ts) contains exactly one provider — deterministic — which is the spec'd v1 state (real inference is explicitly next-PRD scope, carried in the epic's Out of Scope). There is no default provider: the CLI resolves only an explicitly named provider (--provider/LHC_PROVIDER) and fails with provider_not_configured rather than silently substituting the deterministic one.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The lifecycle exercise runs the real production SDK (createSdk) in background mode through the production registry's deterministic provider and the real drainSettled awaitable — no test-only seams in the sequence itself; the one test-only control is freezing Date in the test file, which is outside the production path.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Stale comment, cosmetic only: src/cli/index.ts:473-475 still describes 'inspect view' as a not-implemented stub that 'Story 3 makes real' — Story 3 landed, the route is real, and the command is in the help text. The comment is now false; the code is correct.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"blockingFindings\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"nonBlockingFindings\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"E04-ER1-001\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"severity\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"minor\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"title\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Epic-level docs still carry literal 'byte-identical' phrasing superseded by approved ruling-011 (threadId-normalized file equality)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"evidence\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"epic.md AC-5.3/TC-5.2 (lines 149, 156-157), test-plan.md TC-5.2 (line 38), and coverage.md line 48 state byte-identical materialized-file equality; Story 4 ruling-011 (approved, recorded in team-impl-log.md and stories/04-lifecycle-exercise.md) relaxed file equality to substitute the one random thread id, with pull-output hash equality remaining literal. The impl log itself flags this for epic-review awareness. The shipped test (lifecycle.test.ts:17-22, 97-104) implements the ruled contract correctly; only the epic-level docs lag.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"affectedFiles\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/epic.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/test-plan.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/stories/coverage.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"requirementIds\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"AC-5.3\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"AC-5.4\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"TC-5.2\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"recommendedFixScope\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"quick-fix\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"blocking\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":false},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"E04-ER1-002\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"severity\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"observation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"title\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Tech design not backfilled for two surface-respecting mechanism substitutions (overview boundary via pull.meta; health queue via report join)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"evidence\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tech-design.md DD-5 says overview takes boundary/zone 'via status', but ViewStatus (src/shared/view.ts:128-135) exposes no boundaryPosition — overview.ts:125-129 reads it from pull.meta, the only surface that exposes it, at the cost of a full pull render per overview call. Tech-design Flow 4 and the System View name listQueuedWork as a health input, but health.ts derives queue counts from FormReportEntry queue joins (consistent-by-construction with the same report's state counts). Both substitutions stay on surfaces and satisfy their ACs; neither is recorded as a design deviation.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"affectedFiles\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/tech-design.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/src/domains/inspect/internal/overview.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/src/domains/inspect/internal/health.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"requirementIds\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"AC-1.1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"AC-4.5\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"recommendedFixScope\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"quick-fix\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"blocking\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":false},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"E04-ER1-003\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"severity\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"observation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"title\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Stale stub-era comment on the 'inspect view' CLI route\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"evidence\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"src/cli/index.ts:473-475 says the route is 'routed ahead of its story so the CLI mirrors the SDK surface: the stub returns the structured not-implemented result; Story 3 makes it real (and adds it to the help text)' — Story 3 landed; runInspectView is the real implementation and the command appears in the help text (line 72). Comment is false, code is correct.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"affectedFiles\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/src/cli/index.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"requirementIds\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"recommendedFixScope\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"quick-fix\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"blocking\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":false}],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"unresolvedItems\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Uncommitted working-tree changes carry the sanctioned PRD AC-4.3 search-deferral backfill (packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/00-prd.md, 01-tech-arch.md) plus impl-log state bookkeeping — these are epic-mandated doc changes (epic.md Out of Scope: 'backfilled with this epic') and should be committed as part of epic closeout.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Epic closeout must record the M3/M4 quality-half transfer to the inference epic explicitly (epic.md Assumptions: 'Closeout records this explicitly') — not yet written; this review is upstream of that closeout step.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"gateResult\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"pass\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"resultId\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"7037aa32-e09d-4f0a-b954-6917da95a54b\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"outcome\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"block\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"provider\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"codex\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"model\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"gpt-5.5\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"reviewerLabel\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"epic-reviewer-2\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"crossStoryFindings\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Stories 1-4 are wired through the SDK and CLI and the configured gates pass: `pnpm verify` passed with 328 tests and `pnpm verify-all` passed with 366 tests / 44 files, including the process suite.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Flow 4 has unresolved cross-story semantic drift: `inspect.health.queue` reports one queued/claimed count per pending form report entry, while the epic and tech design describe queued/claimed work counts from owner queue detail. A turn-derivation work item that owns two forms is counted twice by health.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"architectureFindings\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"`domains/inspect/**` imports owner surfaces only; `check-boundaries` passed and the inspect source check found no raw SQL/table access in inspect.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"`inspect.view` and lifecycle determinism intentionally follow approved rulings, but the epic-level artifacts still contain older wording for materialized-file byte identity and the tech design still says `loadCost.bandTokens` is the stored band-count sum.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"epicCoverageAssessment\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Most epic ACs have direct default/process suite coverage across `messages-read`, `inspect-overview`, `inspect-health`, `inspect-view`, `lifecycle`, and `cli-process-inspect`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"AC-4.5 is not conclusively covered because the tests assert consistency with pending form-entry counts, not queue-detail work-item counts.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"productionPathFindings\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"No material fake adapter, shim, placeholder, or non-real production execution path was found in the audited SDK/CLI inspect, message-read, thread-view, and lifecycle paths.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Material production-path issue: `inspect.health` derives queue counts from `FormReportEntry.queue` per form entry and does not count live queue work rows; on a mutation-in-flight production fixture it returned `{queued:5, claimed:0}` while owner `listQueuedWork` surfaces showed four live work items.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"blockingFindings\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"EV-04-001\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"severity\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"major\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"title\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Health queue counts do not match queue-detail work counts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"evidence\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Epic AC-4.5 requires queued and claimed work counts from owner queue detail; tech-design Flow 4 says `health` composes `messages.report` + `turns.report` + `listQueuedWork` both owners. In `src/domains/inspect/internal/health.ts:40-105`, `composeHealth` only reads `messages.report` and `turns.report`, then increments `queue` once for every report entry with `entry.queue` at lines 89-95. In an ad hoc mutation-in-flight fixture, `inspect.health` returned `healthQueue:{queued:5,claimed:0}` while `messages.listQueuedWork` + `turns.listQueuedWork` returned four work item ids: `w-m5-prompt_smoothing-v2`, `w-t2-turn_derivation-v2`, `w-c1-chunk_summary_brief-v2`, `w-c1-chunk_summary_detailed-v2`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"affectedFiles\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/src/domains/inspect/internal/health.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/test/inspect-health.test.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/test/lifecycle.test.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/epic.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/tech-design.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/test-plan.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"requirementIds\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"AC-4.5\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"TC-4.1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tech-design Flow 4: Health\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"recommendedFixScope\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"human-ruling\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"blocking\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":true}],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"nonBlockingFindings\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"EV-04-002\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"severity\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"minor\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"title\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Accepted deviation wording is not propagated through all epic-level artifacts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"evidence\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Story 4 and ruling-011 accept materialized-file equality after substituting only the random threadId, and tests implement that in `test/lifecycle.test.ts:97-103` and `:282-302`. The epic and test plan still say materialized files are byte-identical without that qualifier at `epic.md:149-157` and `test-plan.md:38-39`. Story 3/ruling-011 accepts measured pull-served `loadCost.bandTokens` and nullable `sourceState`, while `tech-design.md:94` still says `loadCost.bandTokens` is the sum of stored band counts and `stories/03-view-contents-report.md:119-121` still says no deviations.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"affectedFiles\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/epic.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/tech-design.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/test-plan.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/stories/03-view-contents-report.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/stories/04-lifecycle-exercise.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"requirementIds\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"AC-2.3\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"AC-2.5\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"AC-5.3\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"AC-5.4\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"recommendedFixScope\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"quick-fix\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"blocking\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":false}],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"unresolvedItems\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Decide whether `HealthReport.queue` is intended to count live work items or pending/retrying form entries. The current implementation and tests use form-entry counts; the epic/tech-design wording points to queue-detail work counts.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"gateResult\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"pass\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}]},\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"errors\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"warnings\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"artifacts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"result-envelope\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"path\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/artifacts/epic/001-epic-review.json\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"startedAt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"2026-06-12T15:43:43.996Z\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"finishedAt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"2026-06-12T15:54:28.853Z\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}\\\\\\\\n2\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\"}]},\\\\\\\"parent_tool_use_id\\\\\\\":null,\\\\\\\"session_id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"77af4b8d-fd36-430f-8eea-47bd404bad4c\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"uuid\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"424e2f32-086e-4dfc-9d55-76643b377be1\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"timestamp\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"2026-06-12T16:04:02.490Z\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"tool_use_result\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"file\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"filePath\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/artifacts/epic/001-epic-review.json\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"content\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"epic-review\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"version\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"blocked\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"outcome\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"block\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"result\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"outcome\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"block\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"canonicalReview\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"outcome\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"block\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"reviewerLabels\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"epic-reviewer-1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"epic-reviewer-2\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"reconciliationSummary\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Both reviewers agree on nearly the whole epic: gates green (independently re-run by this canonical pass: pnpm verify-all exit 0, 366/366 tests, 44 files, process suite ran), composition discipline structural, read-only enforced at two layers, approved rulings (011 Story 3 loadCost/sourceState, 011 Story 4 threadId-normalized file equality, 023 strict-read-purity) implemented exactly, no fake or placeholder production paths. The one material disagreement is the health queue section. epic-reviewer-1 treated the listQueuedWork→report-entry-join substitution as a surface-respecting design drift ('arguably stronger for AC-4.5', observation only); epic-reviewer-2 blocked on it with a reproduced divergence (queued:5 vs 4 live work items). Independent verification confirms reviewer-2 on every link: health.ts:92-95 increments queue once per FormReportEntry with a queue join and never calls listQueuedWork; turns/internal/forms.ts:266,296 ('Both turn forms map to the one turn_derivation kind') makes one live turn_derivation work item join onto two form rows (turn_rendering + lower_band_projection); and the shipped test inspect-health.test.ts:174 itself pins queued:5 for the post-edit cascade whose owners' queue detail holds exactly 4 work items (prompt_smoothing, turn_derivation, chunk_summary_brief, chunk_summary_detailed) — so the divergence is pinned by the default suite, not edge-case-only. Epic AC-4.5's governing text ('queued and claimed work counts from the owners' queue detail') and the epic NFR ('where a report describes another surface's output ... equality is contractual') are not satisfied as written, no ruling covers the substitution (impl-log rulings 011/011/023 cover other contracts), and the tech design is internally ambiguous (Flow 4 names listQueuedWork as an input yet says 'queue = counts from the live-item joins'). Per the conservative rule, the blocker is retained, not downgraded: EV-04-001 stands as the single blocking finding (absorbing the health half of E04-ER1-002), with human-ruling scope because both fix paths alter a spec'd contract — either count work items from queue detail (breaking the queued+claimed = pending+retrying identity TC-4.1 leans on) or ratify form-entry semantics and amend AC-4.5/tech-design wording. Reviewer-1's doc-lag finding (E04-ER1-001) and reviewer-2's (EV-04-002) are the same issue and are merged into one minor finding, fully verified against epic.md:149/156, test-plan.md:38, coverage.md:48, tech-design.md:94 (DD-4 stored-sum wording vs view-report.ts:67 measured tokens), and stories/03-view-contents-report.md ('Spec Deviations: None' despite approved ruling-011). Reviewer-1's remaining observations (overview boundary via pull.meta because ViewStatus at shared/view.ts:128-135 exposes no boundaryPosition; stale stub-era comment at cli/index.ts:473-475) were independently confirmed and retained as non-blocking. No reviewer finding was discarded as unsupported.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"crossStoryFindings\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Story handoffs completed as tracked: the Story 2 interim view-summary source (pull().meta) was swapped to threadView.describe in Story 3 (overview.ts:121-131), and the Story 2 inspect.view stub was replaced by the real composeViewReport — receipt-tracked risks are closed in code, not just in the log.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Shared vocabulary discipline holds: report shapes live in src/shared/inspect.ts and Story 3 consumes them without redefinition; messages.show reuses the owner's report(messageId) read (DD-2); one lifecycle script (test/fixtures/lifecycle.ts) drives all four TC-5 legs including the process-suite leg (DD-8).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Confirmed cross-story semantic drift (the blocker): Flow 4's health.queue counts form-report entries with live queue joins, while Epic 02's queue-detail surfaces (listQueuedWork) count work items — a turn_derivation work item owning both turn forms is counted twice by health (EV-04-001).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Cumulative test baseline never regressed across the four accepted stories (290 → 300 → 313 → 320 → 328 default tests); epic gate re-run for this canonical review: 366/366 tests across 44 files with the process suite executed.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"All four story receipts in team-impl-log.md show accepted status with verifier PASS and disposition of every finding; approved rulings are reflected accurately in shipped code — but the health-queue substitution was never the subject of any ruling.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"architectureFindings\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Composition discipline is structural: domains/inspect imports only other domains' index surfaces, check-boundaries.mjs carries the inspect allowlist plus the Epic 04 source check rejecting raw storage access from domains/inspect/**, and the gate runs it; the domain-graph addition is acyclic as designed.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Read-only is enforced at two layers per DD-6: every new operation runs under touch suppression, and tests assert absence-of-delta over observable state including the Story 3 storedView field added to the snapshot.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Tech design Flow 4 is internally ambiguous and the ambiguity was resolved silently in code: the flow names listQueuedWork (both owners) as a health input and the External Contracts table lists it as 'Used by ... health', yet the same paragraph says 'queue = queued/claimed counts from the live-item joins'. health.ts implemented the latter reading and never calls listQueuedWork; no design deviation was recorded and no ruling was sought (feeds EV-04-001).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"DD-5 ('overview takes boundary via status') is impossible as written: ViewStatus (src/shared/view.ts:128-135) exposes no boundaryPosition, so overview.ts:121-128 reads it from pull.meta — the only surface exposing it — at the cost of a full pull render per overview call. Surface-respecting, but unrecorded in the tech design (E04-ER1-002).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Approved public-contract deviations are implemented exactly as ruled: loadCost.bandTokens measured from pull-served band messages (view-report.ts:67, ruling-011 Story 3), sourceState/meta nullable for never-compacted threads, threadId-normalized materialized-file equality with literal pull-output hash equality (lifecycle.test.ts:17-22, ruling-011 Story 4).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Error model and CLI conventions inherited cleanly: one additive caller_error code (invalid_bounds, approved); CLI commands are thin argv-to-SDK wrappers so parity including refusals is structural.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"epicCoverageAssessment\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"22 of 23 ACs and 16 of 17 TCs are implemented, traceable, and green: test describe blocks name their TC/AC pairs across inspect-overview, inspect-view, messages-read, inspect-health, lifecycle, and cli-process-inspect test files; epic gate re-run by this canonical review passed (pnpm verify-all, exit 0, 366/366 tests, 44 files, process suite ran).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"AC-4.5 is not conclusively covered as written: TC-4.1's and TC-4.3's shipped assertions (inspect-health.test.ts:63, :174) pin the form-entry semantics — queue equals pending+retrying entry counts — not 'work counts from the owners' queue detail'; on the post-edit cascade the pinned value (queued:5) diverges from the queue-detail work-item count (4).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"AC-5.3/AC-5.4 ship as threadId-normalized file equality per approved Story 4 ruling-011 (the thread id is the design's one random value; everything else byte-identical, Date frozen so pull-output hash equality stays literal); the relaxation is principled and recorded in the story spec, but epic-level docs still carry the superseded literal wording (EV-04-002).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Zero-provider NFR verified structurally for the whole epic: inspect/list/show/describe run green under a throwing provider; the lifecycle's only provider is the registry's deterministic one.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Closeout items still owed: the M3/M4 quality-half transfer to the inference epic must be recorded, and the sanctioned PRD/tech-arch search-deferral backfill exists only as uncommitted working-tree changes (see unresolvedItems).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"productionPathFindings\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"No fake adapters, shims, or placeholder execution paths remain on any production path: the not_implemented code survives only as vocabulary in shared/errors.ts, the Story 2 inspect.view stub was replaced by the real implementation in Story 3, and the lifecycle exercise runs the real production SDK through the production registry.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Provider posture is honest by construction: the registry contains exactly one provider (deterministic — the spec'd v1 state), there is no default provider, and the CLI fails with provider_not_configured rather than silently substituting.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Confirmed material production-path issue (EV-04-001): inspect.health's queue section reports per-form-entry counts, not live work-item counts; on the standard mutation-in-flight cascade it reports queued:5 while the owners' listQueuedWork surfaces hold 4 work items, so the report disagrees with the queue-detail surface an operator would drill into.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Cosmetic only: src/cli/index.ts:473-475 still carries the stub-era comment describing 'inspect view' as not-implemented; the route is real and in the help text (E04-ER1-003).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"blockingFindings\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"EV-04-001\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"severity\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"major\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"title\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Health queue counts are form-entry counts, not the queue-detail work counts AC-4.5 specifies — confirmed divergence, pinned by the shipped tests\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"evidence\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Epic AC-4.5 requires 'queued and claimed work counts from the owners' queue detail'; tech-design Flow 4 and its External Contracts table name listQueuedWork (both owners) as a health input. composeHealth (src/domains/inspect/internal/health.ts:40-106) reads only messages.report + turns.report and increments queue once per FormReportEntry carrying a queue join (lines 89-95); it never calls listQueuedWork. The divergence is structural: the turns report join maps BOTH turn forms (turn_rendering, lower_band_projection) to the one turn_derivation work-item kind (src/domains/turns/internal/forms.ts:266,294-301), so one live turn-derivation item is counted twice. The shipped default suite pins the divergent value: inspect-health.test.ts:174 asserts queue {queued:5, claimed:0} for the post-edit cascade whose owners' queue detail holds exactly 4 work items (prompt_smoothing, turn_derivation, chunk_summary_brief, chunk_summary_detailed) — matching epic-reviewer-2's independent reproduction. This violates AC-4.5's source clause and the epic NFR 'where a report describes another surface's output ... equality is contractual', and no approved ruling covers the substitution. Human ruling required because both fix paths alter spec'd contract text: counting work items from queue detail breaks the queued+claimed = pending+retrying identity the current TC-4.1 assertion leans on, while ratifying form-entry semantics requires amending AC-4.5, tech-design Flow 4/External Contracts, and the test-plan TC-4.1 spine.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"affectedFiles\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/src/domains/inspect/internal/health.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/test/inspect-health.test.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/test/lifecycle.test.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/epic.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/tech-design.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/test-plan.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"requirementIds\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"AC-4.5\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"TC-4.1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tech-design Flow 4: Health\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"recommendedFixScope\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"human-ruling\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"blocking\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":true}],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"nonBlockingFindings\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"EV-04-002\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"severity\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"minor\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"title\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Epic-level artifacts not updated for approved ruling-011 deviations (threadId-normalized file equality; measured loadCost.bandTokens)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"evidence\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Merged from E04-ER1-001 and EV-04-002; all citations verified. Story 4 ruling-011 (approved) relaxed materialized-file equality to substitute only the random thread id (implemented in lifecycle.test.ts:17-22 and the file-equality assertions), but epic.md AC-5.3/TC-5.2 (lines 149, 156), test-plan.md TC-5.2 (line 38), and stories/coverage.md (line 48) still say byte-identical/hash-equal with no qualifier. Story 3 ruling-011 (approved) made loadCost.bandTokens measured from pull-served band messages (view-report.ts:67), but tech-design.md DD-4 (line 94) still says 'sum of stored band counts', and stories/03-view-contents-report.md's Spec Deviations section still says 'None.' despite that approved public-contract deviation. Code and tests implement the ruled contracts correctly; only the docs lag.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"affectedFiles\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/epic.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/test-plan.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/stories/coverage.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/tech-design.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/stories/03-view-contents-report.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"requirementIds\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"AC-2.3\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"AC-2.5\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"AC-5.3\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"AC-5.4\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"TC-5.2\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"recommendedFixScope\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"quick-fix\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"blocking\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":false},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"E04-ER1-002\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"severity\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"observation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"title\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Tech design DD-5 not backfilled: overview reads the visibility boundary from pull.meta because ViewStatus exposes no boundaryPosition\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"evidence\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tech-design.md DD-5 says overview composes boundary/zone 'via status', but ViewStatus (src/shared/view.ts:128-135) has no boundaryPosition field — overview.ts:121-128 reads it from pull.meta, the only surface exposing it, at the cost of a full pull render per overview call. Surface-respecting and AC-1.1-satisfying, but unrecorded as a design deviation. (The health half of the original finding — the listQueuedWork substitution — is escalated into blocking finding EV-04-001 and not duplicated here.)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"affectedFiles\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/tech-design.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/src/domains/inspect/internal/overview.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"requirementIds\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"AC-1.1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"recommendedFixScope\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"quick-fix\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"blocking\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":false},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"E04-ER1-003\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"severity\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"observation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"title\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Stale stub-era comment on the 'inspect view' CLI route\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"evidence\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"src/cli/index.ts:473-475 still says the route 'returns the structured not-implemented result; Story 3 makes it real (and adds it to the help text)' — Story 3 landed, runInspectView is the real implementation, and the command is in the help text. Comment is false; code is correct.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"affectedFiles\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/src/cli/index.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"requirementIds\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"recommendedFixScope\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"quick-fix\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"blocking\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":false}],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"unresolvedItems\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Human ruling required for EV-04-001: decide whether HealthReport.queue counts live work items (per epic AC-4.5's 'work counts from the owners' queue detail' — requires reworking health.ts to consume listQueuedWork and re-stating TC-4.1's consistency clause, since queued+claimed would no longer equal pending+retrying when a turn derivation is in flight) or pending/retrying form entries (per the shipped implementation and tests — requires amending AC-4.5, tech-design Flow 4 and External Contracts, and the test-plan TC-4.1 spine to ratify the deviation).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Uncommitted working-tree changes carry the sanctioned PRD AC-4.3 search-deferral backfill (packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/00-prd.md, 01-tech-arch.md) plus impl-log bookkeeping — epic-mandated doc changes that must be committed as part of epic closeout.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Epic closeout must record the M3/M4 quality-half transfer to the inference epic explicitly (epic.md Assumptions); not yet written — this review is upstream of that closeout step.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"gateResult\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"pass\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"verifierResults\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"resultId\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"6b026f0b-72fd-4f16-88b6-28f3f56e2b90\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"outcome\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"pass\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"provider\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"claude-code\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"model\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"claude-fable-5\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"reviewerLabel\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"epic-reviewer-1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"crossStoryFindings\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Story 2 → Story 3 handoff completed as tracked: overview's interim view-summary source (pull().meta, Story 2 approved deviation) was swapped to threadView.describe in Story 3 (overview.ts), and the Story 2 inspect.view stub was replaced with the real composeViewReport. The receipt-tracked risk is closed in code, not just in the log.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Shared vocabulary discipline holds across stories: report shapes live in src/shared/inspect.ts (Story 2) and Story 3 consumes them without redefinition; messages.show reuses the owner's reportMessageForms read (DD-2) rather than new join machinery; the lifecycle fixture (test/fixtures/lifecycle.ts) is one script driven by all four TC-5 legs including the process-suite leg (DD-8), so replay/teardown/CLI runs exercise the same sequence.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The read-only delta helper (test/fixtures/read-only-delta.ts, Story 2) wraps Story 1's ops retroactively as the test plan required: inspect-overview/view/health tests use expectReadOnly, messages-read uses the same observableState snapshot directly, and the Story 3 storedView field was added to the snapshot so a forgotten view touch is caught even when pull meta would not show it.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Cumulative baseline never regressed: 290 → 300 → 313 → 320 → 328 default tests across the four accepted stories; epic gate run for this review shows 366 tests / 44 files with the process suite included.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"All four story receipts in team-impl-log.md show accepted status with verifier PASS, impl-lead-run gates, and disposition of every finding (SV-01-001..005, SV-03-001, SV-04-001); approved rulings (023 strict-read-purity, 011 Story 3 loadCost/sourceState, 011 Story 4 threadId-normalized file equality) are all reflected accurately in the shipped code.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"architectureFindings\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Composition discipline is structural, not aspirational: domains/inspect imports only the five other domains' index surfaces (verified in source), check-boundaries.mjs carries the inspect dependency allowlist plus the Epic 04 source check rejecting raw storage access from domains/inspect/** (scripts/check-boundaries.mjs:77-115), and the gate runs it. The domain graph addition is acyclic as designed.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Read-only is enforced at two layers as DD-6 demanded: every new operation (inspect.overview/view/health, threadView.describe, messages.listMessages/show) runs under runWithThreadTouchSuppressed so a background scheduler can never hang a catch-up drain off a read, and tests assert absence-of-delta over observable state including raw derived_form rows.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Minor drift from tech-design mechanics, surface-respecting in both cases: (1) DD-5 says overview takes boundary 'via status' but ViewStatus does not expose boundaryPosition — the implementation reads it from pull.meta (overview.ts:125-129, commented), which is the only surface exposing it; side effect is that every overview call performs a full pull render. (2) Tech design Flow 4 names listQueuedWork as a health input, but health.ts derives queue counts from the report entries' queue joins instead — consistent-by-construction with the same report's state counts, which is arguably stronger for AC-4.5, but the tech design was not updated to record either substitution.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Approved public-contract deviations are implemented exactly as ruled: loadCost.bandTokens measured from pull-served band messages rather than stored sums (ruling-011 Story 3; AC-2.3's equality is the governing contract and the shared/inspect.ts comment documents why stored sums structurally cannot match), and sourceState/meta nullable for never-compacted threads.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Error model and CLI conventions inherited cleanly: one additive caller_error code (invalid_bounds, Story 1 approved ruling, Epic 03 precedent), no other additions; CLI commands are thin argv-to-SDK wrappers with semantic validation kept in the SDK so parity including refusals is structural (cli/inspect.ts, cli/messages-read.ts).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"epicCoverageAssessment\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"All 23 ACs and 17 TCs are implemented and traceable: test describe blocks name their TC/AC pairs explicitly across inspect-overview.test.ts (TC-1.1–1.3), inspect-view.test.ts (TC-2.1–2.3 + describe purity legs), messages-read.test.ts (TC-3.1–3.3 + three architecture-risk suites), inspect-health.test.ts (TC-4.1–4.3 + provider-free leg), lifecycle.test.ts (TC-5.1–5.3 with all four AC-5.2 checkpoint assertions including the receipt-vs-health cross-check), and cli-process-inspect.test.ts (TC-3.4, TC-5.4, process suite).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"AC-5.3/AC-5.4 materialized-file equality ships as threadId-normalized equality per approved Story 4 ruling-011: thread id is the design's one random value, the test substitutes only it and requires every other byte identical, with Date frozen so pull-output hash equality stays literal with zero normalization. The relaxation is principled, documented in the test header, and recorded in the story spec.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Zero-provider NFR is verified structurally for the whole epic: inspect/list/show/describe run green under a throwing provider, and the lifecycle's only provider is the registry's deterministic one (zero network, zero real-provider calls — AC-5.1).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Epic gate run by this reviewer: pnpm verify-all PASS, exit 0, 366/366 tests across 44 files with the process suite executed (LHC_PROCESS_SUITE=1 is baked into verify-all), build/typecheck/lint/boundaries all green.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Closeout items still owed per the epic's own assumptions: the M3/M4 quality-half transfer to the inference epic must be recorded at closeout, and the search-deferral PRD/tech-arch backfill exists but is uncommitted (see unresolvedItems).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"productionPathFindings\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"No fake adapters, shims, or placeholder execution paths remain on any production path. Grep for stub/placeholder/not-implemented across src/ finds only SQL parameter placeholders, comments, and the not_implemented code's vocabulary entry in shared/errors.ts — no operation returns it anymore; the Story 2 inspect.view stub was replaced by the real implementation in Story 3.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Provider posture is honest by construction: the registry (src/providers/registry.ts) contains exactly one provider — deterministic — which is the spec'd v1 state (real inference is explicitly next-PRD scope, carried in the epic's Out of Scope). There is no default provider: the CLI resolves only an explicitly named provider (--provider/LHC_PROVIDER) and fails with provider_not_configured rather than silently substituting the deterministic one.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The lifecycle exercise runs the real production SDK (createSdk) in background mode through the production registry's deterministic provider and the real drainSettled awaitable — no test-only seams in the sequence itself; the one test-only control is freezing Date in the test file, which is outside the production path.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Stale comment, cosmetic only: src/cli/index.ts:473-475 still describes 'inspect view' as a not-implemented stub that 'Story 3 makes real' — Story 3 landed, the route is real, and the command is in the help text. The comment is now false; the code is correct.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"blockingFindings\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"nonBlockingFindings\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"E04-ER1-001\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"severity\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"minor\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"title\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Epic-level docs still carry literal 'byte-identical' phrasing superseded by approved ruling-011 (threadId-normalized file equality)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"evidence\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"epic.md AC-5.3/TC-5.2 (lines 149, 156-157), test-plan.md TC-5.2 (line 38), and coverage.md line 48 state byte-identical materialized-file equality; Story 4 ruling-011 (approved, recorded in team-impl-log.md and stories/04-lifecycle-exercise.md) relaxed file equality to substitute the one random thread id, with pull-output hash equality remaining literal. The impl log itself flags this for epic-review awareness. The shipped test (lifecycle.test.ts:17-22, 97-104) implements the ruled contract correctly; only the epic-level docs lag.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"affectedFiles\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/epic.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/test-plan.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/stories/coverage.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"requirementIds\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"AC-5.3\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"AC-5.4\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"TC-5.2\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"recommendedFixScope\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"quick-fix\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"blocking\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":false},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"E04-ER1-002\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"severity\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"observation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"title\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Tech design not backfilled for two surface-respecting mechanism substitutions (overview boundary via pull.meta; health queue via report join)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"evidence\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tech-design.md DD-5 says overview takes boundary/zone 'via status', but ViewStatus (src/shared/view.ts:128-135) exposes no boundaryPosition — overview.ts:125-129 reads it from pull.meta, the only surface that exposes it, at the cost of a full pull render per overview call. Tech-design Flow 4 and the System View name listQueuedWork as a health input, but health.ts derives queue counts from FormReportEntry queue joins (consistent-by-construction with the same report's state counts). Both substitutions stay on surfaces and satisfy their ACs; neither is recorded as a design deviation.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"affectedFiles\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/tech-design.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/src/domains/inspect/internal/overview.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/src/domains/inspect/internal/health.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"requirementIds\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"AC-1.1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"AC-4.5\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"recommendedFixScope\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"quick-fix\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"blocking\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":false},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"E04-ER1-003\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"severity\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"observation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"title\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Stale stub-era comment on the 'inspect view' CLI route\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"evidence\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"src/cli/index.ts:473-475 says the route is 'routed ahead of its story so the CLI mirrors the SDK surface: the stub returns the structured not-implemented result; Story 3 makes it real (and adds it to the help text)' — Story 3 landed; runInspectView is the real implementation and the command appears in the help text (line 72). Comment is false, code is correct.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"affectedFiles\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/src/cli/index.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"requirementIds\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"recommendedFixScope\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"quick-fix\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"blocking\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":false}],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"unresolvedItems\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Uncommitted working-tree changes carry the sanctioned PRD AC-4.3 search-deferral backfill (packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/00-prd.md, 01-tech-arch.md) plus impl-log state bookkeeping — these are epic-mandated doc changes (epic.md Out of Scope: 'backfilled with this epic') and should be committed as part of epic closeout.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Epic closeout must record the M3/M4 quality-half transfer to the inference epic explicitly (epic.md Assumptions: 'Closeout records this explicitly') — not yet written; this review is upstream of that closeout step.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"gateResult\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"pass\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"resultId\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"7037aa32-e09d-4f0a-b954-6917da95a54b\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"outcome\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"block\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"provider\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"codex\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"model\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"gpt-5.5\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"reviewerLabel\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"epic-reviewer-2\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"crossStoryFindings\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Stories 1-4 are wired through the SDK and CLI and the configured gates pass: `pnpm verify` passed with 328 tests and `pnpm verify-all` passed with 366 tests / 44 files, including the process suite.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Flow 4 has unresolved cross-story semantic drift: `inspect.health.queue` reports one queued/claimed count per pending form report entry, while the epic and tech design describe queued/claimed work counts from owner queue detail. A turn-derivation work item that owns two forms is counted twice by health.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"architectureFindings\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"`domains/inspect/**` imports owner surfaces only; `check-boundaries` passed and the inspect source check found no raw SQL/table access in inspect.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"`inspect.view` and lifecycle determinism intentionally follow approved rulings, but the epic-level artifacts still contain older wording for materialized-file byte identity and the tech design still says `loadCost.bandTokens` is the stored band-count sum.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"epicCoverageAssessment\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Most epic ACs have direct default/process suite coverage across `messages-read`, `inspect-overview`, `inspect-health`, `inspect-view`, `lifecycle`, and `cli-process-inspect`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"AC-4.5 is not conclusively covered because the tests assert consistency with pending form-entry counts, not queue-detail work-item counts.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"productionPathFindings\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"No material fake adapter, shim, placeholder, or non-real production execution path was found in the audited SDK/CLI inspect, message-read, thread-view, and lifecycle paths.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Material production-path issue: `inspect.health` derives queue counts from `FormReportEntry.queue` per form entry and does not count live queue work rows; on a mutation-in-flight production fixture it returned `{queued:5, claimed:0}` while owner `listQueuedWork` surfaces showed four live work items.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"blockingFindings\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"EV-04-001\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"severity\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"major\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"title\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Health queue counts do not match queue-detail work counts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"evidence\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Epic AC-4.5 requires queued and claimed work counts from owner queue detail; tech-design Flow 4 says `health` composes `messages.report` + `turns.report` + `listQueuedWork` both owners. In `src/domains/inspect/internal/health.ts:40-105`, `composeHealth` only reads `messages.report` and `turns.report`, then increments `queue` once for every report entry with `entry.queue` at lines 89-95. In an ad hoc mutation-in-flight fixture, `inspect.health` returned `healthQueue:{queued:5,claimed:0}` while `messages.listQueuedWork` + `turns.listQueuedWork` returned four work item ids: `w-m5-prompt_smoothing-v2`, `w-t2-turn_derivation-v2`, `w-c1-chunk_summary_brief-v2`, `w-c1-chunk_summary_detailed-v2`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"affectedFiles\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/src/domains/inspect/internal/health.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/test/inspect-health.test.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/test/lifecycle.test.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/epic.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/tech-design.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/test-plan.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"requirementIds\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"AC-4.5\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"TC-4.1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tech-design Flow 4: Health\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"recommendedFixScope\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"human-ruling\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"blocking\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":true}],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"nonBlockingFindings\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"EV-04-002\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"severity\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"minor\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"title\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Accepted deviation wording is not propagated through all epic-level artifacts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"evidence\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Story 4 and ruling-011 accept materialized-file equality after substituting only the random threadId, and tests implement that in `test/lifecycle.test.ts:97-103` and `:282-302`. The epic and test plan still say materialized files are byte-identical without that qualifier at `epic.md:149-157` and `test-plan.md:38-39`. Story 3/ruling-011 accepts measured pull-served `loadCost.bandTokens` and nullable `sourceState`, while `tech-design.md:94` still says `loadCost.bandTokens` is the sum of stored band counts and `stories/03-view-contents-report.md:119-121` still says no deviations.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"affectedFiles\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/epic.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/tech-design.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/test-plan.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/stories/03-view-contents-report.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/stories/04-lifecycle-exercise.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"requirementIds\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"AC-2.3\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"AC-2.5\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"AC-5.3\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"AC-5.4\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"recommendedFixScope\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"quick-fix\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"blocking\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":false}],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"unresolvedItems\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Decide whether `HealthReport.queue` is intended to count live work items or pending/retrying form entries. The current implementation and tests use form-entry counts; the epic/tech-design wording points to queue-detail work counts.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"gateResult\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"pass\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}]},\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"errors\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"warnings\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"artifacts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"result-envelope\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"path\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/artifacts/epic/001-epic-review.json\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"startedAt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"2026-06-12T15:43:43.996Z\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"finishedAt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"2026-06-12T15:54:28.853Z\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"numLines\\\\\\\":2,\\\\\\\"startLine\\\\\\\":1,\\\\\\\"totalLines\\\\\\\":2}}}\\\\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/04-inspection/artifacts/epic/streams/002-epic-reverify.stdout.log:38:{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"user\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"message\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"role\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"user\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"content\\\\\\\":[{\\\\\\\"tool_use_id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"toolu_01BNNJTAK1uEH3PoMhRhJjxc\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"tool_result\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"content\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"1\\\\\\\\t# Epic 04: Inspection\\\\\\\\n2\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n3\\\\\\\\t**Status:** Validated — ready for story publishing and tech design\\\\\\\\n4\\\\\\\\t**PRD:** `../00-prd.md` Feature 4\\\\\\\\n5\\\\\\\\t**Tech Arch:** `../01-tech-arch.md`\\\\\\\\n6\\\\\\\\t**Depends on:** Epic 01 (record and read-back), Epic 02 (report/requeue surfaces, mutation cascade), Epic 03 (view storage, status, pull — in build; consumed as spec'd)\\\\\\\\n7\\\\\\\\t**Counts:** 23 ACs / 17 TCs across 5 flows\\\\\\\\n8\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n9\\\\\\\\t## Onboarding Context\\\\\\\\n10\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n11\\\\\\\\tRead first: `../../01-onboard/02-domain-design.md` §Inspect (the domain's charter: read-only, reads through other domains' surfaces, never writes, repairs, or derives), §Messages (read ownership), §Thread view (the view state this epic reports on). The Epic 02 spec pack defines the report surfaces this epic composes (`messages.report`, `turns.report`, queue detail). The Epic 03 spec pack defines the view storage and status shapes the view-contents report reads.\\\\\\\\n12\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n13\\\\\\\\tThis is the last epic of the v1 PRD. It adds no tables, no migration, no derivations, and no provider use. It completes the read side of the product and closes with a full-surface lifecycle exercise — the first time every epic's surface runs in one sequence.\\\\\\\\n14\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n15\\\\\\\\t## User Profile\\\\\\\\n16\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n17\\\\\\\\t**Primary user:** the operator (Lee, mentees later) auditing threads from the CLI; secondarily, agents inside a harness using the same reads mid-task, and — for Flow 5 only — the future PI extension, whose call order the lifecycle exercise rehearses.\\\\\\\\n18\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n19\\\\\\\\t**Context:** the operator arrives with a question — what does this thread hold, what did the agent actually see, did the rebuild land, why is this view degraded — and acts on the answer with Feature 2's mutation and requeue operations. Inspect tells them where to point those.\\\\\\\\n20\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n21\\\\\\\\t**Mental model:** \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"inspect describes; it never changes. Every report reads the same state the serving surfaces read, so a report never disagrees with what the agent was actually served. When a report names a subject, I can drill to it — view entry → chunk → turn → message → blocks.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n22\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n23\\\\\\\\t**Key constraint:** read-only, structurally — no inspect operation writes rows, transitions state, or moves the boundary; and no operation in this epic invokes a provider. Reports compose the owning domains' surfaces; inspect owns no rows and reads no other domain's tables directly.\\\\\\\\n24\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n25\\\\\\\\t## Feature Overview\\\\\\\\n26\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n27\\\\\\\\tInspection answers questions about a thread without changing it. Four reads: a thread overview (size, composition, derivation state at a glance), a view-contents report (what the active view holds, entry by entry, and what loading it costs), message listing and viewing (the drill-down target every other report points into), and a derivation health report (counts by state, failures with reasons, what repair would touch). Plus the lifecycle exercise: the whole SDK surface driven end-to-end in PI-extension call order.\\\\\\\\n28\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n29\\\\\\\\tEverything here reads through the owning domains' surfaces. Inspect owns no rows. The one surface addition outside the inspect domain: `thread-view` gains a read-only `describe` operation exposing the stored view row (arrangement, gaps, config, provenance), because inspect may not read `thread_view` tables directly and no existing operation exposes them.\\\\\\\\n30\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n31\\\\\\\\t### Flow Summary\\\\\\\\n32\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n33\\\\\\\\t| Flow | Name | Surface |\\\\\\\\n34\\\\\\\\t|------|------|---------|\\\\\\\\n35\\\\\\\\t| 1 | Thread overview | `inspect.overview` |\\\\\\\\n36\\\\\\\\t| 2 | View contents and load cost | `inspect.view` (+ `threadView.describe`) |\\\\\\\\n37\\\\\\\\t| 3 | Message listing and viewing | `messages.list` / `messages.show` |\\\\\\\\n38\\\\\\\\t| 4 | Derivation health and rebuild visibility | `inspect.health` |\\\\\\\\n39\\\\\\\\t| 5 | Full-surface lifecycle exercise | test-only; no new surface |\\\\\\\\n40\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n41\\\\\\\\t## Scope\\\\\\\\n42\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n43\\\\\\\\t### In Scope\\\\\\\\n44\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n45\\\\\\\\t- `inspect` domain surface: `overview`, `view`, `health` — read-only composition over owning domains' reports\\\\\\\\n46\\\\\\\\t- `threadView.describe`: read-only exposure of the stored active view (arrangement entries, gaps, config, source-state provenance)\\\\\\\\n47\\\\\\\\t- Message read completion on the `messages` surface: listing options (bounds, include-deleted) and single-message view with blocks and derivation form states\\\\\\\\n48\\\\\\\\t- CLI: `lhc inspect overview|view|health`, `lhc messages list` (extended), `lhc messages show` — JSON, SDK-parity, process-suite legs\\\\\\\\n49\\\\\\\\t- The lifecycle exercise: create → intake → drain → status → compact → pull → inspect → mutate → rebuild → compact → pull → materialize, through the real SDK, deterministic provider, with replay-equality and instance-teardown legs\\\\\\\\n50\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n51\\\\\\\\t### Out of Scope\\\\\\\\n52\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n53\\\\\\\\t- **Message search — deferred post-v1.** No search ships in v1. The shape (substring vs FTS, ranking, result granularity) gets decided from real usage after LHC and the PI extension are integrated. The PRD and tech arch carry this deferral (Feature 4 scope, Future Directions, tech-arch deferred list) — backfilled with this epic, so tech design inherits no contradiction.\\\\\\\\n54\\\\\\\\t- Mutations and repair execution (Feature 2 owns; inspect reports on them and previews repair, never executes)\\\\\\\\n55\\\\\\\\t- Any new derivation, any provider call, any real inference (next PRD cycle owns the inference feature)\\\\\\\\n56\\\\\\\\t- The PI extension itself (next PRD)\\\\\\\\n57\\\\\\\\t- Watch/follow modes, streaming output, UI rendering — CLI is JSON one-shots\\\\\\\\n58\\\\\\\\t- Cross-thread reports (single thread scope, same as every epic)\\\\\\\\n59\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n60\\\\\\\\t### Assumptions\\\\\\\\n61\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n62\\\\\\\\t- Epic 03 is in build. This epic consumes its surfaces as spec'd (`status`, `pull`, view storage shapes). **Handoff gate:** before this epic's tech design freezes, the tech design must check Epic 02's and Epic 03's final deviation tables and update every consumed contract this epic references; the tech design records that check's outcome in its Spec Validation table.\\\\\\\\n63\\\\\\\\t- Epic 03's Story 0 fixture (deterministic provider, ~12 turns, 4 chunks, tool-heavy middle, manufactured failure states) is reusable here and extends with mutation-in-flight states; no new fixture family.\\\\\\\\n64\\\\\\\\t- Schema is terminal at v6 for this PRD: this epic ships **no migration**. If tech design finds a needed index, that is a v7 migration decision to surface, not assume.\\\\\\\\n65\\\\\\\\t- The deleted-message contract is Epic 02's, inherited: deleted messages drop from reads, source events retain the originals. This epic adds the audit option to see them, not a new policy.\\\\\\\\n66\\\\\\\\t- M3/M4 milestone gates close mechanically only: view *quality* cannot be reviewed while bands render deterministic-provider output. The quality halves of those gates transfer to the inference feature's epic. Closeout records this explicitly.\\\\\\\\n67\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n68\\\\\\\\t## Flow 1: Thread Overview\\\\\\\\n69\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n70\\\\\\\\tOne call answers \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"what is this thread\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": identity, record size, composition, derivation state, view state, visibility state. The numbers an operator scans before deciding to compact, repair, or drill in.\\\\\\\\n71\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n72\\\\\\\\t#### Acceptance Criteria\\\\\\\\n73\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n74\\\\\\\\t- **AC-1.1**: `inspect.overview` returns, in one read-only call: thread identity and metadata; event count and order span; message counts (total visible, by kind, deleted counted separately) with visible token sum; turn counts (open, closed); chunk count and closed-but-unchunked turn count; derivation counts by state (pending, retrying, failed, blocked, ready); active-view summary (viewId, createdAt, compactPoint, coveredFrom) or null when never compacted; visibility boundary position and current zone token sum.\\\\\\\\n75\\\\\\\\t- **AC-1.2**: Counts honor the deleted contract: deleted messages appear only in the deleted count — excluded from visible counts, kind breakdowns, and token sums; event counts are unaffected (the record retains everything).\\\\\\\\n76\\\\\\\\t- **AC-1.3**: Every thread shape reports cleanly: fresh-empty, mid-first-turn, never-compacted, compacted, mid-rebuild. Absent pieces report as zeros or null — never omitted fields, never shape errors.\\\\\\\\n77\\\\\\\\t- **AC-1.4**: The overview is a pure read: no work items created, no state changed, repeated calls with no intervening writes return identical results.\\\\\\\\n78\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n79\\\\\\\\t#### Test Conditions\\\\\\\\n80\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n81\\\\\\\\t- **TC-1.1** (AC-1.1, AC-1.3): Overview shape variants: fresh-empty, mid-first-turn, never-compacted-with-record, compacted, and mid-rebuild all return the full shape with absent pieces as zeros/nulls. The compacted tool-heavy fixture asserts exact expected counts (messages by kind, turns, chunks, derivation states, view summary, boundary).\\\\\\\\n82\\\\\\\\t- **TC-1.2** (AC-1.2): Delete one message; overview → visible count and token sum drop, deleted count = 1, kind breakdown excludes it, event count unchanged.\\\\\\\\n83\\\\\\\\t- **TC-1.3** (AC-1.4): Overview twice with no writes between → deep-equal results; no `work_item` rows created; zero provider calls.\\\\\\\\n84\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n85\\\\\\\\t## Flow 2: View Contents and Load Cost\\\\\\\\n86\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n87\\\\\\\\tWhat the agent actually sees, and what it costs. The report reads the stored active view (via `threadView.describe`) and the live tail, entry by entry, with the cost a pull would incur right now.\\\\\\\\n88\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n89\\\\\\\\t#### Acceptance Criteria\\\\\\\\n90\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n91\\\\\\\\t- **AC-2.1**: The report names the active view — viewId, createdAt, profile name and resolved config, compactPoint, coveredFrom — and lists every band entry in served order (brief → detailed → smooth): subject kind and id, form used, degraded flag; plus every gap with its reason; plus per-band stored token counts. All of it from the stored snapshot, not recomputed.\\\\\\\\n92\\\\\\\\t- **AC-2.2**: The tail section reports message count and token cost *as currently served*: tool results at-or-behind the visibility boundary are costed at their short form, everything else full.\\\\\\\\n93\\\\\\\\t- **AC-2.3**: `loadCost` totals what `pull` serves now — bands plus tail — and a test asserts equality against an actual pull's measured content. The report never disagrees with the surface it describes.\\\\\\\\n94\\\\\\\\t- **AC-2.4**: A never-compacted thread reports view: null with a tail-only loadCost under the same equality contract (the whole record served as tail).\\\\\\\\n95\\\\\\\\t- **AC-2.5**: The report carries the view's recorded source-state provenance (what the compact saw: max event order, form counts) and is a pure read under AC-1.4's contract.\\\\\\\\n96\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n97\\\\\\\\t#### Test Conditions\\\\\\\\n98\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n99\\\\\\\\t- **TC-2.1** (AC-2.1, AC-2.5): On a compacted fixture with one degraded entry and one gap → report matches the stored arrangement exactly: subjects, forms used, degraded flags, gap reasons, band token counts, config, provenance.\\\\\\\\n100\\\\\\\\t- **TC-2.2** (AC-2.2, AC-2.3): On a boundary-advanced fixture → tail cost counts short forms short; `loadCost.total` equals the token measure of an actual `pull`'s messages using the same estimator.\\\\\\\\n101\\\\\\\\t- **TC-2.3** (AC-2.4): Never-compacted thread → view null, tail spans the record, cost-parity assertion holds.\\\\\\\\n102\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n103\\\\\\\\t## Flow 3: Message Listing and Viewing\\\\\\\\n104\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n105\\\\\\\\tThe drill-down floor. Every report names subjects; this flow is where an operator or agent reads the actual record. Epic 01 shipped `listMessages` bare; this flow completes it and adds the single-message view.\\\\\\\\n106\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n107\\\\\\\\t#### Acceptance Criteria\\\\\\\\n108\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n109\\\\\\\\t- **AC-3.1**: Listing returns messages in record order with kind, block summary, token estimate, turn membership, and deleted status, with bounded-listing options (range/limit) so large threads list without loading everything.\\\\\\\\n110\\\\\\\\t- **AC-3.2**: `messages.show` returns one message in full: every block with complete content (the record — full tool results, not view-shortened forms), token estimate, turn membership, and the message's derivation forms with their states and metadata (joined from the owner's report, including tool-outcome metadata where present).\\\\\\\\n111\\\\\\\\t- **AC-3.3**: Deleted messages are excluded by default and listable with an explicit include-deleted option, marked deleted — never silently mixed in. `show` on a deleted message returns the record marked deleted (audit is the point), never a not-found.\\\\\\\\n112\\\\\\\\t- **AC-3.4**: CLI parity: `lhc messages list` and `lhc messages show` mirror the SDK operations — same options, same result JSON.\\\\\\\\n113\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n114\\\\\\\\t#### Test Conditions\\\\\\\\n115\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n116\\\\\\\\t- **TC-3.1** (AC-3.1): List on the fixture → record order, kinds, token estimates, turn ids correct; range and limit options honored exactly.\\\\\\\\n117\\\\\\\\t- **TC-3.2** (AC-3.2): Show on a drained tool-result message → full original content present, forms listed with states, outcome metadata present.\\\\\\\\n118\\\\\\\\t- **TC-3.3** (AC-3.3): Delete one message → default list excludes it; include-deleted lists it marked; show on its id → record with deleted flag.\\\\\\\\n119\\\\\\\\t- **TC-3.4** (AC-3.4): Spawned-CLI list and show on a fixture thread → JSON equals the in-process SDK results (process suite).\\\\\\\\n120\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n121\\\\\\\\t## Flow 4: Derivation Health and Rebuild Visibility\\\\\\\\n122\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n123\\\\\\\\tThe state of every derived form, aggregated to act on. This is also where a mutation's rebuild is watched: cleared forms show pending with queued work, then land ready.\\\\\\\\n124\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n125\\\\\\\\t#### Acceptance Criteria\\\\\\\\n126\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n127\\\\\\\\t- **AC-4.1**: `inspect.health` aggregates across owners — counts by owner, form kind, and state (ready, pending, retrying, failed, blocked) — assembled entirely from the owners' report surfaces, never from direct `derived_form` or `work_item` reads.\\\\\\\\n128\\\\\\\\t- **AC-4.2**: Failures carry actionable detail: subject id, form kind, reason, attempts, last error — enough to decide and target a requeue without raw SQL.\\\\\\\\n129\\\\\\\\t- **AC-4.3**: The report previews repair: which forms a requeue pass would touch (failed and not blocked), reported and never executed.\\\\\\\\n130\\\\\\\\t- **AC-4.4**: Rebuild visibility: after an edit or delete, health shows the cascade-cleared forms as pending with their queued work visible; after the queue drains, the same forms report ready. Two reads bracket a rebuild.\\\\\\\\n131\\\\\\\\t- **AC-4.5**: Live queue visibility: `health.queue` reports queued and claimed counts **per form-report entry**, tallied from each entry's live queue-item join in the owners' reports — every pending/retrying form rides one live queue item, so `queued + claimed` equals `pending + retrying` in the same report by construction. These are per-entry counts, not raw `listQueuedWork` work-item counts: one `turn_derivation` work item can back multiple form entries, so a raw work-item count would break that identity.\\\\\\\\n132\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n133\\\\\\\\t#### Test Conditions\\\\\\\\n134\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n135\\\\\\\\t- **TC-4.1** (AC-4.1, AC-4.5): Fixture with manufactured mixed states (ready, failed-transient, failed-permanent, blocked, pending) → exact counts per owner, kind, and state; queue section consistent with pending counts.\\\\\\\\n136\\\\\\\\t- **TC-4.2** (AC-4.2, AC-4.3): Failed and blocked forms present → failure detail exact (subject, form, reason, attempts); repair preview lists exactly the failed-not-blocked set.\\\\\\\\n137\\\\\\\\t- **TC-4.3** (AC-4.4): Edit a mid-thread message → health shows the cascade's cleared set pending with queued work (exact subjects per the cascade contract); `drainSettled` → same set ready; nothing outside the cascade changed state.\\\\\\\\n138\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n139\\\\\\\\t## Flow 5: Full-Surface Lifecycle Exercise\\\\\\\\n140\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n141\\\\\\\\tNot a capstone — nothing here proves integration readiness, because no real inference exists yet (that gate belongs to the inference epic). This is the cross-epic seam proof: the first sequence that drives every surface the PI extension will call, in its call order, against one SDK instance. What it catches: contract drift between the three built epics' surfaces, lifecycle ordering bugs, state leaks between operations.\\\\\\\\n142\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n143\\\\\\\\tThe sequence (PI-extension call order): create thread → intake multi-turn tool-heavy batches → background drain settles → status → compact (profile) → pull → inspect (overview, view, health) → edit + delete → rebuild drains → health confirms → second compact → pull → materialize.\\\\\\\\n144\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n145\\\\\\\\t#### Acceptance Criteria\\\\\\\\n146\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n147\\\\\\\\t- **AC-5.1**: The full sequence completes through one real SDK configuration with the deterministic provider: every operation returns ok, zero network, zero real-provider calls.\\\\\\\\n148\\\\\\\\t- **AC-5.2**: Checkpoint coherence: post-compact pull serves bands + tail; post-mutation health shows the cleared set pending; post-drain health shows it ready; the second compact's view reflects post-edit content; the second compact receipt's sweep section agrees with the health report taken immediately before it.\\\\\\\\n149\\\\\\\\t- **AC-5.3**: End-to-end determinism: the whole sequence replayed on a fresh thread produces literally byte-identical pull outputs (they carry no thread id, so equality is asserted with no normalization), and a materialized file byte-identical after normalizing only the intentionally random thread id — the two runs' thread ids are asserted to differ and every other byte must be exact (Story 4 ruling-011).\\\\\\\\n150\\\\\\\\t- **AC-5.4**: No in-memory dependency: tearing down the SDK instance between phases and continuing on a fresh `createSdk` yields the same end state as the uninterrupted run.\\\\\\\\n151\\\\\\\\t- **AC-5.5**: Operator parity: inspect and view reads driven through the spawned CLI at checkpoints return the same JSON as the in-process SDK calls at those checkpoints.\\\\\\\\n152\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n153\\\\\\\\t#### Test Conditions\\\\\\\\n154\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n155\\\\\\\\t- **TC-5.1** (AC-5.1, AC-5.2): Scripted lifecycle with checkpoint assertions at each named step; receipt-vs-health cross-check exact.\\\\\\\\n156\\\\\\\\t- **TC-5.2** (AC-5.3): Replay on a fresh thread → literal hash equality on every pull output; materialized-file hash equality after substituting only the random thread id, asserting the two thread ids differ and every other byte is exact.\\\\\\\\n157\\\\\\\\t- **TC-5.3** (AC-5.4): Teardown and recreate the SDK between intake/compact/mutation phases → final pull and health identical to TC-5.1's, and the materialized file hash-equal after substituting only the random thread id (every other byte exact).\\\\\\\\n158\\\\\\\\t- **TC-5.4** (AC-5.5): Process-suite leg: spawned CLI inspect/view/messages reads at three checkpoints equal the in-process results.\\\\\\\\n159\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n160\\\\\\\\t## Data Contracts\\\\\\\\n161\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n162\\\\\\\\tContract-level shapes; field mechanics are tech design's. All operations `Promise<OpResult<...>>` taking `ThreadRef`; all read-only.\\\\\\\\n163\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n164\\\\\\\\t**`InspectOverview`**: thread (id, createdAt, metadata); events (count, span); messages (visible count, byKind, deletedCount, visibleTokens); turns (open, closed); chunks (count, unchunkedTurns); derivation (counts by state); view (summary | null); visibility (boundaryPosition, zoneTokens).\\\\\\\\n165\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n166\\\\\\\\t**`ViewContentsReport`**: meta (viewId, createdAt, profile, config, compactPoint, coveredFrom) | null; bands — ordered entries (subjectKind, subjectId, formUsed, degraded) and stored tokens per band; gaps (band, subjectId, reason); tail (messageCount, tokens as served); loadCost (bandTokens, tailTokens, total — equals what pull serves); sourceState (provenance as stored).\\\\\\\\n167\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n168\\\\\\\\t**`threadView.describe`**: the stored active view row exposed read-only — arrangement, gaps, config, source state, identity/timestamps. Absent view → ok with null (not an error). This is the one surface addition outside inspect; it exists so inspect never touches `thread_view` tables.\\\\\\\\n169\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n170\\\\\\\\t**`HealthReport`**: owners[] (owner, kind, counts by state); failures[] (owner, subjectKind, subjectId, form, reason, attempts, lastError?); repairPreview[] (owner, subjectKind, subjectId, form); queue (queued, claimed).\\\\\\\\n171\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n172\\\\\\\\t**`MessageDetail`** (show): the full `MessageRecord` (all blocks, full content, token estimate, turnId, deleted flag) plus forms: the owner's report entries for this message.\\\\\\\\n173\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n174\\\\\\\\t**List options**: bounded listing (range/limit) and includeDeleted; exact option names are tech design's, the *existence* of bounds and the deleted default are contract.\\\\\\\\n175\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n176\\\\\\\\t**CLI**: `lhc inspect overview|view|health`, `lhc messages list [--include-deleted] [bounds]`, `lhc messages show --message-id <id>` — JSON to stdout, SDK-parity, same error classes as every epic (`caller_error`, `state_corruption`, `system_error`).\\\\\\\\n177\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n178\\\\\\\\t## Non-Functional Requirements\\\\\\\\n179\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n180\\\\\\\\t- **Read-only, structurally**: no inspect operation writes — no work rows, no state transitions, no boundary movement. Asserted, not assumed.\\\\\\\\n181\\\\\\\\t- **No inference in this epic**: no Epic 04 operation invokes a provider; tests may use Epic 02 deterministic-provider setup (draining queued work through the fake) to prepare the derived states under test.\\\\\\\\n182\\\\\\\\t- **Local and fast**: reports compose domain-surface reads over one thread file; no network, no scans proportional to anything but the thread.\\\\\\\\n183\\\\\\\\t- **Consistency with served reality**: where a report describes another surface's output (loadCost vs pull), equality is contractual and tested, not aspirational.\\\\\\\\n184\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n185\\\\\\\\t## Tech Design Questions\\\\\\\\n186\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n187\\\\\\\\t1. `messages.show` shape: new operation vs `listMessages` option — and whether the forms join reuses `messages.report(messageId)` internally or a shared internal read.\\\\\\\\n188\\\\\\\\t2. `threadView.describe` mapping: stored row → report shape, and whether `describe` and `inspect.view` share assembly code with `status` (they read overlapping state).\\\\\\\\n189\\\\\\\\t3. `loadCost` mechanism: measure by internally invoking `pull` vs summing the same sources pull reads — equality is the contract either way; pick for cost and locality.\\\\\\\\n190\\\\\\\\t4. Health assembly: per-owner report calls composed in inspect vs a shared internal join — must stay on surfaces either way.\\\\\\\\n191\\\\\\\\t5. Lifecycle exercise placement: which legs run in the default suite (TC-5.1 likely) vs the process suite (TC-5.2/5.3/5.4), and where the scripted sequence lives so both legs share it.\\\\\\\\n192\\\\\\\\t6. Listing bounds: offset/limit vs event-order range — pick from CLI ergonomics; both bounded.\\\\\\\\n193\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n194\\\\\\\\t## Story Breakdown\\\\\\\\n195\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n196\\\\\\\\tNo Story 0, decided not defaulted: Story-0s exist to land shared substrate that multiple stories would otherwise each half-build — migrations, new tables, config plumbing, fixture families (Epics 01–03 each had at least one of these). This epic has none. The foundation work that does exist is single-consumer and lands with its consumer: the `inspect` surface skeleton and report shapes belong to Story 2 (shapes live in `shared/` per house pattern, so Story 3 consumes without redefining), `threadView.describe` belongs to Story 3 (its only caller is `inspect.view`), and the fixture extension for mutation-in-flight states belongs to Story 2 (its first consumer). A foundation story here would own no checkable behavior of its own. Four stories.\\\\\\\\n197\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n198\\\\\\\\t### Story 1: Message Read Surface\\\\\\\\n199\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n200\\\\\\\\tCompletes Flow 3: listing options, `show` with forms join, deleted-audit option, CLI extension + parity. Depends only on built code (Epics 01–02). ACs: 3.1–3.4. TCs: 3.1–3.4. ~7 tests.\\\\\\\\n201\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n202\\\\\\\\t### Story 2: Inspect Domain — Overview and Health\\\\\\\\n203\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n204\\\\\\\\tThe `inspect` domain is born: surface skeleton, `overview` and `health` composed from owners' reports, CLI commands, fixture extension for mutation-in-flight states. Flows 1 and 4. ACs: 1.1–1.4, 4.1–4.5. TCs: 1.1–1.3, 4.1–4.3. ~9 tests.\\\\\\\\n205\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n206\\\\\\\\t### Story 3: View-Contents Report\\\\\\\\n207\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n208\\\\\\\\t`threadView.describe` (read-only surface addition) + `inspect.view` with the loadCost-equals-pull contract. Flow 2. Depends on Epic 03 Stories 0–2 landed (view storage exists). ACs: 2.1–2.5. TCs: 2.1–2.3. ~6 tests.\\\\\\\\n209\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n210\\\\\\\\t### Story 4: Lifecycle Exercise\\\\\\\\n211\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n212\\\\\\\\tThe scripted full-surface sequence with checkpoint, replay, teardown, and CLI-parity legs. Flow 5. Depends on Stories 1–3 here plus Epic 03 complete. ACs: 5.1–5.5. TCs: 5.1–5.4. ~6 tests.\\\\\\\\n213\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n214\\\\\\\\t**Sequencing**: Story 1 can start immediately (no Epic 03 dependency). Story 2 next (fixture states; health consumes built reports). Story 3 waits on Epic 03's view storage stories. Story 4 last. If Epic 03's build slips, Stories 1–2 proceed; 3–4 wait.\\\\\\\\n215\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n216\\\\\\\\t## Traceability\\\\\\\\n217\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n218\\\\\\\\t| PRD | This epic |\\\\\\\\n219\\\\\\\\t|-----|-----------|\\\\\\\\n220\\\\\\\\t| AC-4.1 (view contents and cost) | Flow 2 |\\\\\\\\n221\\\\\\\\t| AC-4.2 (messages listable/viewable) | Flow 3 |\\\\\\\\n222\\\\\\\\t| PRD AC-4.3 (search) | **Deferred post-v1** — Out of Scope; PRD and tech arch already backfilled |\\\\\\\\n223\\\\\\\\t| AC-4.4 (rebuild visibility) | Flow 4 (AC-4.4) |\\\\\\\\n224\\\\\\\\t| AC-4.5 (derivation health) | Flow 4 (AC-4.1–4.3, 4.5) |\\\\\\\\n225\\\\\\\\t| Feature 4 scope: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"thread size and composition, turn and chunk counts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | Flow 1 |\\\\\\\\n226\\\\\\\\t| M4 \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"SDK exercised end-to-end at its integration points\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | Flow 5 (mechanical half; quality half transfers to the inference epic) |\\\\\\\\n227\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n228\\\\\\\\t### AC → TC Table\\\\\\\\n229\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n230\\\\\\\\t| AC | TCs |\\\\\\\\n231\\\\\\\\t|----|-----|\\\\\\\\n232\\\\\\\\t| AC-1.1 | TC-1.1 |\\\\\\\\n233\\\\\\\\t| AC-1.2 | TC-1.2 |\\\\\\\\n234\\\\\\\\t| AC-1.3 | TC-1.1 |\\\\\\\\n235\\\\\\\\t| AC-1.4 | TC-1.3 |\\\\\\\\n236\\\\\\\\t| AC-2.1 | TC-2.1 |\\\\\\\\n237\\\\\\\\t| AC-2.2 | TC-2.2 |\\\\\\\\n238\\\\\\\\t| AC-2.3 | TC-2.2 |\\\\\\\\n239\\\\\\\\t| AC-2.4 | TC-2.3 |\\\\\\\\n240\\\\\\\\t| AC-2.5 | TC-2.1 |\\\\\\\\n241\\\\\\\\t| AC-3.1 | TC-3.1 |\\\\\\\\n242\\\\\\\\t| AC-3.2 | TC-3.2 |\\\\\\\\n243\\\\\\\\t| AC-3.3 | TC-3.3 |\\\\\\\\n244\\\\\\\\t| AC-3.4 | TC-3.4 |\\\\\\\\n245\\\\\\\\t| AC-4.1 | TC-4.1 |\\\\\\\\n246\\\\\\\\t| AC-4.2 | TC-4.2 |\\\\\\\\n247\\\\\\\\t| AC-4.3 | TC-4.2 |\\\\\\\\n248\\\\\\\\t| AC-4.4 | TC-4.3 |\\\\\\\\n249\\\\\\\\t| AC-4.5 | TC-4.1 |\\\\\\\\n250\\\\\\\\t| AC-5.1 | TC-5.1 |\\\\\\\\n251\\\\\\\\t| AC-5.2 | TC-5.1 |\\\\\\\\n252\\\\\\\\t| AC-5.3 | TC-5.2 |\\\\\\\\n253\\\\\\\\t| AC-5.4 | TC-5.3 |\\\\\\\\n254\\\\\\\\t| AC-5.5 | TC-5.4 |\\\\\\\\n255\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n256\\\\\\\\t## Completeness Self-Check\\\\\\\\n257\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n258\\\\\\\\t- [ ] Every PRD Feature 4 AC mapped or explicitly deferred (PRD AC-4.3 search deferred, named, with PRD/tech-arch deferral recorded)\\\\\\\\n259\\\\\\\\t- [ ] Every flow has ACs and TCs; every AC covered by at least one TC (23/23 per table)\\\\\\\\n260\\\\\\\\t- [ ] No TC references an undefined AC\\\\\\\\n261\\\\\\\\t- [ ] Read-only contract stated per flow and as NFR\\\\\\\\n262\\\\\\\\t- [ ] No new storage, no migration — stated as assumption\\\\\\\\n263\\\\\\\\t- [ ] Epic 03 in-build dependency named with deviation-table check before tech design freeze\\\\\\\\n264\\\\\\\\t- [ ] Lifecycle exercise scoped as seam proof, not integration gate; M3/M4 quality-half transfer recorded\\\\\\\\n265\\\\\\\\t- [ ] No Epic 04 operation invokes inference; provider use only in Epic 02 test setup wording carried\\\\\\\\n266\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\"}]},\\\\\\\"parent_tool_use_id\\\\\\\":null,\\\\\\\"session_id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"77af4b8d-fd36-430f-8eea-47bd404bad4c\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"uuid\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"edc584b8-9c27-46b5-a766-9ebfb7408b74\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"timestamp\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"2026-06-12T16:04:02.827Z\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"tool_use_result\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"file\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"filePath\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/04-inspection/epic.md\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"content\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"# Epic 04: Inspection\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**Status:** Validated — ready for story publishing and tech design\\\\\\\\n**PRD:** `../00-prd.md` Feature 4\\\\\\\\n**Tech Arch:** `../01-tech-arch.md`\\\\\\\\n**Depends on:** Epic 01 (record and read-back), Epic 02 (report/requeue surfaces, mutation cascade), Epic 03 (view storage, status, pull — in build; consumed as spec'd)\\\\\\\\n**Counts:** 23 ACs / 17 TCs across 5 flows\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Onboarding Context\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nRead first: `../../01-onboard/02-domain-design.md` §Inspect (the domain's charter: read-only, reads through other domains' surfaces, never writes, repairs, or derives), §Messages (read ownership), §Thread view (the view state this epic reports on). The Epic 02 spec pack defines the report surfaces this epic composes (`messages.report`, `turns.report`, queue detail). The Epic 03 spec pack defines the view storage and status shapes the view-contents report reads.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThis is the last epic of the v1 PRD. It adds no tables, no migration, no derivations, and no provider use. It completes the read side of the product and closes with a full-surface lifecycle exercise — the first time every epic's surface runs in one sequence.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## User Profile\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**Primary user:** the operator (Lee, mentees later) auditing threads from the CLI; secondarily, agents inside a harness using the same reads mid-task, and — for Flow 5 only — the future PI extension, whose call order the lifecycle exercise rehearses.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**Context:** the operator arrives with a question — what does this thread hold, what did the agent actually see, did the rebuild land, why is this view degraded — and acts on the answer with Feature 2's mutation and requeue operations. Inspect tells them where to point those.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**Mental model:** \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"inspect describes; it never changes. Every report reads the same state the serving surfaces read, so a report never disagrees with what the agent was actually served. When a report names a subject, I can drill to it — view entry → chunk → turn → message → blocks.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**Key constraint:** read-only, structurally — no inspect operation writes rows, transitions state, or moves the boundary; and no operation in this epic invokes a provider. Reports compose the owning domains' surfaces; inspect owns no rows and reads no other domain's tables directly.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Feature Overview\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nInspection answers questions about a thread without changing it. Four reads: a thread overview (size, composition, derivation state at a glance), a view-contents report (what the active view holds, entry by entry, and what loading it costs), message listing and viewing (the drill-down target every other report points into), and a derivation health report (counts by state, failures with reasons, what repair would touch). Plus the lifecycle exercise: the whole SDK surface driven end-to-end in PI-extension call order.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nEverything here reads through the owning domains' surfaces. Inspect owns no rows. The one surface addition outside the inspect domain: `thread-view` gains a read-only `describe` operation exposing the stored view row (arrangement, gaps, config, provenance), because inspect may not read `thread_view` tables directly and no existing operation exposes them.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### Flow Summary\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n| Flow | Name | Surface |\\\\\\\\n|------|------|---------|\\\\\\\\n| 1 | Thread overview | `inspect.overview` |\\\\\\\\n| 2 | View contents and load cost | `inspect.view` (+ `threadView.describe`) |\\\\\\\\n| 3 | Message listing and viewing | `messages.list` / `messages.show` |\\\\\\\\n| 4 | Derivation health and rebuild visibility | `inspect.health` |\\\\\\\\n| 5 | Full-surface lifecycle exercise | test-only; no new surface |\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Scope\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### In Scope\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- `inspect` domain surface: `overview`, `view`, `health` — read-only composition over owning domains' reports\\\\\\\\n- `threadView.describe`: read-only exposure of the stored active view (arrangement entries, gaps, config, source-state provenance)\\\\\\\\n- Message read completion on the `messages` surface: listing options (bounds, include-deleted) and single-message view with blocks and derivation form states\\\\\\\\n- CLI: `lhc inspect overview|view|health`, `lhc messages list` (extended), `lhc messages show` — JSON, SDK-parity, process-suite legs\\\\\\\\n- The lifecycle exercise: create → intake → drain → status → compact → pull → inspect → mutate → rebuild → compact → pull → materialize, through the real SDK, deterministic provider, with replay-equality and instance-teardown legs\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### Out of Scope\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- **Message search — deferred post-v1.** No search ships in v1. The shape (substring vs FTS, ranking, result granularity) gets decided from real usage after LHC and the PI extension are integrated. The PRD and tech arch carry this deferral (Feature 4 scope, Future Directions, tech-arch deferred list) — backfilled with this epic, so tech design inherits no contradiction.\\\\\\\\n- Mutations and repair execution (Feature 2 owns; inspect reports on them and previews repair, never executes)\\\\\\\\n- Any new derivation, any provider call, any real inference (next PRD cycle owns the inference feature)\\\\\\\\n- The PI extension itself (next PRD)\\\\\\\\n- Watch/follow modes, streaming output, UI rendering — CLI is JSON one-shots\\\\\\\\n- Cross-thread reports (single thread scope, same as every epic)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### Assumptions\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- Epic 03 is in build. This epic consumes its surfaces as spec'd (`status`, `pull`, view storage shapes). **Handoff gate:** before this epic's tech design freezes, the tech design must check Epic 02's and Epic 03's final deviation tables and update every consumed contract this epic references; the tech design records that check's outcome in its Spec Validation table.\\\\\\\\n- Epic 03's Story 0 fixture (deterministic provider, ~12 turns, 4 chunks, tool-heavy middle, manufactured failure states) is reusable here and extends with mutation-in-flight states; no new fixture family.\\\\\\\\n- Schema is terminal at v6 for this PRD: this epic ships **no migration**. If tech design finds a needed index, that is a v7 migration decision to surface, not assume.\\\\\\\\n- The deleted-message contract is Epic 02's, inherited: deleted messages drop from reads, source events retain the originals. This epic adds the audit option to see them, not a new policy.\\\\\\\\n- M3/M4 milestone gates close mechanically only: view *quality* cannot be reviewed while bands render deterministic-provider output. The quality halves of those gates transfer to the inference feature's epic. Closeout records this explicitly.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Flow 1: Thread Overview\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nOne call answers \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"what is this thread\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": identity, record size, composition, derivation state, view state, visibility state. The numbers an operator scans before deciding to compact, repair, or drill in.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n#### Acceptance Criteria\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- **AC-1.1**: `inspect.overview` returns, in one read-only call: thread identity and metadata; event count and order span; message counts (total visible, by kind, deleted counted separately) with visible token sum; turn counts (open, closed); chunk count and closed-but-unchunked turn count; derivation counts by state (pending, retrying, failed, blocked, ready); active-view summary (viewId, createdAt, compactPoint, coveredFrom) or null when never compacted; visibility boundary position and current zone token sum.\\\\\\\\n- **AC-1.2**: Counts honor the deleted contract: deleted messages appear only in the deleted count — excluded from visible counts, kind breakdowns, and token sums; event counts are unaffected (the record retains everything).\\\\\\\\n- **AC-1.3**: Every thread shape reports cleanly: fresh-empty, mid-first-turn, never-compacted, compacted, mid-rebuild. Absent pieces report as zeros or null — never omitted fields, never shape errors.\\\\\\\\n- **AC-1.4**: The overview is a pure read: no work items created, no state changed, repeated calls with no intervening writes return identical results.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n#### Test Conditions\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- **TC-1.1** (AC-1.1, AC-1.3): Overview shape variants: fresh-empty, mid-first-turn, never-compacted-with-record, compacted, and mid-rebuild all return the full shape with absent pieces as zeros/nulls. The compacted tool-heavy fixture asserts exact expected counts (messages by kind, turns, chunks, derivation states, view summary, boundary).\\\\\\\\n- **TC-1.2** (AC-1.2): Delete one message; overview → visible count and token sum drop, deleted count = 1, kind breakdown excludes it, event count unchanged.\\\\\\\\n- **TC-1.3** (AC-1.4): Overview twice with no writes between → deep-equal results; no `work_item` rows created; zero provider calls.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Flow 2: View Contents and Load Cost\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nWhat the agent actually sees, and what it costs. The report reads the stored active view (via `threadView.describe`) and the live tail, entry by entry, with the cost a pull would incur right now.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n#### Acceptance Criteria\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- **AC-2.1**: The report names the active view — viewId, createdAt, profile name and resolved config, compactPoint, coveredFrom — and lists every band entry in served order (brief → detailed → smooth): subject kind and id, form used, degraded flag; plus every gap with its reason; plus per-band stored token counts. All of it from the stored snapshot, not recomputed.\\\\\\\\n- **AC-2.2**: The tail section reports message count and token cost *as currently served*: tool results at-or-behind the visibility boundary are costed at their short form, everything else full.\\\\\\\\n- **AC-2.3**: `loadCost` totals what `pull` serves now — bands plus tail — and a test asserts equality against an actual pull's measured content. The report never disagrees with the surface it describes.\\\\\\\\n- **AC-2.4**: A never-compacted thread reports view: null with a tail-only loadCost under the same equality contract (the whole record served as tail).\\\\\\\\n- **AC-2.5**: The report carries the view's recorded source-state provenance (what the compact saw: max event order, form counts) and is a pure read under AC-1.4's contract.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n#### Test Conditions\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- **TC-2.1** (AC-2.1, AC-2.5): On a compacted fixture with one degraded entry and one gap → report matches the stored arrangement exactly: subjects, forms used, degraded flags, gap reasons, band token counts, config, provenance.\\\\\\\\n- **TC-2.2** (AC-2.2, AC-2.3): On a boundary-advanced fixture → tail cost counts short forms short; `loadCost.total` equals the token measure of an actual `pull`'s messages using the same estimator.\\\\\\\\n- **TC-2.3** (AC-2.4): Never-compacted thread → view null, tail spans the record, cost-parity assertion holds.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Flow 3: Message Listing and Viewing\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe drill-down floor. Every report names subjects; this flow is where an operator or agent reads the actual record. Epic 01 shipped `listMessages` bare; this flow completes it and adds the single-message view.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n#### Acceptance Criteria\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- **AC-3.1**: Listing returns messages in record order with kind, block summary, token estimate, turn membership, and deleted status, with bounded-listing options (range/limit) so large threads list without loading everything.\\\\\\\\n- **AC-3.2**: `messages.show` returns one message in full: every block with complete content (the record — full tool results, not view-shortened forms), token estimate, turn membership, and the message's derivation forms with their states and metadata (joined from the owner's report, including tool-outcome metadata where present).\\\\\\\\n- **AC-3.3**: Deleted messages are excluded by default and listable with an explicit include-deleted option, marked deleted — never silently mixed in. `show` on a deleted message returns the record marked deleted (audit is the point), never a not-found.\\\\\\\\n- **AC-3.4**: CLI parity: `lhc messages list` and `lhc messages show` mirror the SDK operations — same options, same result JSON.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n#### Test Conditions\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- **TC-3.1** (AC-3.1): List on the fixture → record order, kinds, token estimates, turn ids correct; range and limit options honored exactly.\\\\\\\\n- **TC-3.2** (AC-3.2): Show on a drained tool-result message → full original content present, forms listed with states, outcome metadata present.\\\\\\\\n- **TC-3.3** (AC-3.3): Delete one message → default list excludes it; include-deleted lists it marked; show on its id → record with deleted flag.\\\\\\\\n- **TC-3.4** (AC-3.4): Spawned-CLI list and show on a fixture thread → JSON equals the in-process SDK results (process suite).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Flow 4: Derivation Health and Rebuild Visibility\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe state of every derived form, aggregated to act on. This is also where a mutation's rebuild is watched: cleared forms show pending with queued work, then land ready.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n#### Acceptance Criteria\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- **AC-4.1**: `inspect.health` aggregates across owners — counts by owner, form kind, and state (ready, pending, retrying, failed, blocked) — assembled entirely from the owners' report surfaces, never from direct `derived_form` or `work_item` reads.\\\\\\\\n- **AC-4.2**: Failures carry actionable detail: subject id, form kind, reason, attempts, last error — enough to decide and target a requeue without raw SQL.\\\\\\\\n- **AC-4.3**: The report previews repair: which forms a requeue pass would touch (failed and not blocked), reported and never executed.\\\\\\\\n- **AC-4.4**: Rebuild visibility: after an edit or delete, health shows the cascade-cleared forms as pending with their queued work visible; after the queue drains, the same forms report ready. Two reads bracket a rebuild.\\\\\\\\n- **AC-4.5**: Live queue visibility: `health.queue` reports queued and claimed counts **per form-report entry**, tallied from each entry's live queue-item join in the owners' reports — every pending/retrying form rides one live queue item, so `queued + claimed` equals `pending + retrying` in the same report by construction. These are per-entry counts, not raw `listQueuedWork` work-item counts: one `turn_derivation` work item can back multiple form entries, so a raw work-item count would break that identity.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n#### Test Conditions\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- **TC-4.1** (AC-4.1, AC-4.5): Fixture with manufactured mixed states (ready, failed-transient, failed-permanent, blocked, pending) → exact counts per owner, kind, and state; queue section consistent with pending counts.\\\\\\\\n- **TC-4.2** (AC-4.2, AC-4.3): Failed and blocked forms present → failure detail exact (subject, form, reason, attempts); repair preview lists exactly the failed-not-blocked set.\\\\\\\\n- **TC-4.3** (AC-4.4): Edit a mid-thread message → health shows the cascade's cleared set pending with queued work (exact subjects per the cascade contract); `drainSettled` → same set ready; nothing outside the cascade changed state.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Flow 5: Full-Surface Lifecycle Exercise\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nNot a capstone — nothing here proves integration readiness, because no real inference exists yet (that gate belongs to the inference epic). This is the cross-epic seam proof: the first sequence that drives every surface the PI extension will call, in its call order, against one SDK instance. What it catches: contract drift between the three built epics' surfaces, lifecycle ordering bugs, state leaks between operations.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe sequence (PI-extension call order): create thread → intake multi-turn tool-heavy batches → background drain settles → status → compact (profile) → pull → inspect (overview, view, health) → edit + delete → rebuild drains → health confirms → second compact → pull → materialize.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n#### Acceptance Criteria\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- **AC-5.1**: The full sequence completes through one real SDK configuration with the deterministic provider: every operation returns ok, zero network, zero real-provider calls.\\\\\\\\n- **AC-5.2**: Checkpoint coherence: post-compact pull serves bands + tail; post-mutation health shows the cleared set pending; post-drain health shows it ready; the second compact's view reflects post-edit content; the second compact receipt's sweep section agrees with the health report taken immediately before it.\\\\\\\\n- **AC-5.3**: End-to-end determinism: the whole sequence replayed on a fresh thread produces literally byte-identical pull outputs (they carry no thread id, so equality is asserted with no normalization), and a materialized file byte-identical after normalizing only the intentionally random thread id — the two runs' thread ids are asserted to differ and every other byte must be exact (Story 4 ruling-011).\\\\\\\\n- **AC-5.4**: No in-memory dependency: tearing down the SDK instance between phases and continuing on a fresh `createSdk` yields the same end state as the uninterrupted run.\\\\\\\\n- **AC-5.5**: Operator parity: inspect and view reads driven through the spawned CLI at checkpoints return the same JSON as the in-process SDK calls at those checkpoints.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n#### Test Conditions\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- **TC-5.1** (AC-5.1, AC-5.2): Scripted lifecycle with checkpoint assertions at each named step; receipt-vs-health cross-check exact.\\\\\\\\n- **TC-5.2** (AC-5.3): Replay on a fresh thread → literal hash equality on every pull output; materialized-file hash equality after substituting only the random thread id, asserting the two thread ids differ and every other byte is exact.\\\\\\\\n- **TC-5.3** (AC-5.4): Teardown and recreate the SDK between intake/compact/mutation phases → final pull and health identical to TC-5.1's, and the materialized file hash-equal after substituting only the random thread id (every other byte exact).\\\\\\\\n- **TC-5.4** (AC-5.5): Process-suite leg: spawned CLI inspect/view/messages reads at three checkpoints equal the in-process results.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Data Contracts\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nContract-level shapes; field mechanics are tech design's. All operations `Promise<OpResult<...>>` taking `ThreadRef`; all read-only.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**`InspectOverview`**: thread (id, createdAt, metadata); events (count, span); messages (visible count, byKind, deletedCount, visibleTokens); turns (open, closed); chunks (count, unchunkedTurns); derivation (counts by state); view (summary | null); visibility (boundaryPosition, zoneTokens).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**`ViewContentsReport`**: meta (viewId, createdAt, profile, config, compactPoint, coveredFrom) | null; bands — ordered entries (subjectKind, subjectId, formUsed, degraded) and stored tokens per band; gaps (band, subjectId, reason); tail (messageCount, tokens as served); loadCost (bandTokens, tailTokens, total — equalsor unresolvable `--session` id fails with an actionable message; it never silently creates a new thread.\\n344\\t\\n345\\tFork is the one case where a thread is created mid-run rather than chosen at launch. Fork detection does not depend on a `session_start` fork reason — recon found print-mode `--fork` reports `reason=startup`. The fork point comes from the `session_before_fork` hook (`entryId`, `position`) when present; in Epic 1, PI's session tree (the `parentId` chain) is also available as fork evidence, because PI still runs its own session here. The seeded thread is built by replay (Flow 3) and the source thread is never written. The long-term home for fork lineage is LHC thread metadata (Feature 2+, once PI's session file goes vestigial); this epic does not depend on PI's session tree as the permanent source.\\n346\\t\\n347\\t### Model-call function (host-supplied)\\n348\\t\\n349\\tThe contract LHC depends on (`packages/lhc/src/inference/types.ts`, Epic 05):\\n350\\t\\n351\\t```\\n352\\tModelCall = (input) => Promise<ModelCallResult>\\n353\\t\\n354\\tinput:  { provider: string, model: string,\\n355\\t          messages: { role: \\\"system\\\" | \\\"user\\\", content: string }[] }\\n356\\t\\n357\\tresult: { ok: true,  text: string }\\n358\\t      | { ok: false, kind: ModelCallFailureKind, message: string }\\n359\\t\\n360\\tModelCallFailureKind =\\n361\\t  \\\"auth\\\" | \\\"invalid_request\\\"          (terminal)\\n362\\t  | \\\"rate_limit\\\" | \\\"timeout\\\" | \\\"network\\\"  (retryable)\\n363\\t  | \\\"empty_output\\\"                    (adapter-generated; host never returns it)\\n364\\t  | \\\"other\\\"                           (thrown exceptions classify here)\\n365\\t```\\n366\\t\\n367\\t`provider` and `model` are opaque routing keys; only the host's function interprets them. Single-turn only — no tools, no streaming, no multi-turn state.\\n368\\t\\n369\\t### Model assignments\\n370\\t\\n371\\t```\\n372\\tModelAssignment = { provider: string, model: string, prompt: string }\\n373\\tassignments: Record<FormKind, ModelAssignment>   // all seven, validated complete at init\\n374\\t```\\n375\\t\\n376\\t`FormKind` is the seven derivation kinds: `smoothed_prompt`, `tool_call_summary`, `tool_result_summary`, `turn_rendering`, `lower_band_projection`, `chunk_summary_detailed`, `chunk_summary_brief`. `prompt` must name a registered prompt. Initialization fails loudly if any kind is missing, references an unknown prompt, or carries a placeholder.\\n377\\t\\n378\\t### Error shapes\\n379\\t\\n380\\tCapture operations return LHC's `OpResult` (typed ok/error with a code), never throwing into the PI hook. When the thread is writable, a capture failure is recorded against the thread and surfaced through health (Flow 2, Flow 6). When the store is unavailable, the extension records a diagnostic/health signal outside the thread because no durable gap can be written. Neither path propagates an exception that would break the PI session.\\n381\\t\\n382\\t---\\n383\\t\\n384\\t## Non-Functional Requirements\\n385\\t\\n386\\t- **Observe-only.** Nothing the model sees changes in this epic. PI's native context handling runs untouched; the extension registers no `context` hook behavior here.\\n387\\t- **No perceptible latency.** Capture work does not block the user's interactive turn. Derivations run in LHC's background scheduler mode.\\n388\\t- **Crash safety.** A hard kill leaves a dangling open turn; the next run that resolves the same thread tolerates it without loss or duplication. There is no mapping to lose in an early-crash window — the thread is resolved from the launch choice, not recovered from a session file.\\n389\\t- **No stale context held.** The extension holds only plain data across hooks. It never caches a PI context or session-manager object beyond the handler that received it.\\n390\\t- **Headless parity.** Every user-facing behavior in this epic (startup-validation reporting) works in PI's `rpc`, `json`, and `print` modes, guarded on UI availability — never assuming a TUI. (Headless conformance as a delivered guarantee is Epic 4; the constraint is honored here so nothing built now blocks it.)\\n391\\t- **Fail loud on misconfiguration.** Missing, unknown, or placeholder assignments fail at initialization with an actionable error, not a silent skip.\\n392\\t\\n393\\t---\\n394\\t\\n395\\t## Tech Design Questions\\n396\\t\\n397\\t1. **Fork derived-form reuse** — whether provenance identity (deterministic IDs) can be proven safe enough to copy derived forms across a fork, or whether forks always requeue. Informed by LHC's deterministic-ID property.\\n398\\t2. **`thinkingSignature` handling** — assistant thinking carries an opaque encrypted reasoning token; store it on the event or drop it. An LHC call.\\n399\\t3. **Converter structure** — the converter is the productionized M0 prototype; how it consumes `message_end` streams and emits batched `MessageEventInput[]` per the `intakeStream.messageEvents` contract.\\n400\\t4. **Title derivation** — what populates a thread's registry `title` for the picker (first user prompt, a derived summary, or operator-set), and how a mid-session rename updates it.\\n401\\t\\n402\\t> Thread mapping is **not** a tech design question — the thread is resolved at launch through the registry (Flow 1), with no stored PI-session→thread mapping, so the former \\\"mapping persistence shape\\\" and \\\"resolution by external key\\\" questions are dissolved, not deferred. (PI keeps its own session file in this observe-only epic; it is just not where thread identity lives.)\\n403\\t\\n404\\t> Image / file-reference handling is **not** a tech design question — it is an M0 decision (A-7), settled before this epic's tech design per the PRD's M0 gate.\\n405\\t\\n406\\t---\\n407\\t\\n408\\t## Story Breakdown\\n409\\t\\n410\\tPartitioning follows the build path: each story lands testable behavior on the structure the previous one established. Capture comes first; verification proves it; fork and inference build on it. The flow-per-story cut was adjusted in two places: verification (Flow 6) is sequenced right after capture because it is how capture is proven, and fork (Flow 3) is its own story because its replay-seeding machinery is distinct from the reattach logic of Flow 1.\\n411\\t\\n412\\t### Story 0: Extension Foundation\\n413\\t\\n414\\t**Delivers:** The walking skeleton: extension package scaffold, the PI extension entry point and hook registration rail, the plain-data-only state holder, the LHC instance init/dispose seam (stubbed), test fixture and corpus-loading harness, and typed fail-closed stubs.\\n415\\t**Governing idea:** Every later story adds behavior to a structure that already loads in PI, holds no stale context, and fails closed with typed errors.\\n416\\t**Prerequisite:** None.\\n417\\t**Boundary / risk notes:** Stubs fail closed, never fake success (tech arch convention). The plain-data-only rule (AC-1.3) is established here as the state-holder shape so no later story is tempted to cache a context object.\\n418\\t**Flows/ACs covered:** None directly; smoke tests only — extension loads, registers hooks, init/dispose seam runs, fixture loader produces valid intake-event shapes.\\n419\\t**Estimated test count:** 4–6 smoke tests\\n420\\t\\n421\\t### Story 1: Session Lifecycle and Thread Resolution\\n422\\t\\n423\\t**Delivers:** `initLhc`/dispose per session, launch-driven thread resolution (new / `--session` partial-id / `--continue` / `--resume` cwd picker) through the registry, reload reconstruction from the resolved thread id, plain-data-only state.\\n424\\t**Governing idea:** The right thread is resolved from the operator's launch choice and found again across reload and restart, with no two-record mapping to drift.\\n425\\t**Prerequisite:** Story 0.\\n426\\t**Boundary / risk notes:** Resolution is launch-input-driven, so there is no early-crash mapping window. The cwd column, partial-id resolve, and cwd-filtered list are the small LHC additions (A-8); the picker UI is thin pi-lhc. An unresolvable `--session` id must fail loudly, never silently create a new thread (AC-1.6).\\n427\\t**Flows/ACs covered:** Flow 1 complete — AC-1.1 through AC-1.7.\\n428\\t**Estimated test count:** 11–14\\n429\\t\\n430\\t### Story 2: Event Capture and Turn Derivation\\n431\\t\\n432\\t**Delivers:** The converter — PI `message_end` → LHC intake events with content-part fan-out, turn derivation (PI per-step turns collapsed to one LHC turn), parallel-tool correlation, error-result and aborted-turn capture, idempotency-keyed dedup, capture-failure isolation.\\n433\\t**Governing idea:** Every PI message lands as ordered LHC events exactly once, and LHC turn boundaries are derived correctly from PI traffic.\\n434\\t**Prerequisite:** Story 1.\\n435\\t**Boundary / risk notes:** The turn-derivation contract (AC-2.2) is the highest-risk item — building it wrong was the single failure recon prevented. Capture failure must isolate to a recorded gap, never an exception into the hook (AC-2.7). Runtime-change capture (AC-2.8) is in-stream-only — if not grabbed when it fires, it is lost.\\n436\\t**Flows/ACs covered:** Flow 2 complete — AC-2.1 through AC-2.8.\\n437\\t**Estimated test count:** 15–18\\n438\\t\\n439\\t### Story 3: Capture Verification\\n440\\t\\n441\\t**Delivers:** Corpus replay through the converter, read-back matching against fixtures, deterministic re-replay, and inspect overview/health reflecting the captured session.\\n442\\t**Governing idea:** Capture correctness is provable from recorded corpora without serving anything to a model.\\n443\\t**Prerequisite:** Story 2. Consumes M0 corpora as fixtures.\\n444\\t**Boundary / risk notes:** This story's fixtures are the M0 corpora (A-2). If corpora are not yet broad, the story builds the replay-equality and inspect machinery against whatever corpora exist and widens as M0 delivers.\\n445\\t**Flows/ACs covered:** Flow 6 complete — AC-6.1 through AC-6.3.\\n446\\t**Estimated test count:** 8–10\\n447\\t\\n448\\t### Story 4: Fork as New Thread\\n449\\t\\n450\\t**Delivers:** New-thread-per-fork seeded by replay to the fork point, source-thread immutability, derived-form reuse-or-requeue by provenance.\\n451\\t**Governing idea:** A fork is a new thread that mechanically reproduces the source up to the fork point and never writes the source.\\n452\\t**Prerequisite:** Story 2 (replay needs capture), Story 1 (new thread + mapping).\\n453\\t**Boundary / risk notes:** Derived-form reuse (AC-3.3) is the one optional optimization — correct behavior must hold whether reuse is taken or the derivations requeue. Reuse safety is a tech design question (#2).\\n454\\t**Flows/ACs covered:** Flow 3 complete — AC-3.1 through AC-3.3.\\n455\\t**Estimated test count:** 6–8\\n456\\t\\n457\\t### Story 5: Inference Host Routing\\n458\\t\\n459\\t**Delivers:** The injected model-call function over PI's model registry and auth, multi-lane routing, provider-error classification into LHC's failure kinds, and the end-to-end proof that a captured thread's queued derivation work runs through the function and records its outcome.\\n460\\t**Governing idea:** LHC's derivations reach the user's existing PI logins through one function that treats provider/model as opaque keys, and the capture→derivation→recorded-outcome loop closes.\\n461\\t**Prerequisite:** Story 1 (instance exists) for the function and classification (AC-4.1–AC-4.4); Story 2 (captured content to derive from) for the closed-loop proof (AC-4.5).\\n462\\t**Boundary / risk notes:** This is the host side of the LHC v1 inference seam (A-5) — the function shape must match `ModelCall` exactly so LHC's adapter consumes it unchanged. AC-4.5 is the PRD M1 \\\"confirm derivations land\\\" gate; it proves the loop, not just the function.\\n463\\t**Flows/ACs covered:** Flow 4 complete — AC-4.1 through AC-4.5.\\n464\\t**Estimated test count:** 10–12\\n465\\t\\n466\\t### Story 6: Startup Validation and Assignment Config\\n467\\t\\n468\\t**Delivers:** Seven-assignment validation against PI's registry at session start, unreachable-lane reporting (interactive and headless), config load with defaults, user override on next start, fail-loud on misconfiguration.\\n469\\t**Governing idea:** Bad lanes are visible before first use, and capture survives them.\\n470\\t**Prerequisite:** Story 5 (validation probes the routing function).\\n471\\t**Boundary / risk notes:** Validation failure must leave capture running (AC-5.3) — the two concerns are independent. Reporting must not assume a TUI (NFR headless parity).\\n472\\t**Flows/ACs covered:** Flow 5 complete — AC-5.1 through AC-5.5.\\n473\\t**Estimated test count:** 9–11\\n474\\t\\n475\\t### Sequencing\\n476\\t\\n477\\t```mermaid\\n478\\tflowchart LR\\n479\\t  S0[\\\"S0: Foundation\\\"] --> S1[\\\"S1: Lifecycle + Mapping\\\"]\\n480\\t  S1 --> S2[\\\"S2: Capture + Turn Derivation\\\"]\\n481\\t  S2 --> S3[\\\"S3: Capture Verification\\\"]\\n482\\t  S2 --> S4[\\\"S4: Fork\\\"]\\n483\\t  S1 --> S5[\\\"S5: Inference Routing\\\"]\\n484\\t  S2 -.->|closed-loop proof| S5\\n485\\t  S5 --> S6[\\\"S6: Startup Validation + Config\\\"]\\n486\\t```\\n487\\t\\n488\\tCapture spine S0→S1→S2→S3 is strictly linear. S4 (fork) depends on capture. S5→S6 (inference) branch from S1; the function and classification (AC-4.1–AC-4.4) are independent of the capture spine, while the closed-loop proof (AC-4.5) draws on Story 2's captured content. Total estimated tests: 63–79.\\n489\\t\\n490\\t---\\n491\\t\\n492\\t## Traceability\\n493\\t\\n494\\t| AC | TCs | Story |\\n495\\t|----|-----|-------|\\n496\\t| AC-1.1 | TC-1.1 | 1 |\\n497\\t| AC-1.2 | TC-1.2 | 1 |\\n498\\t| AC-1.3 | TC-1.3 | 1 |\\n499\\t| AC-1.4 | TC-1.4 | 1 |\\n500\\t| AC-1.5 | TC-1.5 | 1 |\\n501\\t| AC-1.6 | TC-1.6 | 1 |\\n502\\t| AC-1.7 | TC-1.7 | 1 |\\n503\\t| AC-2.1 | TC-2.1 | 2 |\\n504\\t| AC-2.2 | TC-2.2, TC-2.3 | 2 |\\n505\\t| AC-2.3 | TC-2.4 | 2 |\\n506\\t| AC-2.4 | TC-2.5 | 2 |\\n507\\t| AC-2.5 | TC-2.6 | 2 |\\n508\\t| AC-2.6 | TC-2.7 | 2 |\\n509\\t| AC-2.7 | TC-2.8 | 2 |\\n510\\t| AC-2.8 | TC-2.9 | 2 |\\n511\\t| AC-3.1 | TC-3.1 | 4 |\\n512\\t| AC-3.2 | TC-3.2 | 4 |\\n513\\t| AC-3.3 | TC-3.3 | 4 |\\n514\\t| AC-4.1 | TC-4.1 | 5 |\\n515\\t| AC-4.2 | TC-4.2 | 5 |\\n516\\t| AC-4.3 | TC-4.3 | 5 |\\n517\\t| AC-4.4 | TC-4.4 | 5 |\\n518\\t| AC-4.5 | TC-4.5 | 5 |\\n519\\t| AC-5.1 | TC-5.1 | 6 |\\n520\\t| AC-5.2 | TC-5.2 | 6 |\\n521\\t| AC-5.3 | TC-5.3 | 6 |\\n522\\t| AC-5.4 | TC-5.4 | 6 |\\n523\\t| AC-5.5 | TC-5.5, TC-5.6 | 6 |\\n524\\t| AC-6.1 | TC-6.1 | 3 |\\n525\\t| AC-6.2 | TC-6.2 | 3 |\\n526\\t| AC-6.3 | TC-6.3 | 3 |\\n527\\t\\n528\\t**Test Conditions**\\n529\\t\\n530\\t- **TC-1.1** — Fresh `session_start` with no thread-selecting launch flag: one LHC instance initialized in background mode, one new registry thread created with cwd/title metadata, and no PI-session-to-thread mapping record created.\\n531\\t- **TC-1.2** — `session_start` with launch input resolving an existing registry thread: initializes against that same thread and creates no second thread.\\n532\\t- **TC-1.3** — A session replacement invalidates the prior context object; the extension, holding only plain data, continues capture using the fresh context.\\n533\\t- **TC-1.4** — `session_shutdown` disposes with flush; reattach finds the thread complete through the last pre-shutdown event.\\n534\\t- **TC-1.5** — Reload re-initializes the extension; thread reference reconstructed from the resolved thread id; capture continues on the same thread.\\n535\\t- **TC-1.6** — Launch by mode resolves correctly: no flag creates a new thread; `--session` resolves by full and by partial id; `--continue` resolves the most recent; an ambiguous/unresolvable id fails with an actionable message and creates no thread.\\n536\\t- **TC-1.7** — `--resume` lists cwd-scoped threads with title and creation time and resolves a selection; an empty cwd lists nothing rather than failing.\\n537\\t- **TC-2.1** — A user/assistant/toolResult sequence records ordered events; an assistant `[thinking, text, toolCall]` fans out in that order.\\n538\\t- **TC-2.2** — A one-prompt-two-tools-then-answer corpus (the worked example) yields exactly one LHC turn with one `turn_end` at agent-run completion; PI's per-step `turn_end`s produce none.\\n539\\t- **TC-2.3** — Session order derives from converter source-event order, not `turnIndex`; two agent runs each starting at `turnIndex 0` order correctly.\\n540\\t- **TC-2.4** — Parallel tool calls with out-of-order completion correlate each result to its call by `toolCallId`.\\n541\\t- **TC-2.5** — A tool result with `isError` is captured as a `tool_result` with the error flag and content; nothing dropped.\\n542\\t- **TC-2.6** — A graceful-interrupt corpus records the partial assistant content with the aborted disposition; the turn closes complete-but-aborted.\\n543\\t- **TC-2.7** — A re-delivered event (reload/replay) is skipped by idempotency key; a normal resume re-delivers no historical events.\\n544\\t- **TC-2.8** — A malformed/unmappable event on a writable thread records a durable, queryable gap surfaced in health; an unavailable thread store surfaces an extension diagnostic / health signal with no durable gap. Both continue the session with no exception reaching the hook.\\n545\\t- **TC-2.9** — A `model_select` and a `thinking_level_select` each record an ordered `runtime_note` event carrying the new and previous values; ordering relative to surrounding messages is preserved.\\n546\\t- **TC-3.1** — Fork creates a new thread; the source thread receives no writes — its logical read-back (events, messages, turns) is unchanged. Detection resolves the fork point from the `session_before_fork` hook, with PI's session tree as available fallback evidence in Epic 1, not from a fork reason code.\\n547\\t- **TC-3.2** — Fork replay seeds the new thread; read-back matches the source through the fork point.\\n548\\t- **TC-3.3** — Fork with safe provenance reuses derived forms; fork with unprovable provenance requeues them; both yield a correct thread.\\n549\\t- **TC-4.1** — The model-call function resolves a (provider, model) pair through PI's registry and returns completion text for a single-turn message list.\\n550\\t- **TC-4.2** — Two derivation kinds assigned to different (provider, model) pairs both route correctly in one session.\\n551\\t- **TC-4.5** — A captured thread with queued derivation work, drained through LHC's scheduler with the injected function wired, persists at least one ready derived form (function returns text) and one classified failure (function returns a failure), both queryable through inspect/health — proving the capture→derivation→recorded-outcome loop, not just the function in isolation.\\n552\\t- **TC-4.3** — Auth and invalid-request map to terminal; rate-limit, timeout, network map to retryable; a thrown exception maps to `other`.\\n553\\t- **TC-4.4** — A resolved call with no output is classified `empty_output` by the adapter; the host function returns text-or-transport-failure only.\\n554\\t- **TC-5.1** — All seven assignments validate against the registry at session start before first use.\\n555\\t- **TC-5.2** — An unreachable lane reports kind + (provider, model) + fix; the report appears in a headless mode, not only the TUI.\\n556\\t- **TC-5.3** — A validation failure leaves capture running; the affected lane's derivations fail classified and queryable.\\n557\\t- **TC-5.4** — Each of the seven kinds loads a (provider, model, prompt) from config with shipped defaults; the prompt resolves to a registered prompt.\\n558\\t- **TC-5.5** — A user override of a kind's provider/model/prompt takes effect on the next session start with no code change.\\n559\\t- **TC-5.6** — An incomplete or unknown assignment fails loudly at initialization with an actionable error; no placeholder default masks it.\\n560\\t- **TC-6.1** — Each corpus (chatty, tool-heavy, parallel-tool, error-result, aborted-turn) replays to a thread whose read-back matches the fixture.\\n561\\t- **TC-6.2** — The same corpus replayed twice yields identical read-back (IDs and order stable).\\n562\\t- **TC-6.3** — inspect overview/health report event/message counts, last event position, and any gaps or failed derivations.\\n563\\t\\n564\\t---\\n565\\t\\n566\\t## Validation Checklist\\n567\\t\\n568\\t- [x] User Profile uses the four-field shape (Primary User, Context, Mental Model, Key Constraint)\\n569\\t- [x] Every flow has numbered ACs and TCs mapping to them\\n570\\t- [x] Every AC appears in the traceability table with at least one TC and one story\\n571\\t- [x] Scope states in, out, and where out-of-scope items are handled\\n572\\t- [x] Migration (POC threads) stated explicitly as out of scope\\n573\\t- [x] Assumptions carry status and impact-if-wrong, including the M0 dependency\\n574\\t- [x] Data contracts cover every operation the flows exercise, including the turn-derivation worked example, abort/crash shapes, and error shapes\\n575\\t- [x] The load-bearing turn-derivation contract (PI turn ≠ LHC turn) is stated with a worked example\\n576\\t- [x] No implementation detail below the extension/SDK boundary (no storage layout, no module internals, no library names beyond the named boundary contracts)\\n577\\t- [x] Behavior described as what the system does, not why it matters\\n578\\t- [x] Story breakdown sequenced with prerequisites, governing ideas, and test estimates\\n579\\t- [ ] Consumer test: a tech designer can design from this without asking foundational questions — pending review\\n580\\t\"}]},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"3bd99b80-3187-4d52-9c3e-b6b2c0cfc11e\",\"uuid\":\"c4afe03a-4fae-4631-b84a-8ae81c2ed598\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-06-14T04:03:38.343Z\",\"tool_use_result\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"file\":{\"filePath\":\"/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md\",\"content\":\"# Epic 1: Connector Core\\n\\n**Status:** Draft for review. **Ready for tech design once M0 lands.** Per the PRD's M0 gate, this epic's capture-verification tests consume the M0 corpus fixtures (Story 3) and two M0 decisions must be settled before tech design: the image/file-reference handling choice and the tool-call rendering choice (A5). These are named as M0 inputs below, not left as open tech design questions.\\n**PRD:** `../00-prd.md` — Feature 1\\n**Tech Arch:** `../01-tech-arch.md`\\n**Wiring research:** `../notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md` (verified against PI v0.79.2)\\n**Domain model:** `../../onboard/01-core-concepts.md`, `../../onboard/02-domain-design.md`\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Onboarding Context\\n\\n**PI** is the coding agent this extension loads into. PI runs a **session** (its unit of work — one running conversation with a model). PI exposes an **extension API**: an extension registers handlers for **hooks** (lifecycle events PI fires — session start, message finalized, turn ended, shutdown) and the **context hook** (PI asks the extension what message array to send the model). An extension runs in-process inside the PI process.\\n\\n**LHC** (Long Horizon Context) is the SDK this extension drives. It is consumed one way: a host process initializes one **LHC instance** in-process (`initLhc`) and operates it directly. LHC owns all context behavior and persists to one SQLite file per **thread** (LHC's durable record of a conversation). A **registry** (one SQLite catalog, default `~/.lhc/registry.sqlite`) lists every thread by id, title, cwd, and creation time.\\n\\n**The LHC thread is the system of record for the conversation, and the registry is the catalog of threads.** What an earlier draft got wrong was treating PI's session file as the place a thread mapping had to be stored and recovered; it does not, because a thread's identity arrives at launch — the operator selects it — so there is no mapping to recover and no pointer to keep in sync. What is true *in this epic* is narrower than \\\"PI has no session file\\\": this epic is observe-only, so **PI still runs its own session normally** — it persists its session, serves its own context to the model, and tracks its own fork lineage, exactly as vanilla PI does. LHC records alongside that as the durable conversation record. PI's session file becomes vestigial only in Feature 2, when LHC's served context replaces PI's transcript; until then the two coexist, and nothing the model sees changes.\\n\\nInside LHC:\\n- An **intake event** is the unit of capture. New traffic enters as ordered intake events; LHC records them durably. The seven event kinds are `user_prompt`, `assistant_text`, `assistant_thinking`, `tool_call`, `tool_result`, `runtime_note`, and `turn_end`.\\n- A **message** is projected from one or more intake events; a **turn** groups the messages of one exchange.\\n- A **derivation** produces a **derived form** from recorded content (for example, a summary of a tool result). The seven derived-form kinds are `smoothed_prompt`, `tool_call_summary`, `tool_result_summary`, `turn_rendering`, `lower_band_projection`, `chunk_summary_detailed`, `chunk_summary_brief`. Each derivation runs through one **model-call function** the host supplies.\\n- A **thread-view** is the bounded message array LHC serves to a model. Epic 2 builds and serves it; this epic does not.\\n\\n**This extension** maps PI's hook surface to LHC's operation surface. It holds no context state beyond a thread reference and a small set of told-the-user flags. Inference flows one direction: LHC asks for a model call, the extension routes it through PI's existing provider auth.\\n\\n**M0** is the pre-epic period that produces the recorded PI **corpora** (real sessions captured to fixtures) and the **converter** prototype (PI events → intake events). Live recon (June 2026) already established the PI event shapes this epic depends on; M0's remaining work is corpus breadth, the productionized converter, and derivation dial-in. This epic's capture-verification tests consume M0 corpora as fixtures.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Feature Overview\\n\\nA PI session run with the extension loaded records into an LHC thread. Every user message, assistant message, thinking block, tool call, tool result, runtime change (model and thinking-level switches), and derived turn boundary is captured as ordered, duplicate-safe intake events. The thread is resolved at launch — a new thread, a picked one, the most recent, or a named id — through the registry, and capture continues on that thread across resume, reload, restart, and fork. Background derivations run through the user's existing PI logins. PI's own context handling runs unchanged; the extension changes nothing the model sees. Capture is verified by replaying recorded corpora through the converter and matching the read-back, without serving any context.\\n\\n### Flow Summary\\n\\n| Flow | Capability | Surface (tech arch) |\\n|------|------------|---------------------|\\n| 1 | Session lifecycle and launch-driven thread resolution | Session Lifecycle |\\n| 2 | Event capture and turn derivation | Event Capture |\\n| 3 | Fork as new thread | Session Lifecycle + Event Capture |\\n| 4 | Inference host routing | Inference Host |\\n| 5 | Startup validation and assignment config | Inference Host |\\n| 6 | Capture verification | Event Capture (read-back via inspect) |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## User Profile\\n\\n**Primary User:** A developer using PI as their daily coding agent, with the `pi-lhc` extension loaded.\\n**Context:** Running normal coding sessions — chatty exchanges, tool-heavy agent runs, sessions resumed across days, the occasional fork or mid-task kill. They are not interacting with LHC directly; capture runs in the background.\\n**Mental Model:** \\\"I use PI exactly as before. In the background my whole session is recorded into a durable thread, so my context can be managed later. Nothing about how the agent behaves changes yet.\\\"\\n**Key Constraint:** This epic is observe-only. The extension must add no perceptible latency and must change nothing the model sees — PI's native context handling stays in control. A capture failure must never break the PI session: writable-thread failures become durable, queryable gaps; store-unavailable failures become visible extension diagnostics because the gap cannot be written.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Scope\\n\\n### In Scope\\n\\n- LHC instance lifecycle: initialize on session start, dispose on shutdown, reconstruct on reload — holding only plain data (thread reference, told-the-user flags) across hooks.\\n- Launch-driven thread resolution: a new thread by default, a cwd-scoped picker (`--resume`/`-r`), the most recent thread (`--continue`/`-c`), or a named thread (`--session <id>`, partial-id match) — all resolved through the registry catalog.\\n- Event capture: PI messages and turns mapped to LHC intake events (the productionized converter), preserving order and idempotency.\\n- Runtime-change capture: model-select and thinking-level-select changes captured in order as `runtime_note` events, because they are observable only in-stream.\\n- Turn derivation: LHC turn boundaries derived from PI traffic, not taken one-to-one from PI's per-agent-step `turn_end`.\\n- Fork handling: a new thread per fork, seeded by replaying the source thread to the fork point, source unchanged.\\n- The injected model-call function over PI's model registry and auth.\\n- Model-assignment config (the seven derivation kinds) and startup validation with visible, actionable reporting.\\n- Capture verification: recorded corpora replay identically through the converter; inspect surfaces reflect the captured session.\\n\\n### Out of Scope\\n\\n- Serving context to the model — Feature 2 / Epic 2. PI's native context handling runs as-is throughout this epic.\\n- Intercepting or disabling PI's own compaction — Feature 2 / Epic 2.\\n- Operator commands and agent self-inspection tools beyond what capture verification reads — Feature 3 / Epic 3.\\n- Status-bar / footer UX beyond actionable startup-validation reporting — Feature 3 / Epic 3.\\n- Packaging, the runner CLI, headless-mode conformance as a deliverable — Feature 4 / Epic 4.\\n- Web-search, service-tier, and researcher-subagent extensions — Feature 5 / Epic 5.\\n- Derivation prompt and model dial-in (quality tuning) — the paired M0 dial-in period; this epic ships default assignments and proves the routing, not the tuning.\\n\\n### Migration\\n\\nExisting POC threads are out of scope. They are on the pre-SDK storage shape; this epic starts fresh on new threads and lets old POC threads age out. No import or migration is built.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Assumptions\\n\\n| # | Assumption | Status | Impact if wrong |\\n|---|------------|--------|-----------------|\\n| A-1 | The PI event shapes captured by live recon (event order, parallel-tool fan-out, abort shapes, resume behavior) hold for the targeted PI version. | Validated against v0.79.2; assumed stable across the 0.79 line | Converter mapping is wrong. Mitigated by corpus replay (Flow 6), which catches drift as a read-back mismatch rather than a silent error. |\\n| A-2 | M0 delivers recorded PI corpora as test fixtures and a converter prototype to productionize. | Pending (M0) | The epic spec stands, but the build and the verification flow's tests wait on M0 corpora. Capture ACs are spec-complete; their fixtures are an M0 input. |\\n| A-3 | PI resume serves prior history through the context hook and re-fires no historical `message_end` events; duplication arises only on reload and crash-replay. | Validated (recon) | If resume re-fired history, dedup load would be higher. Idempotency keys (Flow 2) already cover the reload/replay paths, so the contract holds either way. |\\n| A-4 | The LHC construction export is `initLhc` (renamed from `createSdk`) by the time this epic is implemented. | Pending; mechanical rename | Low. If the rename slips, code reads `createSdk`; this spec uses `initLhc` as the agreed name. Tracked in tech arch TA4. |\\n| A-5 | The model-call function is the host side of the inference seam already specced in the LHC v1 line (Epic 05): `ModelCall`, `ModelCallResult`, `ModelCallFailureKind`, `ModelAssignment`. | Validated against current LHC types | If the seam shape changed, routing AC wording shifts. Verified present in `packages/lhc/src/inference/types.ts`. |\\n| A-6 | LHC `turn_end` with no open turn is a contractual no-op (Epic 01), tolerating PI's pre-first-prompt and dangling events. | Validated (LHC v1 spec) | If not a no-op, the converter would need to guard turn closes. Confirmed specced behavior. |\\n| A-7 | The image / file-reference handling decision (placeholder vs. extending the intake payload schema) is settled by M0 before this epic's tech design. | Pending (M0 gate) | The converter's content-part mapping (Flow 2) depends on it. Recon found `@file.png` stays literal text in print mode; the interactive paste shape is uncaptured. M0 corpora establish frequency; the decision is an M0 output, not a tech design question. |\\n| A-8 | The LHC registry gains a `cwd` column (set at thread creation), a `--resume` cwd filter on `listThreads`, partial-id match on `resolve`, and a `title` set at creation / updated on rename. | **Epic 1 implementation scope** (LHC-side); not yet in LHC code | AC-1.6 and AC-1.7 depend on these directly, so they are a build prerequisite for Story 1, not optional polish. The work is small and mechanical — the registry already carries id/title/created — but \\\"small\\\" is not \\\"non-blocking\\\": the cwd-scoped picker and partial-id resolve cannot pass without them. They are gated before Story 1's launch-mode ACs. |\\n| A-9 | Foreign PI extensions that persist state via session custom entries are not supported under pi-lhc in v1; pi-lhc threads own pi-lhc and conversation state only. | Decided (scope) | A third-party extension relying on PI session-file persistence loses its store when LHC replaces the session file. A passthrough lane for foreign custom entries is an explicit later design, named in the tech arch. No Epic 1 behavior depends on it. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Flow 1: Session Lifecycle and Launch-Driven Thread Resolution\\n\\nA PI session starts, and the first thing the extension settles is which thread this run records into. The thread's identity arrives with the launch: no flag means a new thread; `--resume`/`-r` opens a cwd-scoped picker over the registry; `--continue`/`-c` takes the most recent thread; `--session <id>` names one directly (a partial id matches a prefix). Every case resolves through the registry catalog — the operator's launch choice *is* the thread identity, so there is no mapping to recover. The extension then initializes one LHC instance in background scheduler mode against the resolved thread. Across PI's hooks it holds only plain data (the thread reference and a few flags) and retains no PI context object, because session replacement invalidates those objects.\\n\\n```mermaid\\nsequenceDiagram\\n  participant OP as operator (launch)\\n  participant E as extension\\n  participant R as registry\\n  participant L as LHC instance\\n  OP->>E: session_start (launch flags)\\n  alt no flag\\n    E->>R: newThread (cwd, title)\\n  else --resume / -r\\n    E->>R: listThreads (cwd) — picker — resolve(id)\\n  else --continue / -c\\n    E->>R: resolve most recent\\n  else --session id\\n    E->>R: resolve(id, partial match)\\n  end\\n  E->>L: initLhc (background) on resolved thread\\n  Note over E,L: hooks fire — extension holds only threadRef and flags\\n  OP->>E: session_shutdown (reason)\\n  E->>L: dispose (flush/cleanup)\\n```\\n\\n**AC-1.1:** On `session_start` with no thread-selecting launch flag, the extension creates a new LHC thread (registering it in the catalog with its cwd), initializes one LHC instance in background scheduler mode against it, and does not drive the derivation queue itself.\\n\\n**AC-1.2:** On `session_start` that resolves to an existing thread (via `--continue`, `--session`, or a picker selection), the extension initializes against that thread by resolving it through the registry. No second thread is created for an already-resolved thread.\\n\\n**AC-1.3:** Across all hooks, the extension holds only plain data (thread reference, file path, told-the-user flags) between events. It retains no PI context or session-manager object across hook boundaries; each handler uses the fresh context PI provides. A session replacement (new/resume/fork) that invalidates prior context objects does not break capture.\\n\\n**AC-1.4:** On `session_shutdown`, the extension disposes the LHC instance with flush and cleanup. A subsequent run that resolves the same thread finds it complete through the last event recorded before shutdown — no trailing loss.\\n\\n**AC-1.5:** On reload (extension torn down and re-initialized while the same session continues), the extension reconstructs the thread reference from durable state (the resolved thread id), not from retained in-memory objects, and capture continues on the same thread.\\n\\n**AC-1.6:** Launch resolves the thread by mode: no flag creates a new thread; `--session <id>` resolves a named thread by full or partial id; `--continue`/`-c` resolves the most recently created thread; an ambiguous or unresolvable id fails with an actionable message rather than silently creating a new thread.\\n\\n**AC-1.7:** `--resume`/`-r` lists threads scoped to the current working directory, each shown with its title and creation time, and resolves the operator's selection. With no threads for the cwd, the picker reports an empty list rather than failing.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Flow 2: Event Capture and Turn Derivation\\n\\nFrom the first prompt on, the extension maps each finalized PI message to LHC intake events and records them in order. PI fires a turn boundary per agent step; an LHC turn spans a whole exchange (one user prompt and everything the agent did in response). The converter derives LHC turn boundaries from PI traffic rather than copying PI's. Capture is duplicate-safe; writable-thread failures become durable gaps, store-unavailable failures become visible extension diagnostics, and neither breaks the session.\\n\\n```mermaid\\nsequenceDiagram\\n  participant PI as PI session\\n  participant E as converter\\n  participant L as LHC intake\\n  PI->>E: message_end(user)\\n  E->>L: user_prompt event (opens LHC turn)\\n  PI->>E: message_end(assistant [thinking,text,toolCall×N])\\n  E->>L: assistant_thinking, assistant_text, tool_call×N\\n  PI->>E: message_end(toolResult) ×N\\n  E->>L: tool_result×N (correlated by toolCallId)\\n  Note over PI,E: PI fires turn_end per agent step — NOT mapped 1:1\\n  PI->>E: agent run completes (no further tool calls)\\n  E->>L: turn_end event (closes the LHC turn)\\n```\\n\\n**AC-2.1:** Every PI message finalized through `message_end` — user, assistant, tool result — is mapped to LHC intake events and recorded in source order. An assistant message fans out to per-content-part events in thinking → text → tool-call order: `assistant_thinking`, `assistant_text`, and one `tool_call` per call.\\n\\n**AC-2.2:** LHC turn boundaries are derived from PI traffic, not from PI's per-agent-step `turn_end`. One LHC turn spans a user prompt and all subsequent agent activity until the next user prompt or the end of the agent run. The converter emits exactly one LHC `turn_end` event per LHC turn, at the agent run's completion — never one per PI `turn_end`, and never keyed off PI's per-agent-run `turnIndex` as a session counter.\\n\\n**AC-2.3:** Parallel tool calls are captured with correct correlation: when one assistant message carries multiple `tool_call` parts and their results complete out of arrival order, each `tool_result` event is matched to its call by `toolCallId`, not by arrival order.\\n\\n**AC-2.4:** A tool result carrying an error is captured as a `tool_result` event with its error content and an error flag set. No event is dropped because a tool failed.\\n\\n**AC-2.5:** A graceful interrupt — a complete turn PI marks aborted, with partial assistant content preserved — is captured whole: the partial content is recorded and the aborted disposition is carried through. The interrupted content is not discarded.\\n\\n**AC-2.6:** Capture is duplicate-safe. Re-delivered events (reload, crash-replay) are recognized by idempotency key and skipped rather than recorded twice. On normal resume PI re-delivers no historical events, so duplication only arises on the reload and replay paths.\\n\\n**AC-2.7:** A capture failure does not break the PI session, and does not vanish silently. When the thread is writable, a malformed or unmappable event records a durable, queryable gap that surfaces in thread health. When the thread store itself is unavailable, the failure surfaces as an extension diagnostic / health signal rather than a durable gap (the record cannot be written), and no exception propagates into the PI hook. Either way the session continues, and capture adds no perceptible latency to interactive use.\\n\\n**AC-2.8:** Runtime changes that PI fires only in-stream — model selection (`model_select`) and thinking-level selection (`thinking_level_select`) — are captured in order as `runtime_note` events carrying the change (the new model or level, and the previous one). These are recorded at the moment they fire because no durable record holds them otherwise; nothing in this epic consumes them, and their presence in the thread is what lets later epics attribute a turn to the model that produced it.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Flow 3: Fork as New Thread\\n\\nA user forks a PI session. PI creates a new session file and leaves the original untouched. A fork produces a new LHC thread, seeded by replaying the source thread's recorded events up to the fork point. The source thread is never written. Derived forms carry over when their provenance proves the reuse safe; otherwise they requeue on the new thread.\\n\\n**AC-3.1:** Forking a PI session creates a new LHC thread. The source thread receives no writes — it is unchanged by the fork.\\n\\n**AC-3.2:** The new thread is seeded by replaying the source thread's recorded events up to the fork point. The seeded thread's read-back (events, messages, turns) matches the source's read-back through that point.\\n\\n**AC-3.3:** Derived forms may be reused from the source thread when provenance identity proves the reuse safe; when safety cannot be proven, the affected derivations requeue on the new thread. The forked thread's read-back is correct under either path.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Flow 4: Inference Host Routing\\n\\nThe extension gives the LHC instance one model-call function at initialization. LHC calls it when a derivation needs a completion, passing a (provider, model) pair and messages. The function resolves the pair through PI's model registry and auth and performs a single-turn completion. LHC treats provider and model as opaque routing keys; only the host's function interprets them. Provider errors map to LHC's failure classification, which records them as classified, queryable failures. A captured thread's queued derivation work runs through this path end to end and records its outcome against the thread.\\n\\n**AC-4.1:** The extension supplies one model-call function to the LHC instance at initialization. Given a (provider, model) pair and a system/user message list, the function resolves the pair through PI's model registry and auth and returns either the completion text or a classified failure.\\n\\n**AC-4.2:** Different derivation kinds may route to different (provider, model) pairs within the same session. The function routes each call by its provided keys; LHC does not interpret the keys.\\n\\n**AC-4.3:** Provider errors map to LHC's failure classification: auth and invalid-request are terminal; rate-limit, timeout, and network are retryable. A thrown exception classifies as the generic `other` kind.\\n\\n**AC-4.4:** A call that resolves but produces no output is classified as `empty_output` by the LHC adapter. The host's function never returns `empty_output` itself — it returns text or a transport/auth failure.\\n\\n**AC-4.5:** A captured thread's background derivation work invokes the injected model-call function and records a result against the thread: a queued derivation runs through LHC's scheduler, calls the function, and persists either a ready derived form or a classified failure, queryable through inspect/health. This closes the loop end to end — capture to derivation to recorded outcome — not just the function in isolation.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Flow 5: Startup Validation and Assignment Config\\n\\nAt session start, before any derivation runs, the extension validates that all seven derivation assignments point at lanes PI can reach. Unreachable lanes are reported up front, naming the kind, the (provider, model), and the fix. A validation failure does not stop capture; the affected derivations fail, classified and queryable. Assignments come from config; a user overrides any kind's provider, model, or prompt without touching code.\\n\\n**AC-5.1:** At session start the extension validates all seven model assignments against PI's registry before first derivation use.\\n\\n**AC-5.2:** An unreachable lane — not logged in, unknown model, unknown provider — is reported before first use, naming the derivation kind, the (provider, model), and the corrective action. The report reaches the user in interactive and headless modes alike (guarded on UI availability, never assuming a TUI).\\n\\n**AC-5.3:** A validation failure leaves capture running. Derivations on the affected lanes fail, classified and queryable through health; the session is not broken.\\n\\n**AC-5.4:** Model assignments load from the extension's config. Each of the seven derivation kinds resolves to a (provider, model, prompt), where the prompt names a registered prompt. The epic ships with default assignments so derivations function; assignment quality is a dial-in concern, not a build gate.\\n\\n**AC-5.5:** A user override — a different provider, model, or prompt for any kind — takes effect on the next session start with no code change. An incomplete or unknown assignment fails loudly at initialization with an actionable error, never a silent skip or a placeholder default that masks the misconfiguration.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Flow 6: Capture Verification\\n\\nCapture is verified without serving anything to a model. A recorded PI corpus replays through the converter; the resulting thread's read-back is matched against the fixture's expectation. Replay is deterministic: the same corpus yields the same thread every time. Inspect surfaces report the captured session — event and message counts, last recorded position, and any gaps.\\n\\n**AC-6.1:** Replaying a recorded PI corpus through the converter produces a thread whose read-back — events, messages, and turns — matches the fixture's expectation, including the chatty, tool-heavy, parallel-tool, error-result, and aborted-turn corpora.\\n\\n**AC-6.2:** Replay is deterministic: the same corpus replayed twice produces identical thread read-back. The deterministic-ID property holds through the converter, so a re-replay does not perturb IDs or ordering.\\n\\n**AC-6.3:** inspect surfaces (overview, health) reflect the captured session: event and message counts, the last recorded event position, and any capture gaps or failed derivations are visible rather than hidden.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Data Contracts\\n\\n### PI events consumed\\n\\nThe extension consumes these PI hooks. Shapes verified against PI v0.79.2 (`../notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md` §1, §5a, §5b).\\n\\n| PI event | Carries | Extension use |\\n|----------|---------|---------------|\\n| `session_start` | `reason` (`startup`/`new`/`resume`/`reload`/`fork`), `previousSessionFile` | Resolve or create the thread; reattach; trigger startup validation |\\n| `message_end` | finalized `user` / `assistant` / `toolResult` message | Map to LHC intake events |\\n| `turn_end` | `turnIndex` (per-agent-run), `message`, `toolResults[]`, `stopReason` | Signal an agent step ended; **not** mapped 1:1 to LHC `turn_end` |\\n| `agent_end` | the agent run's messages | Close the LHC turn — emit one LHC `turn_end` per agent run (the boundary the per-step PI `turn_end` does not give) |\\n| `model_select` | `model`, `previousModel`, `source` | Capture as a `runtime_note` event (AC-2.8) |\\n| `thinking_level_select` | `level`, `previousLevel` | Capture as a `runtime_note` event (AC-2.8) |\\n| `session_shutdown` | `reason`, `targetSessionFile` | Dispose the instance; flush |\\n| `session_before_fork` | `entryId`, `position` | The fork point when present; otherwise PI's session tree is Epic-1 fallback evidence (permanent lineage is LHC metadata, Feature 2+) |\\n\\n> The `context` and `session_before_compact` hooks are consumed by Epic 2 (serving), not this epic.\\n\\n### LHC intake events emitted\\n\\nThe converter emits LHC `MessageEventInput` events. Each carries `eventKind`, `idempotencyKey`, `actor`, `harness`, and a kind-fixed `payload`. The seven kinds and their payloads:\\n\\n| Event kind | Payload |\\n|------------|---------|\\n| `user_prompt` | `{ text }` |\\n| `assistant_text` | `{ text }` |\\n| `assistant_thinking` | `{ text }` |\\n| `tool_call` | `{ toolCallId, toolName, arguments }` |\\n| `tool_result` | `{ toolCallId, content, isError? }` |\\n| `runtime_note` | `{ text }` |\\n| `turn_end` | `{}` (empty) |\\n\\n### PI message → LHC event mapping\\n\\n| PI message (`message_end`) | LHC events emitted (in order) |\\n|----------------------------|-------------------------------|\\n| user `[text]` | one `user_prompt` |\\n| assistant `[thinking?, text?, toolCall×N]` | `assistant_thinking` (if present), then `assistant_text` (if present), then one `tool_call` per call |\\n| toolResult `[text]`, `isError?` | one `tool_result` (correlated by `toolCallId`) |\\n| (agent run completes) | one `turn_end` — see turn derivation below |\\n\\nImage and file-reference content parts: LHC intake payloads are text today. Handling (placeholder vs. payload-schema extension) is an M0 decision (A-7), not a tech design question; until that decision lands, the converter must not silently drop a content part without recording the omission.\\n\\nRuntime-change events: PI's `model_select` and `thinking_level_select` map to `runtime_note` events (AC-2.8). The note text is structured enough to recover the change — the new value and the previous one — but it rides the existing `runtime_note` kind, adding no new intake kind.\\n\\n| PI event | LHC event |\\n|----------|-----------|\\n| `model_select` `{model, previousModel}` | one `runtime_note` recording the model change |\\n| `thinking_level_select` `{level, previousLevel}` | one `runtime_note` recording the level change |\\n\\n### Turn derivation\\n\\nPI fires `turn_start`/`turn_end` per agent step, and resets `turnIndex` to 0 at the start of each agent run. An LHC turn is a whole exchange. The converter derives LHC turns from PI traffic:\\n\\n- An LHC turn **opens** on a `user_prompt` event.\\n- It stays open across every agent step — each PI `turn_end` within the run is **ignored as an LHC turn boundary**.\\n- It **closes** with one LHC `turn_end` event when the agent run completes (no further tool calls; PI's `agent_end`), or implicitly when the next `user_prompt` arrives.\\n\\n**Worked example.** A user asks one question that triggers two tool calls, then gets a final answer. PI emits:\\n\\n```\\nturn_start(0)\\n  message_end(user [text])\\n  message_end(assistant [thinking, toolCall, toolCall])\\n  message_end(toolResult) × 2\\nturn_end(0)\\nturn_start(1)\\n  message_end(assistant [text])\\nturn_end(1)\\nagent_end\\n```\\n\\nThe converter emits **one** LHC turn:\\n\\n```\\nuser_prompt, assistant_thinking, tool_call, tool_call, tool_result, tool_result,\\nassistant_text, turn_end\\n```\\n\\nPI's `turn_end(0)` and `turn_end(1)` produce no LHC `turn_end`; the single LHC `turn_end` is emitted at `agent_end`. Session order comes from source-event order through the converter, never from PI's per-agent-run `turnIndex`.\\n\\n### Abort and crash shapes\\n\\n| Shape | PI signal | Converter behavior |\\n|-------|-----------|--------------------|\\n| Graceful interrupt | assistant `message_end` with partial content and `stopReason: aborted`; `turn_end` `aborted`; `agent_end` fires | Capture the partial content; carry the aborted disposition; close the LHC turn normally (a complete-but-aborted turn) |\\n| Hard kill | user `message_end` only; no assistant close, no `turn_end`, no `agent_end` | On reattach, find a dangling open turn with a trailing user prompt; tolerate it (LHC `turn_end`-with-no-open-turn is a no-op); resume capture without duplicating the recorded prefix |\\n\\n### Idempotency key\\n\\nEach emitted event carries a stable idempotency key so re-delivery (reload, crash-replay) is recognized and skipped. Construction precedence (from the POC mapper, carried forward): PI entry id (`pi:<session>:entry:<entryId>`) → provider response id / tool-call id → content fingerprint as last resort. The exact construction is tech design; the contract is that the key is stable across re-delivery of the same logical event and unique across distinct events.\\n\\n### Thread resolution and fork lineage\\n\\nThe thread is resolved at launch through the registry catalog. The four launch modes and their registry operations:\\n\\n| Launch | Registry operation |\\n|--------|--------------------|\\n| no flag | `newThread({ filePath, cwd, title })` — new thread, registered |\\n| `--session <id>` | `resolve({ threadId })`, full or partial-id match |\\n| `--continue` / `-c` | resolve the most recently created thread |\\n| `--resume` / `-r` | `listThreads({ cwd })` → picker → `resolve({ threadId })` |\\n\\nThe contract: the thread id is an input to the run (from the operator's launch choice or the picker), so resolution holds no in-memory object from a prior process and there is no two-record mapping to drift. An ambiguous or unresolvable `--session` id fails with an actionable message; it never silently creates a new thread.\\n\\nFork is the one case where a thread is created mid-run rather than chosen at launch. Fork detection does not depend on a `session_start` fork reason — recon found print-mode `--fork` reports `reason=startup`. The fork point comes from the `session_before_fork` hook (`entryId`, `position`) when present; in Epic 1, PI's session tree (the `parentId` chain) is also available as fork evidence, because PI still runs its own session here. The seeded thread is built by replay (Flow 3) and the source thread is never written. The long-term home for fork lineage is LHC thread metadata (Feature 2+, once PI's session file goes vestigial); this epic does not depend on PI's session tree as the permanent source.\\n\\n### Model-call function (host-supplied)\\n\\nThe contract LHC depends on (`packages/lhc/src/inference/types.ts`, Epic 05):\\n\\n```\\nModelCall = (input) => Promise<ModelCallResult>\\n\\ninput:  { provider: string, model: string,\\n          messages: { role: \\\"system\\\" | \\\"user\\\", content: string }[] }\\n\\nresult: { ok: true,  text: string }\\n      | { ok: false, kind: ModelCallFailureKind, message: string }\\n\\nModelCallFailureKind =\\n  \\\"auth\\\" | \\\"invalid_request\\\"          (terminal)\\n  | \\\"rate_limit\\\" | \\\"timeout\\\" | \\\"network\\\"  (retryable)\\n  | \\\"empty_output\\\"                    (adapter-generated; host never returns it)\\n  | \\\"other\\\"                           (thrown exceptions classify here)\\n```\\n\\n`provider` and `model` are opaque routing keys; only the host's function interprets them. Single-turn only — no tools, no streaming, no multi-turn state.\\n\\n### Model assignments\\n\\n```\\nModelAssignment = { provider: string, model: string, prompt: string }\\nassignments: Record<FormKind, ModelAssignment>   // all seven, validated complete at init\\n```\\n\\n`FormKind` is the seven derivation kinds: `smoothed_prompt`, `tool_call_summary`, `tool_result_summary`, `turn_rendering`, `lower_band_projection`, `chunk_summary_detailed`, `chunk_summary_brief`. `prompt` must name a registered prompt. Initialization fails loudly if any kind is missing, references an unknown prompt, or carries a placeholder.\\n\\n### Error shapes\\n\\nCapture operations return LHC's `OpResult` (typed ok/error with a code), never throwing into the PI hook. When the thread is writable, a capture failure is recorded against the thread and surfaced through health (Flow 2, Flow 6). When the store is unavailable, the extension records a diagnostic/health signal outside the thread because no durable gap can be written. Neither path propagates an exception that would break the PI session.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Non-Functional Requirements\\n\\n- **Observe-only.** Nothing the model sees changes in this epic. PI's native context handling runs untouched; the extension registers no `context` hook behavior here.\\n- **No perceptible latency.** Capture work does not block the user's interactive turn. Derivations run in LHC's background scheduler mode.\\n- **Crash safety.** A hard kill leaves a dangling open turn; the next run that resolves the same thread tolerates it without loss or duplication. There is no mapping to lose in an early-crash window — the thread is resolved from the launch choice, not recovered from a session file.\\n- **No stale context held.** The extension holds only plain data across hooks. It never caches a PI context or session-manager object beyond the handler that received it.\\n- **Headless parity.** Every user-facing behavior in this epic (startup-validation reporting) works in PI's `rpc`, `json`, and `print` modes, guarded on UI availability — never assuming a TUI. (Headless conformance as a delivered guarantee is Epic 4; the constraint is honored here so nothing built now blocks it.)\\n- **Fail loud on misconfiguration.** Missing, unknown, or placeholder assignments fail at initialization with an actionable error, not a silent skip.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Tech Design Questions\\n\\n1. **Fork derived-form reuse** — whether provenance identity (deterministic IDs) can be proven safe enough to copy derived forms across a fork, or whether forks always requeue. Informed by LHC's deterministic-ID property.\\n2. **`thinkingSignature` handling** — assistant thinking carries an opaque encrypted reasoning token; store it on the event or drop it. An LHC call.\\n3. **Converter structure** — the converter is the productionized M0 prototype; how it consumes `message_end` streams and emits batched `MessageEventInput[]` per the `intakeStream.messageEvents` contract.\\n4. **Title derivation** — what populates a thread's registry `title` for the picker (first user prompt, a derived summary, or operator-set), and how a mid-session rename updates it.\\n\\n> Thread mapping is **not** a tech design question — the thread is resolved at launch through the registry (Flow 1), with no stored PI-session→thread mapping, so the former \\\"mapping persistence shape\\\" and \\\"resolution by external key\\\" questions are dissolved, not deferred. (PI keeps its own session file in this observe-only epic; it is just not where thread identity lives.)\\n\\n> Image / file-reference handling is **not** a tech design question — it is an M0 decision (A-7), settled before this epic's tech design per the PRD's M0 gate.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Story Breakdown\\n\\nPartitioning follows the build path: each story lands testable behavior on the structure the previous one established. Capture comes first; verification proves it; fork and inference build on it. The flow-per-story cut was adjusted in two places: verification (Flow 6) is sequenced right after capture because it is how capture is proven, and fork (Flow 3) is its own story because its replay-seeding machinery is distinct from the reattach logic of Flow 1.\\n\\n### Story 0: Extension Foundation\\n\\n**Delivers:** The walking skeleton: extension package scaffold, the PI extension entry point and hook registration rail, the plain-data-only state holder, the LHC instance init/dispose seam (stubbed), test fixture and corpus-loading harness, and typed fail-closed stubs.\\n**Governing idea:** Every later story adds behavior to a structure that already loads in PI, holds no stale context, and fails closed with typed errors.\\n**Prerequisite:** None.\\n**Boundary / risk notes:** Stubs fail closed, never fake success (tech arch convention). The plain-data-only rule (AC-1.3) is established here as the state-holder shape so no later story is tempted to cache a context object.\\n**Flows/ACs covered:** None directly; smoke tests only — extension loads, registers hooks, init/dispose seam runs, fixture loader produces valid intake-event shapes.\\n**Estimated test count:** 4–6 smoke tests\\n\\n### Story 1: Session Lifecycle and Thread Resolution\\n\\n**Delivers:** `initLhc`/dispose per session, launch-driven thread resolution (new / `--session` partial-id / `--continue` / `--resume` cwd picker) through the registry, reload reconstruction from the resolved thread id, plain-data-only state.\\n**Governing idea:** The right thread is resolved from the operator's launch choice and found again across reload and restart, with no two-record mapping to drift.\\n**Prerequisite:** Story 0.\\n**Boundary / risk notes:** Resolution is launch-input-driven, so there is no early-crash mapping window. The cwd column, partial-id resolve, and cwd-filtered list are the small LHC additions (A-8); the picker UI is thin pi-lhc. An unresolvable `--session` id must fail loudly, never silently create a new thread (AC-1.6).\\n**Flows/ACs covered:** Flow 1 complete — AC-1.1 through AC-1.7.\\n**Estimated test count:** 11–14\\n\\n### Story 2: Event Capture and Turn Derivation\\n\\n**Delivers:** The converter — PI `message_end` → LHC intake events with content-part fan-out, turn derivation (PI per-step turns collapsed to one LHC turn), parallel-tool correlation, error-result and aborted-turn capture, idempotency-keyed dedup, capture-failure isolation.\\n**Governing idea:** Every PI message lands as ordered LHC events exactly once, and LHC turn boundaries are derived correctly from PI traffic.\\n**Prerequisite:** Story 1.\\n**Boundary / risk notes:** The turn-derivation contract (AC-2.2) is the highest-risk item — building it wrong was the single failure recon prevented. Capture failure must isolate to a recorded gap, never an exception into the hook (AC-2.7). Runtime-change capture (AC-2.8) is in-stream-only — if not grabbed when it fires, it is lost.\\n**Flows/ACs covered:** Flow 2 complete — AC-2.1 through AC-2.8.\\n**Estimated test count:** 15–18\\n\\n### Story 3: Capture Verification\\n\\n**Delivers:** Corpus replay through the converter, read-back matching against fixtures, deterministic re-replay, and inspect overview/health reflecting the captured session.\\n**Governing idea:** Capture correctness is provable from recorded corpora without serving anything to a model.\\n**Prerequisite:** Story 2. Consumes M0 corpora as fixtures.\\n**Boundary / risk notes:** This story's fixtures are the M0 corpora (A-2). If corpora are not yet broad, the story builds the replay-equality and inspect machinery against whatever corpora exist and widens as M0 delivers.\\n**Flows/ACs covered:** Flow 6 complete — AC-6.1 through AC-6.3.\\n**Estimated test count:** 8–10\\n\\n### Story 4: Fork as New Thread\\n\\n**Delivers:** New-thread-per-fork seeded by replay to the fork point, source-thread immutability, derived-form reuse-or-requeue by provenance.\\n**Governing idea:** A fork is a new thread that mechanically reproduces the source up to the fork point and never writes the source.\\n**Prerequisite:** Story 2 (replay needs capture), Story 1 (new thread + mapping).\\n**Boundary / risk notes:** Derived-form reuse (AC-3.3) is the one optional optimization — correct behavior must hold whether reuse is taken or the derivations requeue. Reuse safety is a tech design question (#2).\\n**Flows/ACs covered:** Flow 3 complete — AC-3.1 through AC-3.3.\\n**Estimated test count:** 6–8\\n\\n### Story 5: Inference Host Routing\\n\\n**Delivers:** The injected model-call function over PI's model registry and auth, multi-lane routing, provider-error classification into LHC's failure kinds, and the end-to-end proof that a captured thread's queued derivation work runs through the function and records its outcome.\\n**Governing idea:** LHC's derivations reach the user's existing PI logins through one function that treats provider/model as opaque keys, and the capture→derivation→recorded-outcome loop closes.\\n**Prerequisite:** Story 1 (instance exists) for the function and classification (AC-4.1–AC-4.4); Story 2 (captured content to derive from) for the closed-loop proof (AC-4.5).\\n**Boundary / risk notes:** This is the host side of the LHC v1 inference seam (A-5) — the function shape must match `ModelCall` exactly so LHC's adapter consumes it unchanged. AC-4.5 is the PRD M1 \\\"confirm derivations land\\\" gate; it proves the loop, not just the function.\\n**Flows/ACs covered:** Flow 4 complete — AC-4.1 through AC-4.5.\\n**Estimated test count:** 10–12\\n\\n### Story 6: Startup Validation and Assignment Config\\n\\n**Delivers:** Seven-assignment validation against PI's registry at session start, unreachable-lane reporting (interactive and headless), config load with defaults, user override on next start, fail-loud on misconfiguration.\\n**Governing idea:** Bad lanes are visible before first use, and capture survives them.\\n**Prerequisite:** Story 5 (validation probes the routing function).\\n**Boundary / risk notes:** Validation failure must leave capture running (AC-5.3) — the two concerns are independent. Reporting must not assume a TUI (NFR headless parity).\\n**Flows/ACs covered:** Flow 5 complete — AC-5.1 through AC-5.5.\\n**Estimated test count:** 9–11\\n\\n### Sequencing\\n\\n```mermaid\\nflowchart LR\\n  S0[\\\"S0: Foundation\\\"] --> S1[\\\"S1: Lifecycle + Mapping\\\"]\\n  S1 --> S2[\\\"S2: Capture + Turn Derivation\\\"]\\n  S2 --> S3[\\\"S3: Capture Verification\\\"]\\n  S2 --> S4[\\\"S4: Fork\\\"]\\n  S1 --> S5[\\\"S5: Inference Routing\\\"]\\n  S2 -.->|closed-loop proof| S5\\n  S5 --> S6[\\\"S6: Startup Validation + Config\\\"]\\n```\\n\\nCapture spine S0→S1→S2→S3 is strictly linear. S4 (fork) depends on capture. S5→S6 (inference) branch from S1; the function and classification (AC-4.1–AC-4.4) are independent of the capture spine, while the closed-loop proof (AC-4.5) draws on Story 2's captured content. Total estimated tests: 63–79.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Traceability\\n\\n| AC | TCs | Story |\\n|----|-----|-------|\\n| AC-1.1 | TC-1.1 | 1 |\\n| AC-1.2 | TC-1.2 | 1 |\\n| AC-1.3 | TC-1.3 | 1 |\\n| AC-1.4 | TC-1.4 | 1 |\\n| AC-1.5 | TC-1.5 | 1 |\\n| AC-1.6 | TC-1.6 | 1 |\\n| AC-1.7 | TC-1.7 | 1 |\\n| AC-2.1 | TC-2.1 | 2 |\\n| AC-2.2 | TC-2.2, TC-2.3 | 2 |\\n| AC-2.3 | TC-2.4 | 2 |\\n| AC-2.4 | TC-2.5 | 2 |\\n| AC-2.5 | TC-2.6 | 2 |\\n| AC-2.6 | TC-2.7 | 2 |\\n| AC-2.7 | TC-2.8 | 2 |\\n| AC-2.8 | TC-2.9 | 2 |\\n| AC-3.1 | TC-3.1 | 4 |\\n| AC-3.2 | TC-3.2 | 4 |\\n| AC-3.3 | TC-3.3 | 4 |\\n| AC-4.1 | TC-4.1 | 5 |\\n| AC-4.2 | TC-4.2 | 5 |\\n| AC-4.3 | TC-4.3 | 5 |\\n| AC-4.4 | TC-4.4 | 5 |\\n| AC-4.5 | TC-4.5 | 5 |\\n| AC-5.1 | TC-5.1 | 6 |\\n| AC-5.2 | TC-5.2 | 6 |\\n| AC-5.3 | TC-5.3 | 6 |\\n| AC-5.4 | TC-5.4 | 6 |\\n| AC-5.5 | TC-5.5, TC-5.6 | 6 |\\n| AC-6.1 | TC-6.1 | 3 |\\n| AC-6.2 | TC-6.2 | 3 |\\n| AC-6.3 | TC-6.3 | 3 |\\n\\n**Test Conditions**\\n\\n- **TC-1.1** — Fresh `session_start` with no thread-selecting launch flag: one LHC instance initialized in background mode, one new registry thread created with cwd/title metadata, and no PI-session-to-thread mapping record created.\\n- **TC-1.2** — `session_start` with launch input resolving an existing registry thread: initializes against that same thread and creates no second thread.\\n- **TC-1.3** — A session replacement invalidates the prior context object; the extension, holding only plain data, continues capture using the fresh context.\\n- **TC-1.4** — `session_shutdown` disposes with flush; reattach finds the thread complete through the last pre-shutdown event.\\n- **TC-1.5** — Reload re-initializes the extension; thread reference reconstructed from the resolved thread id; capture continues on the same thread.\\n- **TC-1.6** — Launch by mode resolves correctly: no flag creates a new thread; `--session` resolves by full and by partial id; `--continue` resolves the most recent; an ambiguous/unresolvable id fails with an actionable message and creates no thread.\\n- **TC-1.7** — `--resume` lists cwd-scoped threads with title and creation time and resolves a selection; an empty cwd lists nothing rather than failing.\\n- **TC-2.1** — A user/assistant/toolResult sequence records ordered events; an assistant `[thinking, text, toolCall]` fans out in that order.\\n- **TC-2.2** — A one-prompt-two-tools-then-answer corpus (the worked example) yields exactly one LHC turn with one `turn_end` at agent-run completion; PI's per-step `turn_end`s produce none.\\n- **TC-2.3** — Session order derives from converter source-event order, not `turnIndex`; two agent runs each starting at `turnIndex 0` order correctly.\\n- **TC-2.4** — Parallel tool calls with out-of-order completion correlate each result to its call by `toolCallId`.\\n- **TC-2.5** — A tool result with `isError` is captured as a `tool_result` with the error flag and content; nothing dropped.\\n- **TC-2.6** — A graceful-interrupt corpus records the partial assistant content with the aborted disposition; the turn closes complete-but-aborted.\\n- **TC-2.7** — A re-delivered event (reload/replay) is skipped by idempotency key; a normal resume re-delivers no historical events.\\n- **TC-2.8** — A malformed/unmappable event on a writable thread records a durable, queryable gap surfaced in health; an unavailable thread store surfaces an extension diagnostic / health signal with no durable gap. Both continue the session with no exception reaching the hook.\\n- **TC-2.9** — A `model_select` and a `thinking_level_select` each record an ordered `runtime_note` event carrying the new and previous values; ordering relative to surrounding messages is preserved.\\n- **TC-3.1** — Fork creates a new thread; the source thread receives no writes — its logical read-back (events, messages, turns) is unchanged. Detection resolves the fork point from the `session_before_fork` hook, with PI's session tree as available fallback evidence in Epic 1, not from a fork reason code.\\n- **TC-3.2** — Fork replay seeds the new thread; read-back matches the source through the fork point.\\n- **TC-3.3** — Fork with safe provenance reuses derived forms; fork with unprovable provenance requeues them; both yield a correct thread.\\n- **TC-4.1** — The model-call function resolves a (provider, model) pair through PI's registry and returns completion text for a single-turn message list.\\n- **TC-4.2** — Two derivation kinds assigned to different (provider, model) pairs both route correctly in one session.\\n- **TC-4.5** — A captured thread with queued derivation work, drained through LHC's scheduler with the injected function wired, persists at least one ready derived form (function returns text) and one classified failure (function returns a failure), both queryable through inspect/health — proving the capture→derivation→recorded-outcome loop, not just the function in isolation.\\n- **TC-4.3** — Auth and invalid-request map to terminal; rate-limit, timeout, network map to retryable; a thrown exception maps to `other`.\\n- **TC-4.4** — A resolved call with no output is classified `empty_output` by the adapter; the host function returns text-or-transport-failure only.\\n- **TC-5.1** — All seven assignments validate against the registry at session start before first use.\\n- **TC-5.2** — An unreachable lane reports kind + (provider, model) + fix; the report appears in a headless mode, not only the TUI.\\n- **TC-5.3** — A validation failure leaves capture running; the affected lane's derivations fail classified and queryable.\\n- **TC-5.4** — Each of the seven kinds loads a (provider, model, prompt) from config with shipped defaults; the prompt resolves to a registered prompt.\\n- **TC-5.5** — A user override of a kind's provider/model/prompt takes effect on the next session start with no code change.\\n- **TC-5.6** — An incomplete or unknown assignment fails loudly at initialization with an actionable error; no placeholder default masks it.\\n- **TC-6.1** — Each corpus (chatty, tool-heavy, parallel-tool, error-result, aborted-turn) replays to a thread whose read-back matches the fixture.\\n- **TC-6.2** — The same corpus replayed twice yields identical read-back (IDs and order stable).\\n- **TC-6.3** — inspect overview/health report event/message counts, last event position, and any gaps or failed derivations.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Validation Checklist\\n\\n- [x] User Profile uses the four-field shape (Primary User, Context, Mental Model, Key Constraint)\\n- [x] Every flow has numbered ACs and TCs mapping to them\\n- [x] Every AC appears in the traceability table with at least one TC and one story\\n- [x] Scope states in, out, and where out-of-scope items are handled\\n- [x] Migration (POC threads) stated explicitly as out of scope\\n- [x] Assumptions carry status and impact-if-wrong, including the M0 dependency\\n- [x] Data contracts cover every operation the flows exercise, including the turn-derivation worked example, abort/crash shapes, and error shapes\\n- [x] The load-bearing turn-derivation contract (PI turn ≠ LHC turn) is stated with a worked example\\n- [x] No implementation detail below the extension/SDK boundary (no storage layout, no module internals, no library names beyond the named boundary contracts)\\n- [x] Behavior described as what the system does, not why it matters\\n- [x] Story breakdown sequenced with prerequisites, governing ideas, and test estimates\\n- [ ] Consumer test: a tech designer can design from this without asking foundational questions — pending review\\n\",\"numLines\":580,\"startLine\":1,\"totalLines\":580}}}\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/artifacts/01-session-lifecycle-and-thread-resolution/streams/003-implementor.stdout.log:113:{\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_01HuqoctC6TYucCgbmEK4Yvu\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"1\\t// Extension entry. PI loads this module and calls `activate(pi)`; it registers\\n2\\t// the Epic 1 observe-only hook rail and routes each hook to a fail-closed\\n3\\t// handler. It owns no state beyond the plain-data `SessionState` (and, later, an\\n4\\t// `LhcInstance`) held in the connector closure — never a PI `ctx`/session\\n5\\t// object (tech design Flow 1, plain-data rule).\\n6\\timport type {\\n7\\t  ExtensionAPI,\\n8\\t  PiHookHandler,\\n9\\t  PiHookName,\\n10\\t} from \\\"./pi/types.js\\\";\\n11\\timport type { CaptureFailureDiagnostic, SessionState } from \\\"./lifecycle/state.js\\\";\\n12\\t\\n13\\texport { disposeInstance, initInstance, initLhc } from \\\"./lifecycle/instance.js\\\";\\n14\\texport type { LhcInstance } from \\\"./shared/instance.js\\\";\\n15\\t\\n16\\t/** The Epic 1 observe-only hook rail: every PI hook the connector consumes.\\n17\\t *  Deliberately NOT `context` — context serving is Epic 2 and this epic\\n18\\t *  registers no handler for it (story Scope Out). */\\n19\\texport const EPIC_1_HOOKS = [\\n20\\t  \\\"session_start\\\",\\n21\\t  \\\"message_end\\\",\\n22\\t  \\\"turn_end\\\",\\n23\\t  \\\"agent_end\\\",\\n24\\t  \\\"model_select\\\",\\n25\\t  \\\"thinking_level_select\\\",\\n26\\t  \\\"session_before_fork\\\",\\n27\\t  \\\"session_before_switch\\\",\\n28\\t  \\\"session_shutdown\\\",\\n29\\t] as const satisfies readonly PiHookName[];\\n30\\t\\n31\\texport type Epic1Hook = (typeof EPIC_1_HOOKS)[number];\\n32\\t\\n33\\t/** Everything the connector retains across hooks — all plain data, by\\n34\\t *  construction structuredClone-able. There is no field that could hold a PI\\n35\\t *  object. */\\n36\\texport interface ConnectorSnapshot {\\n37\\t  state: SessionState | null;\\n38\\t  lastDiagnostic: CaptureFailureDiagnostic | null;\\n39\\t}\\n40\\t\\n41\\texport interface Connector {\\n42\\t  /** Register the observe-only hook rail on the PI extension API. */\\n43\\t  register(pi: ExtensionAPI): void;\\n44\\t  /** The plain-data state retained across hooks. `null` until a session\\n45\\t   *  resolves a thread (Story 1); never holds a PI ctx. */\\n46\\t  getState(): SessionState | null;\\n47\\t  /** Plain-data snapshot of all retained state (inspection/test seam). */\\n48\\t  snapshot(): ConnectorSnapshot;\\n49\\t  /** The hook handlers, keyed by event — exposed so tests can drive them with\\n50\\t   *  synthetic ctx/events without a live PI. Each accepts any Epic 1 event\\n51\\t   *  (the foundation handlers are uniform no-ops). */\\n52\\t  readonly handlers: Readonly<Record<Epic1Hook, PiHookHandler<Epic1Hook>>>;\\n53\\t}\\n54\\t\\n55\\t/** Build a connector instance. Its mutable state lives in this closure; the\\n56\\t *  registered handlers capture it, so the instance stays live for the session\\n57\\t *  even when `activate` discards the returned object. */\\n58\\texport function createConnector(): Connector {\\n59\\t  // The connector's ENTIRE retained state. Both are plain data and are the only\\n60\\t  // things that survive between hooks. `state` stays null in Epic 1 Story 0\\n61\\t  // (thread resolution is fail-closed until Story 1); Story 1 populates it via\\n62\\t  // createSessionState once a thread resolves.\\n63\\t  let state: SessionState | null = null;\\n64\\t  let lastDiagnostic: CaptureFailureDiagnostic | null = null;\\n65\\t\\n66\\t  // Contain every handler: an observe-only hook must never throw back into PI\\n67\\t  // (a thrown hook breaks the user's session). A caught error becomes a\\n68\\t  // plain-data diagnostic; it is never rethrown.\\n69\\t  const guard = (\\n70\\t    name: Epic1Hook,\\n71\\t    body: PiHookHandler<Epic1Hook>,\\n72\\t  ): PiHookHandler<Epic1Hook> => {\\n73\\t    return async (ctx, event): Promise<void> => {\\n74\\t      try {\\n75\\t        await body(ctx, event);\\n76\\t      } catch (err) {\\n77\\t        lastDiagnostic = {\\n78\\t          code: \\\"hook_handler_error\\\",\\n79\\t          message: `${name}: ${err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err)}`,\\n80\\t          recordedGap: false,\\n81\\t        };\\n82\\t      }\\n83\\t    };\\n84\\t  };\\n85\\t\\n86\\t  // Observe-only foundation: every hook routes to a no-op that receives a FRESH\\n87\\t  // ctx and retains none of it.\\n88\\t  const noop: PiHookHandler<Epic1Hook> = () => {\\n89\\t    // Intentionally empty — later stories replace this with real routing.\\n90\\t  };\\n91\\t\\n92\\t  // Story 0 routes each hook to a fail-closed no-op: the rail is real and routes\\n93\\t  // to a handler, but no capture / resolution / derivation happens or is faked\\n94\\t  // yet. The accumulator is filled for every hook in the loop below.\\n95\\t  const handlers = {} as Record<Epic1Hook, PiHookHandler<Epic1Hook>>;\\n96\\t  for (const name of EPIC_1_HOOKS) handlers[name] = guard(name, noop);\\n97\\t\\n98\\t  return {\\n99\\t    handlers,\\n100\\t    register(pi: ExtensionAPI): void {\\n101\\t      for (const name of EPIC_1_HOOKS) pi.registerHook(name, handlers[name]);\\n102\\t    },\\n103\\t    getState(): SessionState | null {\\n104\\t      return state;\\n105\\t    },\\n106\\t    snapshot(): ConnectorSnapshot {\\n107\\t      return { state, lastDiagnostic };\\n108\\t    },\\n109\\t  };\\n110\\t}\\n111\\t\\n112\\t/** PI entry point: PI calls this with the ExtensionAPI when the extension\\n113\\t *  loads (and again on `/reload`). Registers the observe-only hook rail. */\\n114\\texport function activate(pi: ExtensionAPI): void {\\n115\\t  createConnector().register(pi);\\n116\\t}\\n117\\t\\n118\\texport default activate;\\n119\\t\"}]},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"3bd99b80-3187-4d52-9c3e-b6b2c0cfc11e\",\"uuid\":\"5bf6318b-77dd-42ac-9d65-0caadabbc9b2\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-06-14T04:03:47.467Z\",\"tool_use_result\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"file\":{\"filePath\":\"/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/pi-lhc/src/index.ts\",\"content\":\"// Extension entry. PI loads this module and calls `activate(pi)`; it registers\\n// the Epic 1 observe-only hook rail and routes each hook to a fail-closed\\n// handler. It owns no state beyond the plain-data `SessionState` (and, later, an\\n// `LhcInstance`) held in the connector closure — never a PI `ctx`/session\\n// object (tech design Flow 1, plain-data rule).\\nimport type {\\n  ExtensionAPI,\\n  PiHookHandler,\\n  PiHookName,\\n} from \\\"./pi/types.js\\\";\\nimport type { CaptureFailureDiagnostic, SessionState } from \\\"./lifecycle/state.js\\\";\\n\\nexport { disposeInstance, initInstance, initLhc } from \\\"./lifecycle/instance.js\\\";\\nexport type { LhcInstance } from \\\"./shared/instance.js\\\";\\n\\n/** The Epic 1 observe-only hook rail: every PI hook the connector consumes.\\n *  Deliberately NOT `context` — context serving is Epic 2 and this epic\\n *  registers no handler for it (story Scope Out). */\\nexport const EPIC_1_HOOKS = [\\n  \\\"session_start\\\",\\n  \\\"message_end\\\",\\n  \\\"turn_end\\\",\\n  \\\"agent_end\\\",\\n  \\\"model_select\\\",\\n  \\\"thinking_level_select\\\",\\n  \\\"session_before_fork\\\",\\n  \\\"session_before_switch\\\",\\n  \\\"session_shutdown\\\",\\n] as const satisfies readonly PiHookName[];\\n\\nexport type Epic1Hook = (typeof EPIC_1_HOOKS)[number];\\n\\n/** Everything the connector retains across hooks — all plain data, by\\n *  construction structuredClone-able. There is no field that could hold a PI\\n *  object. */\\nexport interface ConnectorSnapshot {\\n  state: SessionState | null;\\n  lastDiagnostic: CaptureFailureDiagnostic | null;\\n}\\n\\nexport interface Connector {\\n  /** Register the observe-only hook rail on the PI extension API. */\\n  register(pi: ExtensionAPI): void;\\n  /** The plain-data state retained across hooks. `null` until a session\\n   *  resolves a thread (Story 1); never holds a PI ctx. */\\n  getState(): SessionState | null;\\n  /** Plain-data snapshot of all retained state (inspection/test seam). */\\n  snapshot(): ConnectorSnapshot;\\n  /** The hook handlers, keyed by event — exposed so tests can drive them with\\n   *  synthetic ctx/events without a live PI. Each accepts any Epic 1 event\\n   *  (the foundation handlers are uniform no-ops). */\\n  readonly handlers: Readonly<Record<Epic1Hook, PiHookHandler<Epic1Hook>>>;\\n}\\n\\n/** Build a connector instance. Its mutable state lives in this closure; the\\n *  registered handlers capture it, so the instance stays live for the session\\n *  even when `activate` discards the returned object. */\\nexport function createConnector(): Connector {\\n  // The connector's ENTIRE retained state. Both are plain data and are the only\\n  // things that survive between hooks. `state` stays null in Epic 1 Story 0\\n  // (thread resolution is fail-closed until Story 1); Story 1 populates it via\\n  // createSessionState once a thread resolves.\\n  let state: SessionState | null = null;\\n  let lastDiagnostic: CaptureFailureDiagnostic | null = null;\\n\\n  // Contain every handler: an observe-only hook must never throw back into PI\\n  // (a thrown hook breaks the user's session). A caught error becomes a\\n  // plain-data diagnostic; it is never rethrown.\\n  const guard = (\\n    name: Epic1Hook,\\n    body: PiHookHandler<Epic1Hook>,\\n  ): PiHookHandler<Epic1Hook> => {\\n    return async (ctx, event): Promise<void> => {\\n      try {\\n        await body(ctx, event);\\n      } catch (err) {\\n        lastDiagnostic = {\\n          code: \\\"hook_handler_error\\\",\\n          message: `${name}: ${err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err)}`,\\n          recordedGap: false,\\n        };\\n      }\\n    };\\n  };\\n\\n  // Observe-only foundation: every hook routes to a no-op that receives a FRESH\\n  // ctx and retains none of it.\\n  const noop: PiHookHandler<Epic1Hook> = () => {\\n    // Intentionally empty — later stories replace this with real routing.\\n  };\\n\\n  // Story 0 routes each hook to a fail-closed no-op: the rail is real and routes\\n  // to a handler, but no capture / resolution / derivation happens or is faked\\n  // yet. The accumulator is filled for every hook in the loop below.\\n  const handlers = {} as Record<Epic1Hook, PiHookHandler<Epic1Hook>>;\\n  for (const name of EPIC_1_HOOKS) handlers[name] = guard(name, noop);\\n\\n  return {\\n    handlers,\\n    register(pi: ExtensionAPI): void {\\n      for (const name of EPIC_1_HOOKS) pi.registerHook(name, handlers[name]);\\n    },\\n    getState(): SessionState | null {\\n      return state;\\n    },\\n    snapshot(): ConnectorSnapshot {\\n      return { state, lastDiagnostic };\\n    },\\n  };\\n}\\n\\n/** PI entry point: PI calls this with the ExtensionAPI when the extension\\n *  loads (and again on `/reload`). Registers the observe-only hook rail. */\\nexport function activate(pi: ExtensionAPI): void {\\n  createConnector().register(pi);\\n}\\n\\nexport default activate;\\n\",\"numLines\":119,\"startLine\":1,\"totalLines\":119}}}\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/artifacts/01-session-lifecycle-and-thread-resolution/streams/003-implementor.stdout.log:191:{\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_01153vgrgkpcwjNvzKjeKvav\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"1\\t// Local declarations of the PI extension surface the connector consumes.\\n2\\t//\\n3\\t// These mirror `@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent` v0.79.2 as verified by the\\n4\\t// wiring research (docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md,\\n5\\t// headless + interactive recon, June 12 2026). The PI packages are not yet a\\n6\\t// build-time dependency of this workspace, so the connector declares the slice\\n7\\t// of their contract it touches and swaps to the real imports when the\\n8\\t// dependency lands (Spec Deviation, recorded in the story payload). Keeping the\\n9\\t// surface here — rather than scattered `any` — means later stories get real\\n10\\t// type-checking against the contract PI actually fires.\\n11\\t\\n12\\t// ── Hook vocabulary ──────────────────────────────────────────────────\\n13\\t\\n14\\t/** Hooks the connector knows about. Epic 1 registers the observe-only rail;\\n15\\t *  `context` is fired by PI but Epic 1 registers no handler for it — context\\n16\\t *  serving is Epic 2. */\\n17\\texport type PiHookName =\\n18\\t  | \\\"session_start\\\"\\n19\\t  | \\\"message_end\\\"\\n20\\t  | \\\"turn_end\\\"\\n21\\t  | \\\"agent_end\\\"\\n22\\t  | \\\"model_select\\\"\\n23\\t  | \\\"thinking_level_select\\\"\\n24\\t  | \\\"session_before_fork\\\"\\n25\\t  | \\\"session_before_switch\\\"\\n26\\t  | \\\"session_shutdown\\\"\\n27\\t  | \\\"context\\\";\\n28\\t\\n29\\texport type SessionStartReason = \\\"startup\\\" | \\\"reload\\\" | \\\"new\\\" | \\\"resume\\\" | \\\"fork\\\";\\n30\\texport type SessionShutdownReason = \\\"shutdown\\\" | \\\"reload\\\" | \\\"new\\\" | \\\"resume\\\";\\n31\\t\\n32\\texport interface SessionStartEvent {\\n33\\t  reason: SessionStartReason;\\n34\\t  previousSessionFile?: string;\\n35\\t}\\n36\\texport interface MessageEndEvent {\\n37\\t  message: AgentMessage;\\n38\\t}\\n39\\t/** PI fires one turn_end per agent STEP (turnIndex resets to 0 each run); the\\n40\\t *  converter must NOT map it 1:1 to an LHC turn_end (tech design Flow 2). */\\n41\\texport interface TurnEndEvent {\\n42\\t  turnIndex: number;\\n43\\t  message: AgentMessage;\\n44\\t  toolResults: ToolResultMessage[];\\n45\\t  stopReason?: PiStopReason;\\n46\\t}\\n47\\texport interface AgentEndEvent {\\n48\\t  messages: AgentMessage[];\\n49\\t}\\n50\\texport interface ModelSelectEvent {\\n51\\t  model: ModelDescriptor;\\n52\\t  previousModel?: ModelDescriptor;\\n53\\t  source?: string;\\n54\\t}\\n55\\texport interface ThinkingLevelSelectEvent {\\n56\\t  level: string;\\n57\\t  previousLevel: string;\\n58\\t}\\n59\\texport interface SessionBeforeForkEvent {\\n60\\t  entryId: string;\\n61\\t  position?: number;\\n62\\t}\\n63\\texport interface SessionBeforeSwitchEvent {\\n64\\t  intent: \\\"new\\\" | \\\"resume\\\";\\n65\\t}\\n66\\texport interface SessionShutdownEvent {\\n67\\t  reason: SessionShutdownReason;\\n68\\t  targetSessionFile?: string;\\n69\\t}\\n70\\t/** Declared for completeness; Epic 1 registers no handler (observe-only). */\\n71\\texport interface ContextEvent {\\n72\\t  messages: AgentMessage[];\\n73\\t}\\n74\\t\\n75\\t/** Maps each hook to the payload PI delivers with it. */\\n76\\texport interface PiHookEventMap {\\n77\\t  session_start: SessionStartEvent;\\n78\\t  message_end: MessageEndEvent;\\n79\\t  turn_end: TurnEndEvent;\\n80\\t  agent_end: AgentEndEvent;\\n81\\t  model_select: ModelSelectEvent;\\n82\\t  thinking_level_select: ThinkingLevelSelectEvent;\\n83\\t  session_before_fork: SessionBeforeForkEvent;\\n84\\t  session_before_switch: SessionBeforeSwitchEvent;\\n85\\t  session_shutdown: SessionShutdownEvent;\\n86\\t  context: ContextEvent;\\n87\\t}\\n88\\t\\n89\\t/** A per-hook handler receives a FRESH `ctx` from PI on every call; the\\n90\\t *  connector must never retain it across calls (PI replaces session objects on\\n91\\t *  new/resume/fork and a stale reference throws). Tech design Flow 1. */\\n92\\texport type PiHookHandler<N extends PiHookName> = (\\n93\\t  ctx: ExtensionContext,\\n94\\t  event: PiHookEventMap[N],\\n95\\t) => void | Promise<void>;\\n96\\t\\n97\\t// ── Registration-time API (the `pi` object, ExtensionAPI) ────────────\\n98\\t\\n99\\texport type PiCommandHandler = (args: string[], ctx: ExtensionContext) => void | Promise<void>;\\n100\\t\\n101\\texport interface PiToolSpec {\\n102\\t  name: string;\\n103\\t  description?: string;\\n104\\t  promptSnippet?: string;\\n105\\t  promptGuidelines?: string;\\n106\\t  run: (input: unknown, ctx: ExtensionContext) => unknown | Promise<unknown>;\\n107\\t}\\n108\\t\\n109\\t/** The factory-time API. Hook registration and command/tool registration take\\n110\\t *  `pi: ExtensionAPI`; per-hook handlers take the fresh `ctx` (tech design\\n111\\t *  I-7). `registerCommand`/`registerTool`/`appendEntry` are declared because PI\\n112\\t *  exposes them, but Epic 1 (observe-only) registers only hooks. */\\n113\\texport interface ExtensionAPI {\\n114\\t  registerHook<N extends PiHookName>(name: N, handler: PiHookHandler<N>): void;\\n115\\t  registerCommand(name: string, handler: PiCommandHandler): void;\\n116\\t  registerTool(tool: PiToolSpec): void;\\n117\\t  appendEntry(customType: string, data: unknown): void;\\n118\\t}\\n119\\t\\n120\\t// ── Per-hook context (the `ctx` object, ExtensionContext) ────────────\\n121\\t\\n122\\texport interface ModelDescriptor {\\n123\\t  provider: string;\\n124\\t  id: string;\\n125\\t}\\n126\\t\\n127\\t/** Opaque handle the model registry returns; it carries auth/runtime detail PI\\n128\\t *  owns. The connector treats provider/model as opaque routing keys. */\\n129\\texport interface ModelHandle extends ModelDescriptor {\\n130\\t  readonly [k: string]: unknown;\\n131\\t}\\n132\\t\\n133\\texport interface ModelRegistry {\\n134\\t  find(provider: string, model: string): ModelHandle | undefined;\\n135\\t  hasConfiguredAuth(model: ModelHandle | string): boolean;\\n136\\t  getAvailable(): ModelHandle[];\\n137\\t}\\n138\\t\\n139\\texport interface ExtensionUI {\\n140\\t  notify(message: string, options?: { level?: \\\"info\\\" | \\\"warn\\\" | \\\"error\\\" }): void;\\n141\\t}\\n142\\t\\n143\\texport interface SessionEntry {\\n144\\t  type: string;\\n145\\t  readonly [k: string]: unknown;\\n146\\t}\\n147\\t\\n148\\texport interface SessionManager {\\n149\\t  getEntries(): SessionEntry[];\\n150\\t}\\n151\\t\\n152\\texport interface ExtensionContext {\\n153\\t  modelRegistry: ModelRegistry;\\n154\\t  ui: ExtensionUI;\\n155\\t  /** Guard UI surfacing on this — the extension must work in rpc/json/print\\n156\\t   *  modes too (tech design I-9, reporting guards on headless not on ctx.ui). */\\n157\\t  hasUI: boolean;\\n158\\t  sessionManager: SessionManager;\\n159\\t  cwd: string;\\n160\\t}\\n161\\t\\n162\\t// ── Message vocabulary (AgentMessage and its parts) ──────────────────\\n163\\t\\n164\\texport interface TextPart {\\n165\\t  type: \\\"text\\\";\\n166\\t  text: string;\\n167\\t}\\n168\\texport interface ThinkingPart {\\n169\\t  type: \\\"thinking\\\";\\n170\\t  thinking: string;\\n171\\t  /** Opaque provider reasoning token — captured, not interpreted; dropped in\\n172\\t   *  Epic 1 (tech design I-3). */\\n173\\t  thinkingSignature?: string;\\n174\\t}\\n175\\texport interface ToolCallPart {\\n176\\t  type: \\\"toolCall\\\";\\n177\\t  /** Composite, stable across tool_execution_start → tool_result → message_end\\n178\\t   *  (research §5a): `call_<providerId>|fc_<internalId>`. */\\n179\\t  id: string;\\n180\\t  name: string;\\n181\\t  arguments: Record<string, unknown>;\\n182\\t  /** Opaque; captured, not interpreted (tech design I-3). */\\n183\\t  thoughtSignature?: string;\\n184\\t}\\n185\\t/** Unsupported in the text-only intake schema — degrades to a runtime_note in\\n186\\t *  the converter (tech design I-6), never silently dropped. */\\n187\\texport interface ImagePart {\\n188\\t  type: \\\"image\\\";\\n189\\t  mimeType?: string;\\n190\\t  data?: string;\\n191\\t}\\n192\\texport interface FileRefPart {\\n193\\t  type: \\\"fileRef\\\";\\n194\\t  path: string;\\n195\\t}\\n196\\texport type ContentPart = TextPart | ThinkingPart | ToolCallPart | ImagePart | FileRefPart;\\n197\\t\\n198\\texport type PiStopReason = \\\"stop\\\" | \\\"aborted\\\" | \\\"length\\\" | \\\"toolUse\\\" | (string & {});\\n199\\t\\n200\\texport interface TokenUsage {\\n201\\t  inputTokens?: number;\\n202\\t  outputTokens?: number;\\n203\\t}\\n204\\t\\n205\\texport interface UserMessage {\\n206\\t  role: \\\"user\\\";\\n207\\t  content: ContentPart[];\\n208\\t}\\n209\\t/** Also the shape pi-ai's `complete()` returns (research §3); the model-call\\n210\\t *  host maps it to text or a classified failure (Epic 1 Story 5). */\\n211\\texport interface AssistantMessage {\\n212\\t  role: \\\"assistant\\\";\\n213\\t  content: ContentPart[];\\n214\\t  stopReason?: PiStopReason;\\n215\\t  usage?: TokenUsage;\\n216\\t}\\n217\\texport interface ToolResultMessage {\\n218\\t  role: \\\"toolResult\\\";\\n219\\t  toolCallId: string;\\n220\\t  content: ContentPart[];\\n221\\t  isError?: boolean;\\n222\\t}\\n223\\texport type AgentMessage = UserMessage | AssistantMessage | ToolResultMessage;\\n224\\t\"}]},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"3bd99b80-3187-4d52-9c3e-b6b2c0cfc11e\",\"uuid\":\"b757bd1d-9d25-48b7-8303-3f1e0915eb1e\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-06-14T04:03:59.605Z\",\"tool_use_result\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"file\":{\"filePath\":\"/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/pi-lhc/src/pi/types.ts\",\"content\":\"// Local declarations of the PI extension surface the connector consumes.\\n//\\n// These mirror `@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent` v0.79.2 as verified by the\\n// wiring research (docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md,\\n// headless + interactive recon, June 12 2026). The PI packages are not yet a\\n// build-time dependency of this workspace, so the connector declares the slice\\n// of their contract it touches and swaps to the real imports when the\\n// dependency lands (Spec Deviation, recorded in the story payload). Keeping the\\n// surface here — rather than scattered `any` — means later stories get real\\n// type-checking against the contract PI actually fires.\\n\\n// ── Hook vocabulary ──────────────────────────────────────────────────\\n\\n/** Hooks the connector knows about. Epic 1 registers the observe-only rail;\\n *  `context` is fired by PI but Epic 1 registers no handler for it — context\\n *  serving is Epic 2. */\\nexport type PiHookName =\\n  | \\\"session_start\\\"\\n  | \\\"message_end\\\"\\n  | \\\"turn_end\\\"\\n  | \\\"agent_end\\\"\\n  | \\\"model_select\\\"\\n  | \\\"thinking_level_select\\\"\\n  | \\\"session_before_fork\\\"\\n  | \\\"session_before_switch\\\"\\n  | \\\"session_shutdown\\\"\\n  | \\\"context\\\";\\n\\nexport type SessionStartReason = \\\"startup\\\" | \\\"reload\\\" | \\\"new\\\" | \\\"resume\\\" | \\\"fork\\\";\\nexport type SessionShutdownReason = \\\"shutdown\\\" | \\\"reload\\\" | \\\"new\\\" | \\\"resume\\\";\\n\\nexport interface SessionStartEvent {\\n  reason: SessionStartReason;\\n  previousSessionFile?: string;\\n}\\nexport interface MessageEndEvent {\\n  message: AgentMessage;\\n}\\n/** PI fires one turn_end per agent STEP (turnIndex resets to 0 each run); the\\n *  converter must NOT map it 1:1 to an LHC turn_end (tech design Flow 2). */\\nexport interface TurnEndEvent {\\n  turnIndex: number;\\n  message: AgentMessage;\\n  toolResults: ToolResultMessage[];\\n  stopReason?: PiStopReason;\\n}\\nexport interface AgentEndEvent {\\n  messages: AgentMessage[];\\n}\\nexport interface ModelSelectEvent {\\n  model: ModelDescriptor;\\n  previousModel?: ModelDescriptor;\\n  source?: string;\\n}\\nexport interface ThinkingLevelSelectEvent {\\n  level: string;\\n  previousLevel: string;\\n}\\nexport interface SessionBeforeForkEvent {\\n  entryId: string;\\n  position?: number;\\n}\\nexport interface SessionBeforeSwitchEvent {\\n  intent: \\\"new\\\" | \\\"resume\\\";\\n}\\nexport interface SessionShutdownEvent {\\n  reason: SessionShutdownReason;\\n  targetSessionFile?: string;\\n}\\n/** Declared for completeness; Epic 1 registers no handler (observe-only). */\\nexport interface ContextEvent {\\n  messages: AgentMessage[];\\n}\\n\\n/** Maps each hook to the payload PI delivers with it. */\\nexport interface PiHookEventMap {\\n  session_start: SessionStartEvent;\\n  message_end: MessageEndEvent;\\n  turn_end: TurnEndEvent;\\n  agent_end: AgentEndEvent;\\n  model_select: ModelSelectEvent;\\n  thinking_level_select: ThinkingLevelSelectEvent;\\n  session_before_fork: SessionBeforeForkEvent;\\n  session_before_switch: SessionBeforeSwitchEvent;\\n  session_shutdown: SessionShutdownEvent;\\n  context: ContextEvent;\\n}\\n\\n/** A per-hook handler receives a FRESH `ctx` from PI on every call; the\\n *  connector must never retain it across calls (PI replaces session objects on\\n *  new/resume/fork and a stale reference throws). Tech design Flow 1. */\\nexport type PiHookHandler<N extends PiHookName> = (\\n  ctx: ExtensionContext,\\n  event: PiHookEventMap[N],\\n) => void | Promise<void>;\\n\\n// ── Registration-time API (the `pi` object, ExtensionAPI) ────────────\\n\\nexport type PiCommandHandler = (args: string[], ctx: ExtensionContext) => void | Promise<void>;\\n\\nexport interface PiToolSpec {\\n  name: string;\\n  description?: string;\\n  promptSnippet?: string;\\n  promptGuidelines?: string;\\n  run: (input: unknown, ctx: ExtensionContext) => unknown | Promise<unknown>;\\n}\\n\\n/** The factory-time API. Hook registration and command/tool registration take\\n *  `pi: ExtensionAPI`; per-hook handlers take the fresh `ctx` (tech design\\n *  I-7). `registerCommand`/`registerTool`/`appendEntry` are declared because PI\\n *  exposes them, but Epic 1 (observe-only) registers only hooks. */\\nexport interface ExtensionAPI {\\n  registerHook<N extends PiHookName>(name: N, handler: PiHookHandler<N>): void;\\n  registerCommand(name: string, handler: PiCommandHandler): void;\\n  registerTool(tool: PiToolSpec): void;\\n  appendEntry(customType: string, data: unknown): void;\\n}\\n\\n// ── Per-hook context (the `ctx` object, ExtensionContext) ────────────\\n\\nexport interface ModelDescriptor {\\n  provider: string;\\n  id: string;\\n}\\n\\n/** Opaque handle the model registry returns; it carries auth/runtime detail PI\\n *  owns. The connector treats provider/model as opaque routing keys. */\\nexport interface ModelHandle extends ModelDescriptor {\\n  readonly [k: string]: unknown;\\n}\\n\\nexport interface ModelRegistry {\\n  find(provider: string, model: string): ModelHandle | undefined;\\n  hasConfiguredAuth(model: ModelHandle | string): boolean;\\n  getAvailable(): ModelHandle[];\\n}\\n\\nexport interface ExtensionUI {\\n  notify(message: string, options?: { level?: \\\"info\\\" | \\\"warn\\\" | \\\"error\\\" }): void;\\n}\\n\\nexport interface SessionEntry {\\n  type: string;\\n  readonly [k: string]: unknown;\\n}\\n\\nexport interface SessionManager {\\n  getEntries(): SessionEntry[];\\n}\\n\\nexport interface ExtensionContext {\\n  modelRegistry: ModelRegistry;\\n  ui: ExtensionUI;\\n  /** Guard UI surfacing on this — the extension must work in rpc/json/print\\n   *  modes too (tech design I-9, reporting guards on headless not on ctx.ui). */\\n  hasUI: boolean;\\n  sessionManager: SessionManager;\\n  cwd: string;\\n}\\n\\n// ── Message vocabulary (AgentMessage and its parts) ──────────────────\\n\\nexport interface TextPart {\\n  type: \\\"text\\\";\\n  text: string;\\n}\\nexport interface ThinkingPart {\\n  type: \\\"thinking\\\";\\n  thinking: string;\\n  /** Opaque provider reasoning token — captured, not interpreted; dropped in\\n   *  Epic 1 (tech design I-3). */\\n  thinkingSignature?: string;\\n}\\nexport interface ToolCallPart {\\n  type: \\\"toolCall\\\";\\n  /** Composite, stable across tool_execution_start → tool_result → message_end\\n   *  (research §5a): `call_<providerId>|fc_<internalId>`. */\\n  id: string;\\n  name: string;\\n  arguments: Record<string, unknown>;\\n  /** Opaque; captured, not interpreted (tech design I-3). */\\n  thoughtSignature?: string;\\n}\\n/** Unsupported in the text-only intake schema — degrades to a runtime_note in\\n *  the converter (tech design I-6), never silently dropped. */\\nexport interface ImagePart {\\n  type: \\\"image\\\";\\n  mimeType?: string;\\n  data?: string;\\n}\\nexport interface FileRefPart {\\n  type: \\\"fileRef\\\";\\n  path: string;\\n}\\nexport type ContentPart = TextPart | ThinkingPart | ToolCallPart | ImagePart | FileRefPart;\\n\\nexport type PiStopReason = \\\"stop\\\" | \\\"aborted\\\" | \\\"length\\\" | \\\"toolUse\\\" | (string & {});\\n\\nexport interface TokenUsage {\\n  inputTokens?: number;\\n  outputTokens?: number;\\n}\\n\\nexport interface UserMessage {\\n  role: \\\"user\\\";\\n  content: ContentPart[];\\n}\\n/** Also the shape pi-ai's `complete()` returns (research §3); the model-call\\n *  host maps it to text or a classified failure (Epic 1 Story 5). */\\nexport interface AssistantMessage {\\n  role: \\\"assistant\\\";\\n  content: ContentPart[];\\n  stopReason?: PiStopReason;\\n  usage?: TokenUsage;\\n}\\nexport interface ToolResultMessage {\\n  role: \\\"toolResult\\\";\\n  toolCallId: string;\\n  content: ContentPart[];\\n  isError?: boolean;\\n}\\nexport type AgentMessage = UserMessage | AssistantMessage | ToolResultMessage;\\n\",\"numLines\":224,\"startLine\":1,\"totalLines\":224}}}\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/artifacts/01-session-lifecycle-and-thread-resolution/streams/003-implementor.stdout.log:685:{\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_01KmfcpVn9kNztMDKLJV3wNh\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"=== integration research notes (launch flags / session_start) ===\\n15:| `session_start` | `reason: \\\"startup\\\"\\\\|\\\"reload\\\"\\\\|\\\"new\\\"\\\\|\\\"resume\\\"\\\\|\\\"fork\\\"`, `previousSessionFile` | Resolve/create thread, hydrate state, startup validation surfacing |\\n20:| `session_shutdown` | `reason`, `targetSessionFile` | Flush/cleanup |\\n32:**Extension loading:** `~/.pi/agent/extensions/` (global), `.pi/extensions/` (project), or npm package via `settings.json` `packages` array — that's the publish-under-its-own-name path. Hot reload via `/reload` re-fires `session_shutdown` → `session_start{reason:\\\"reload\\\"}`.\\n35:- Session replacement (`/new`, `/resume`, `/fork`): old extension instance is torn down; captured `ctx`/`sessionManager` objects go stale and throw. The POC is littered with `isStalePiContextError` fallback handling because it captures contexts — the new extension should hold *only* plain data (thread id, file path) across events and let each handler use its own fresh `ctx`.\\n92:1. **Thread lifecycle** — PI session ↔ LHC thread mapping (persisted via `appendEntry` in the session file, or a registry sidecar like the POC's `threadId-map.json` — decision needed); create on first event; reattach on resume/reload; handle fork (§6).\\n93:2. **Intake** — message/turn event capture, mapped and batched, ordering preserved, idempotent across replays (PI resume re-fires nothing, but reload/fork paths need the keys).\\n110:Drove published PI 0.79.2 headlessly (`pi --print --mode json -e log-ext.ts`) against gpt-5.4 with a log-every-hook extension, across chatty / tool-heavy / error / resume scenarios. This answers the behavioral questions static types couldn't, and de-risks most of Epic 1's capture contract before M0. Scratch rig: `scratch-pi-recon/`.\\n114:session_start{reason} → before_agent_start{prompt,images} → agent_start\\n130:- **Resume/dedup — the big one.** Reopening an existing session (`--session-id` of a prior session) does **NOT** re-fire historical `message_end` events. Only the new turn's events fire (verified: 4 message events, not 10). PI loads prior history internally and exposes it through the **`context` hook** (first context on resume already held all 7 prior messages). **Consequence: LHC intake never sees duplicate historical events on resume — the dedup concern is far smaller than feared.** Idempotency keys still matter for `/reload` and crash-replay, but normal resume is clean by construction.\\n131:- **Assistant message composition.** Confirmed `[thinking, text]`, `[thinking, toolCall]`, `[toolCall×N]` all occur; thinking carries an encrypted `thinkingSignature` (provider reasoning token — opaque, store-or-drop is an LHC call). The fan-out order in one message is thinking → text → toolCalls.\\n132:- **session_start reason.** `\\\"startup\\\"` on fresh and on resume alike in print mode; `previousSessionFile` was absent. The reason-code taxonomy (`new/resume/reload/fork`) from the docs may surface differently in interactive vs print mode — worth confirming interactively, but not blocking.\\n134:**Fork (`--fork`), captured solo:** forking a session creates a **new** session file (UUID-named); the original is untouched. The fork inherited all prior history via the `context` hook (11 messages present). In print mode `session_start` still reported `reason=startup` and carried no fork lineage in the payload — the lineage lives in the session file's `parentId` chain (below), not the event. `session_before_fork` did not fire in print-mode `--fork` (likely an interactive-command hook). **Fork-as-new-thread (the PRD decision) matches PI's own behavior — PI already makes a new file.**\\n139:- First line: `{type:\\\"session\\\", version:3, id, timestamp, cwd}`.\\n149:- **Graceful interrupt (Esc):** the partial assistant message gets a normal `message_end` with content preserved (`[thinking, text]`, ~120 lines kept) and **`stopReason: \\\"aborted\\\"`**; `turn_end` fires, also `stopReason: \\\"aborted\\\"`; `agent_end` fires. A graceful abort is a *complete, well-formed turn marked aborted* — partial content is real and worth keeping. The session continues normally on the next prompt (verified: follow-up ran `stopReason: \\\"stop\\\"`, no recovery path).\\n151:- Mapper carries `stopReason` through; LHC distinguishes \\\"aborted-but-complete\\\" from \\\"crashed-open.\\\"\\n156:| Transition | Sequence | `session_start.reason` | `previousSessionFile` |\\n160:| `/resume` | `session_before_switch{resume}` → `session_shutdown{resume}` → re-init → `session_start{resume}` | `resume` | populated (left session) |\\n161:- **`session_before_switch`** is the pre-transition hook (fires for new/resume, not reload) — where the extension disposes the old LHC instance and flushes before the swap.\\n162:- **Reload wipes extension in-memory state** (`_loaded` re-fires) but continues the same session — so the thread mapping must reconstruct from the session file, not closures (confirms the PRD extension-state-discipline decision with evidence). `reason: \\\"reload\\\"` lets Lifecycle detect-and-reattach rather than create.\\n163:- **Resume re-fires zero `message_end` events** (confirmed interactively, matching §5a) — history served via `context` only. Dedup is needed for `/reload` + crash-replay, not normal resume.\\n182:5. **What resume actually replays.** ANSWERED (§5a): resume does not re-fire history; PI serves prior context through the `context` hook. Aborts and the context-hook mid-turn view still need the recording period.\\n191:**Epic A — Connector core (intake + lifecycle + inference host):** ~5–6 stories. SDK construction/teardown on session lifecycle, thread mapping + resume/reload/fork handling, message/turn event mapper (converter productionized), `ModelCall` + assignments config + startup validation, ordering/idempotency proof against recorded corpora.\",\"is_error\":false}]},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"3bd99b80-3187-4d52-9c3e-b6b2c0cfc11e\",\"uuid\":\"e29ddcbc-b475-4ae5-a569-e1d21542da31\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-06-14T04:08:37.666Z\",\"tool_use_result\":{\"stdout\":\"=== integration research notes (launch flags / session_start) ===\\n15:| `session_start` | `reason: \\\"startup\\\"\\\\|\\\"reload\\\"\\\\|\\\"new\\\"\\\\|\\\"resume\\\"\\\\|\\\"fork\\\"`, `previousSessionFile` | Resolve/create thread, hydrate state, startup validation surfacing |\\n20:| `session_shutdown` | `reason`, `targetSessionFile` | Flush/cleanup |\\n32:**Extension loading:** `~/.pi/agent/extensions/` (global), `.pi/extensions/` (project), or npm package via `settings.json` `packages` array — that's the publish-under-its-own-name path. Hot reload via `/reload` re-fires `session_shutdown` → `session_start{reason:\\\"reload\\\"}`.\\n35:- Session replacement (`/new`, `/resume`, `/fork`): old extension instance is torn down; captured `ctx`/`sessionManager` objects go stale and throw. The POC is littered with `isStalePiContextError` fallback handling because it captures contexts — the new extension should hold *only* plain data (thread id, file path) across events and let each handler use its own fresh `ctx`.\\n92:1. **Thread lifecycle** — PI session ↔ LHC thread mapping (persisted via `appendEntry` in the session file, or a registry sidecar like the POC's `threadId-map.json` — decision needed); create on first event; reattach on resume/reload; handle fork (§6).\\n93:2. **Intake** — message/turn event capture, mapped and batched, ordering preserved, idempotent across replays (PI resume re-fires nothing, but reload/fork paths need the keys).\\n110:Drove published PI 0.79.2 headlessly (`pi --print --mode json -e log-ext.ts`) against gpt-5.4 with a log-every-hook extension, across chatty / tool-heavy / error / resume scenarios. This answers the behavioral questions static types couldn't, and de-risks most of Epic 1's capture contract before M0. Scratch rig: `scratch-pi-recon/`.\\n114:session_start{reason} → before_agent_start{prompt,images} → agent_start\\n130:- **Resume/dedup — the big one.** Reopening an existing session (`--session-id` of a prior session) does **NOT** re-fire historical `message_end` events. Only the new turn's events fire (verified: 4 message events, not 10). PI loads prior history internally and exposes it through the **`context` hook** (first context on resume already held all 7 prior messages). **Consequence: LHC intake never sees duplicate historical events on resume — the dedup concern is far smaller than feared.** Idempotency keys still matter for `/reload` and crash-replay, but normal resume is clean by construction.\\n131:- **Assistant message composition.** Confirmed `[thinking, text]`, `[thinking, toolCall]`, `[toolCall×N]` all occur; thinking carries an encrypted `thinkingSignature` (provider reasoning token — opaque, store-or-drop is an LHC call). The fan-out order in one message is thinking → text → toolCalls.\\n132:- **session_start reason.** `\\\"startup\\\"` on fresh and on resume alike in print mode; `previousSessionFile` was absent. The reason-code taxonomy (`new/resume/reload/fork`) from the docs may surface differently in interactive vs print mode — worth confirming interactively, but not blocking.\\n134:**Fork (`--fork`), captured solo:** forking a session creates a **new** session file (UUID-named); the original is untouched. The fork inherited all prior history via the `context` hook (11 messages present). In print mode `session_start` still reported `reason=startup` and carried no fork lineage in the payload — the lineage lives in the session file's `parentId` chain (below), not the event. `session_before_fork` did not fire in print-mode `--fork` (likely an interactive-command hook). **Fork-as-new-thread (the PRD decision) matches PI's own behavior — PI already makes a new file.**\\n139:- First line: `{type:\\\"session\\\", version:3, id, timestamp, cwd}`.\\n149:- **Graceful interrupt (Esc):** the partial assistant message gets a normal `message_end` with content preserved (`[thinking, text]`, ~120 lines kept) and **`stopReason: \\\"aborted\\\"`**; `turn_end` fires, also `stopReason: \\\"aborted\\\"`; `agent_end` fires. A graceful abort is a *complete, well-formed turn marked aborted* — partial content is real and worth keeping. The session continues normally on the next prompt (verified: follow-up ran `stopReason: \\\"stop\\\"`, no recovery path).\\n151:- Mapper carries `stopReason` through; LHC distinguishes \\\"aborted-but-complete\\\" from \\\"crashed-open.\\\"\\n156:| Transition | Sequence | `session_start.reason` | `previousSessionFile` |\\n160:| `/resume` | `session_before_switch{resume}` → `session_shutdown{resume}` → re-init → `session_start{resume}` | `resume` | populated (left session) |\\n161:- **`session_before_switch`** is the pre-transition hook (fires for new/resume, not reload) — where the extension disposes the old LHC instance and flushes before the swap.\\n162:- **Reload wipes extension in-memory state** (`_loaded` re-fires) but continues the same session — so the thread mapping must reconstruct from the session file, not closures (confirms the PRD extension-state-discipline decision with evidence). `reason: \\\"reload\\\"` lets Lifecycle detect-and-reattach rather than create.\\n163:- **Resume re-fires zero `message_end` events** (confirmed interactively, matching §5a) — history served via `context` only. Dedup is needed for `/reload` + crash-replay, not normal resume.\\n182:5. **What resume actually replays.** ANSWERED (§5a): resume does not re-fire history; PI serves prior context through the `context` hook. Aborts and the context-hook mid-turn view still need the recording period.\\n191:**Epic A — Connector core (intake + lifecycle + inference host):** ~5–6 stories. SDK construction/teardown on session lifecycle, thread mapping + resume/reload/fork handling, message/turn event mapper (converter productionized), `ModelCall` + assignments config + startup validation, ordering/idempotency proof against recorded corpora.\",\"stderr\":\"\",\"interrupted\":false,\"isImage\":false,\"noOutputExpected\":false}}\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/artifacts/01-session-lifecycle-and-thread-resolution/streams/003-implementor.stdout.log:699:{\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_017BJttBzkdEmVK7dov3ssyd\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"1\\timport { describe, expect, it } from \\\"vitest\\\";\\n2\\timport { activate, createConnector, disposeInstance, EPIC_1_HOOKS, initInstance } from \\\"../../src/index.js\\\";\\n3\\timport { createSessionState } from \\\"../../src/lifecycle/state.js\\\";\\n4\\timport type { ExtensionAPI, ExtensionContext, PiHookName } from \\\"../../src/pi/types.js\\\";\\n5\\timport { makeAgentEnd, makeMessageEnd, makeSessionStart, makeUserMessage } from \\\"../fixtures/synthetic.js\\\";\\n6\\t\\n7\\t// A fake ExtensionAPI that records what the extension registers.\\n8\\tfunction recordingPi(): {\\n9\\t  pi: ExtensionAPI;\\n10\\t  registered: PiHookName[];\\n11\\t  commands: string[];\\n12\\t  tools: string[];\\n13\\t} {\\n14\\t  const registered: PiHookName[] = [];\\n15\\t  const commands: string[] = [];\\n16\\t  const tools: string[] = [];\\n17\\t  const pi: ExtensionAPI = {\\n18\\t    registerHook(name) {\\n19\\t      registered.push(name);\\n20\\t    },\\n21\\t    registerCommand(name) {\\n22\\t      commands.push(name);\\n23\\t    },\\n24\\t    registerTool(tool) {\\n25\\t      tools.push(tool.name);\\n26\\t    },\\n27\\t    appendEntry() {},\\n28\\t  };\\n29\\t  return { pi, registered, commands, tools };\\n30\\t}\\n31\\t\\n32\\t// A synthetic per-hook ctx carrying METHODS (not plain data) and a unique\\n33\\t// marker — so a retained ctx would break the structuredClone plain-data guard.\\n34\\tfunction syntheticCtx(marker: string): ExtensionContext {\\n35\\t  return {\\n36\\t    cwd: `/tmp/${marker}`,\\n37\\t    hasUI: false,\\n38\\t    modelRegistry: {\\n39\\t      find: () => undefined,\\n40\\t      hasConfiguredAuth: () => false,\\n41\\t      getAvailable: () => [],\\n42\\t    },\\n43\\t    ui: { notify: () => {} },\\n44\\t    sessionManager: { getEntries: () => [] },\\n45\\t  };\\n46\\t}\\n47\\t\\n48\\tdescribe(\\\"extension load + hook rail\\\", () => {\\n49\\t  it(\\\"registers exactly the Epic 1 observe-only hook rail and not the context hook\\\", () => {\\n50\\t    const { pi, registered, commands, tools } = recordingPi();\\n51\\t    activate(pi);\\n52\\t\\n53\\t    expect(new Set(registered)).toEqual(new Set(EPIC_1_HOOKS));\\n54\\t    expect(registered).toHaveLength(9);\\n55\\t    expect(registered).not.toContain(\\\"context\\\"); // context serving is Epic 2\\n56\\t    // observe-only: no operator commands / agent tools registered yet (Epic 3)\\n57\\t    expect(commands).toEqual([]);\\n58\\t    expect(tools).toEqual([]);\\n59\\t  });\\n60\\t\\n61\\t  it(\\\"routes every hook to a guarded handler that never throws into PI and retains only plain data\\\", async () => {\\n62\\t    const connector = createConnector();\\n63\\t    // fire several hooks with DISTINCT ctx objects carrying methods\\n64\\t    await connector.handlers.session_start(syntheticCtx(\\\"ctx-1\\\"), makeSessionStart(\\\"startup\\\"));\\n65\\t    await connector.handlers.message_end(syntheticCtx(\\\"ctx-2\\\"), makeMessageEnd(makeUserMessage(\\\"hi\\\")));\\n66\\t    await connector.handlers.agent_end(syntheticCtx(\\\"ctx-3\\\"), makeAgentEnd([]));\\n67\\t\\n68\\t    // (the awaits above completing is the \\\"never throws into PI\\\" assertion)\\n69\\t    // retained state is plain data: structuredClone throws on a stored PI ctx (it has methods)\\n70\\t    expect(() => structuredClone(connector.snapshot())).not.toThrow();\\n71\\t  });\\n72\\t\\n73\\t  it(\\\"keeps SessionState plain-data-only — it survives structuredClone with every field populated\\\", () => {\\n74\\t    const state = createSessionState({ filePath: \\\"/tmp/thread.sqlite\\\" });\\n75\\t    state.flags.startupValidationReported = true;\\n76\\t    state.flags.toldUserAboutGap = true; // open index signature, still plain data\\n77\\t    state.health.lastCaptureFailure = { code: \\\"malformed_event\\\", message: \\\"bad shape\\\", recordedGap: true };\\n78\\t    state.health.startupValidation = { unreachable: [] };\\n79\\t\\n80\\t    const clone = structuredClone(state);\\n81\\t    expect(clone).toEqual(state);\\n82\\t    expect(clone).not.toBe(state);\\n83\\t  });\\n84\\t\\n85\\t  it(\\\"exposes a stubbed init/dispose seam that returns a typed fail-closed result\\\", async () => {\\n86\\t    const initResult = await initInstance({ filePath: \\\"/tmp/thread.sqlite\\\" }, { mode: \\\"background\\\" });\\n87\\t    expect(initResult.ok).toBe(false);\\n88\\t    if (!initResult.ok) {\\n89\\t      expect(initResult.error).toEqual({\\n90\\t        errorClass: \\\"system_error\\\",\\n91\\t        code: \\\"not_implemented\\\",\\n92\\t        reason: \\\"lifecycle.initInstance is not implemented yet (Epic 1 foundation stub)\\\",\\n93\\t      });\\n94\\t    }\\n95\\t\\n96\\t    const disposeResult = await disposeInstance(null);\\n97\\t    expect(disposeResult.ok).toBe(false);\\n98\\t    if (!disposeResult.ok) {\\n99\\t      expect(disposeResult.error).toEqual({\\n100\\t        errorClass: \\\"system_error\\\",\\n101\\t        code: \\\"not_implemented\\\",\\n102\\t        reason: \\\"lifecycle.disposeInstance is not implemented yet (Epic 1 foundation stub)\\\",\\n103\\t      });\\n104\\t    }\\n105\\t  });\\n106\\t});\\n107\\t\"}]},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"3bd99b80-3187-4d52-9c3e-b6b2c0cfc11e\",\"uuid\":\"a3966ea6-6869-416b-ab8c-4e847e617766\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-06-14T04:08:39.278Z\",\"tool_use_result\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"file\":{\"filePath\":\"/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/pi-lhc/test/smoke/extension-load.test.ts\",\"content\":\"import { describe, expect, it } from \\\"vitest\\\";\\nimport { activate, createConnector, disposeInstance, EPIC_1_HOOKS, initInstance } from \\\"../../src/index.js\\\";\\nimport { createSessionState } from \\\"../../src/lifecycle/state.js\\\";\\nimport type { ExtensionAPI, ExtensionContext, PiHookName } from \\\"../../src/pi/types.js\\\";\\nimport { makeAgentEnd, makeMessageEnd, makeSessionStart, makeUserMessage } from \\\"../fixtures/synthetic.js\\\";\\n\\n// A fake ExtensionAPI that records what the extension registers.\\nfunction recordingPi(): {\\n  pi: ExtensionAPI;\\n  registered: PiHookName[];\\n  commands: string[];\\n  tools: string[];\\n} {\\n  const registered: PiHookName[] = [];\\n  const commands: string[] = [];\\n  const tools: string[] = [];\\n  const pi: ExtensionAPI = {\\n    registerHook(name) {\\n      registered.push(name);\\n    },\\n    registerCommand(name) {\\n      commands.push(name);\\n    },\\n    registerTool(tool) {\\n      tools.push(tool.name);\\n    },\\n    appendEntry() {},\\n  };\\n  return { pi, registered, commands, tools };\\n}\\n\\n// A synthetic per-hook ctx carrying METHODS (not plain data) and a unique\\n// marker — so a retained ctx would break the structuredClone plain-data guard.\\nfunction syntheticCtx(marker: string): ExtensionContext {\\n  return {\\n    cwd: `/tmp/${marker}`,\\n    hasUI: false,\\n    modelRegistry: {\\n      find: () => undefined,\\n      hasConfiguredAuth: () => false,\\n      getAvailable: () => [],\\n    },\\n    ui: { notify: () => {} },\\n    sessionManager: { getEntries: () => [] },\\n  };\\n}\\n\\ndescribe(\\\"extension load + hook rail\\\", () => {\\n  it(\\\"registers exactly the Epic 1 observe-only hook rail and not the context hook\\\", () => {\\n    const { pi, registered, commands, tools } = recordingPi();\\n    activate(pi);\\n\\n    expect(new Set(registered)).toEqual(new Set(EPIC_1_HOOKS));\\n    expect(registered).toHaveLength(9);\\n    expect(registered).not.toContain(\\\"context\\\"); // context serving is Epic 2\\n    // observe-only: no operator commands / agent tools registered yet (Epic 3)\\n    expect(commands).toEqual([]);\\n    expect(tools).toEqual([]);\\n  });\\n\\n  it(\\\"routes every hook to a guarded handler that never throws into PI and retains only plain data\\\", async () => {\\n    const connector = createConnector();\\n    // fire several hooks with DISTINCT ctx objects carrying methods\\n    await connector.handlers.session_start(syntheticCtx(\\\"ctx-1\\\"), makeSessionStart(\\\"startup\\\"));\\n    await connector.handlers.message_end(syntheticCtx(\\\"ctx-2\\\"), makeMessageEnd(makeUserMessage(\\\"hi\\\")));\\n    await connector.handlers.agent_end(syntheticCtx(\\\"ctx-3\\\"), makeAgentEnd([]));\\n\\n    // (the awaits above completing is the \\\"never throws into PI\\\" assertion)\\n    // retained state is plain data: structuredClone throws on a stored PI ctx (it has methods)\\n    expect(() => structuredClone(connector.snapshot())).not.toThrow();\\n  });\\n\\n  it(\\\"keeps SessionState plain-data-only — it survives structuredClone with every field populated\\\", () => {\\n    const state = createSessionState({ filePath: \\\"/tmp/thread.sqlite\\\" });\\n    state.flags.startupValidationReported = true;\\n    state.flags.toldUserAboutGap = true; // open index signature, still plain data\\n    state.health.lastCaptureFailure = { code: \\\"malformed_event\\\", message: \\\"bad shape\\\", recordedGap: true };\\n    state.health.startupValidation = { unreachable: [] };\\n\\n    const clone = structuredClone(state);\\n    expect(clone).toEqual(state);\\n    expect(clone).not.toBe(state);\\n  });\\n\\n  it(\\\"exposes a stubbed init/dispose seam that returns a typed fail-closed result\\\", async () => {\\n    const initResult = await initInstance({ filePath: \\\"/tmp/thread.sqlite\\\" }, { mode: \\\"background\\\" });\\n    expect(initResult.ok).toBe(false);\\n    if (!initResult.ok) {\\n      expect(initResult.error).toEqual({\\n        errorClass: \\\"system_error\\\",\\n        code: \\\"not_implemented\\\",\\n        reason: \\\"lifecycle.initInstance is not implemented yet (Epic 1 foundation stub)\\\",\\n      });\\n    }\\n\\n    const disposeResult = await disposeInstance(null);\\n    expect(disposeResult.ok).toBe(false);\\n    if (!disposeResult.ok) {\\n      expect(disposeResult.error).toEqual({\\n        errorClass: \\\"system_error\\\",\\n        code: \\\"not_implemented\\\",\\n        reason: \\\"lifecycle.disposeInstance is not implemented yet (Epic 1 foundation stub)\\\",\\n      });\\n    }\\n  });\\n});\\n\",\"numLines\":107,\"startLine\":1,\"totalLines\":107}}}\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/artifacts/01-session-lifecycle-and-thread-resolution/streams/003-implementor.stdout.log:3086:{\"type\":\"stream_event\",\"event\":{\"type\":\"content_block_delta\",\"index\":0,\"delta\":{\"type\":\"input_json_delta\",\"partial_json\":\"_HOOKS = [\\\\n  \\\\\\\"session_start\\\\\\\",\\\\n  \\\\\\\"message_end\\\\\\\",\\\\n  \\\\\\\"turn_end\\\\\\\",\\\\n  \\\\\\\"agent_end\\\\\\\",\\\\n  \\\\\\\"model_select\\\\\\\",\\\\n  \\\\\\\"th\"}},\"session_id\":\"3bd99b80-3187-4d52-9c3e-b6b2c0cfc11e\",\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"uuid\":\"57e1c1b0-4cf3-45c8-9af8-edb5ec3359d6\"}\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/artifacts/01-session-lifecycle-and-thread-resolution/streams/003-implementor.stdout.log:3153:{\"type\":\"stream_event\",\"event\":{\"type\":\"content_block_delta\",\"index\":0,\"delta\":{\"type\":\"input_json_delta\",\"partial_json\":\"};\\\\n\\\\n  // Observe-only foundation: capture-bearing hooks (message_end, turn_end,\"}},\"session_id\":\"3bd99b80-3187-4d52-9c3e-b6b2c0cfc11e\",\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"uuid\":\"bdfdcc69-f18b-4391-83d1-f6a02a62b75b\"}\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/artifacts/01-session-lifecycle-and-thread-resolution/streams/003-implementor.stdout.log:3158:{\"type\":\"stream_event\",\"event\":{\"type\":\"content_block_delta\",\"index\":0,\"delta\":{\"type\":\"input_json_delta\",\"partial_json\":\" PiHookHandler<Epic1Hook>,\\\\n    session_before_switch: onDispose,\\\\n    session_shutdown: onDispose,\\\\n    message_end: noop,\\\\n    turn_end: noop,\"}},\"session_id\":\"3bd99b80-3187-4d52-9c3e-b6b2c0cfc11e\",\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"uuid\":\"63ab22a9-407f-422f-b1b8-ca8395eff762\"}\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/artifacts/01-session-lifecycle-and-thread-resolution/streams/003-implementor.stdout.log:3169:{\"type\":\"assistant\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-opus-4-8\",\"id\":\"msg_01L1thhg7edm6LJR3kzjoEje\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"toolu_016CwHShWBADCx8WWmqCHpU1\",\"name\":\"Write\",\"input\":{\"file_path\":\"/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/pi-lhc/src/index.ts\",\"content\":\"// Extension entry. PI loads this module and calls `activate(pi)`; it registers\\n// the Epic 1 observe-only hook rail and routes each hook to a fail-closed\\n// handler. The connector owns only plain-data `SessionState` plus the live\\n// `LhcInstance` it constructs per session — never a PI `ctx`/session object,\\n// which PI replaces on new/resume/fork (tech design Flow 1, plain-data rule).\\nimport { mkdirSync } from \\\"node:fs\\\";\\nimport { homedir } from \\\"node:os\\\";\\nimport { join } from \\\"node:path\\\";\\nimport { randomUUID } from \\\"node:crypto\\\";\\nimport type { OpResult, SdkConfig, ThreadRef } from \\\"lhc\\\";\\nimport type {\\n  ExtensionAPI,\\n  ExtensionContext,\\n  PiHookHandler,\\n  PiHookName,\\n} from \\\"./pi/types.js\\\";\\nimport type { CaptureFailureDiagnostic, SessionState } from \\\"./lifecycle/state.js\\\";\\nimport { createSessionState } from \\\"./lifecycle/state.js\\\";\\nimport { disposeInstance, initInstance } from \\\"./lifecycle/instance.js\\\";\\nimport {\\n  parseLaunchFlags,\\n  resolveThread,\\n  type LaunchFlags,\\n} from \\\"./lifecycle/thread-resolution.js\\\";\\nimport { pickThread, type ThreadChoice } from \\\"./lifecycle/picker.js\\\";\\nimport type { LhcInstance } from \\\"./shared/instance.js\\\";\\n\\nexport { disposeInstance, initInstance, initLhc } from \\\"./lifecycle/instance.js\\\";\\nexport type { LhcInstance } from \\\"./shared/instance.js\\\";\\n\\n/** The Epic 1 observe-only hook rail: every PI hook the connector consumes.\\n *  Deliberately NOT `context` — context serving is Epic 2 and this epic\\n *  registers no handler for it (story Scope Out). */\\nexport const EPIC_1_HOOKS = [\\n  \\\"session_start\\\",\\n  \\\"message_end\\\",\\n  \\\"turn_end\\\",\\n  \\\"agent_end\\\",\\n  \\\"model_select\\\",\\n  \\\"thinking_level_select\\\",\\n  \\\"session_before_fork\\\",\\n  \\\"session_before_switch\\\",\\n  \\\"session_shutdown\\\",\\n] as const satisfies readonly PiHookName[];\\n\\nexport type Epic1Hook = (typeof EPIC_1_HOOKS)[number];\\n\\n/** What the connector needs from the host to run a session. All injectable so\\n *  the lifecycle is exercised against a real temp registry/thread in tests and\\n *  the default `~/.lhc` registry in production.\\n *\\n *  `buildSdkConfig` is the inference seam: it returns the SdkConfig (provider /\\n *  inference + policy) to construct the instance from. In Story 1 the real\\n *  config — the PI-model-registry-backed `ModelCall` and assignments — is not\\n *  wired yet (that is Stories 5/6), so the default fails closed; the connector\\n *  then resolves nothing and records a diagnostic rather than faking a session.\\n */\\nexport interface ConnectorDeps {\\n  buildSdkConfig?: (ctx: ExtensionContext) => OpResult<SdkConfig>;\\n  registryPath?: string;\\n  newThreadFilePath?: () => string;\\n  parseLaunch?: () => LaunchFlags;\\n  selectThread?: (\\n    choices: readonly ThreadChoice[],\\n    ctx: ExtensionContext,\\n  ) => Promise<string | null>;\\n}\\n\\n/** Everything the connector retains across hooks as PLAIN data — by\\n *  construction structuredClone-able. The live `LhcInstance` is held separately\\n *  in the closure (it is the connector's own engine, rebuilt on reload) and is\\n *  deliberately not part of the snapshot. */\\nexport interface ConnectorSnapshot {\\n  state: SessionState | null;\\n  lastDiagnostic: CaptureFailureDiagnostic | null;\\n}\\n\\nexport interface Connector {\\n  /** Register the observe-only hook rail on the PI extension API. */\\n  register(pi: ExtensionAPI): void;\\n  /** The plain-data state retained across hooks. `null` until a session\\n   *  resolves a thread; never holds a PI ctx. */\\n  getState(): SessionState | null;\\n  /** The live LHC instance for the current session, or `null`. A test/inspection\\n   *  seam — held by the connector but never part of the plain-data snapshot. */\\n  getInstance(): LhcInstance | null;\\n  /** Plain-data snapshot of all retained state (inspection/test seam). */\\n  snapshot(): ConnectorSnapshot;\\n  /** The hook handlers, keyed by event — exposed so tests can drive them with\\n   *  synthetic ctx/events without a live PI. */\\n  readonly handlers: Readonly<Record<Epic1Hook, PiHookHandler<Epic1Hook>>>;\\n}\\n\\nconst DEFAULT_LHC_DIR = join(homedir(), \\\".lhc\\\");\\n\\n/** Inference host not wired yet (Story 5/6): fail closed so a session resolves\\n *  nothing and records a diagnostic instead of constructing a faked instance. */\\nfunction inferenceNotConfigured(): OpResult<SdkConfig> {\\n  return {\\n    ok: false,\\n    error: {\\n      errorClass: \\\"system_error\\\",\\n      code: \\\"not_implemented\\\",\\n      reason:\\n        \\\"pi-lhc inference host not wired yet (Epic 1 Story 5); SDK construction deferred\\\",\\n    },\\n  };\\n}\\n\\n/** Default new-thread file location: `~/.lhc/threads/<uuid>.sqlite`. The id is\\n *  generated by the registry at creation, so the file name is just a unique\\n *  handle; the directory is ensured up front (DatabaseSync does not create\\n *  parents). */\\nfunction defaultNewThreadFilePath(): string {\\n  const dir = join(DEFAULT_LHC_DIR, \\\"threads\\\");\\n  mkdirSync(dir, { recursive: true });\\n  return join(dir, `${randomUUID()}.sqlite`);\\n}\\n\\nfunction threadIdOf(ref: ThreadRef): string | null {\\n  return \\\"threadId\\\" in ref ? ref.threadId : null;\\n}\\n\\n/** Build a connector instance. Its mutable state lives in this closure; the\\n *  registered handlers capture it, so the instance stays live for the session\\n *  even when `activate` discards the returned object. */\\nexport function createConnector(deps: ConnectorDeps = {}): Connector {\\n  // The connector's retained state. `state` is plain data (the durable thread\\n  // reference + flags); `instance` is the live SDK engine (rebuilt on reload);\\n  // `rememberedThreadId` is the durable id the reload path reattaches by; and\\n  // `lastDiagnostic` is the plain-data health signal. None is a PI object.\\n  let state: SessionState | null = null;\\n  let instance: LhcInstance | null = null;\\n  let rememberedThreadId: string | null = null;\\n  let lastDiagnostic: CaptureFailureDiagnostic | null = null;\\n\\n  const buildSdkConfig = deps.buildSdkConfig ?? (() => inferenceNotConfigured());\\n  const newThreadFilePath = deps.newThreadFilePath ?? defaultNewThreadFilePath;\\n  const parseLaunch = deps.parseLaunch ?? (() => parseLaunchFlags(process.argv));\\n  const selectThread =\\n    deps.selectThread ?? ((): Promise<string | null> => Promise.resolve(null));\\n\\n  function diag(code: string, message: string): CaptureFailureDiagnostic {\\n    return { code, message, recordedGap: false };\\n  }\\n\\n  // On reload (same session continues, in-memory state wiped) reconstruct the\\n  // SAME thread from its durable id rather than re-running the launch — a\\n  // no-flag session would otherwise create a duplicate (AC-1.5). The launch\\n  // (process.argv) is the fallback when no id is remembered, which covers\\n  // --session/--continue/--resume reloads. The permanent home for reload\\n  // lineage is LHC thread metadata (Feature 2+).\\n  function launchFor(event: { reason: string }): LaunchFlags {\\n    if (event.reason === \\\"reload\\\" && rememberedThreadId !== null) {\\n      return { session: rememberedThreadId };\\n    }\\n    return parseLaunch();\\n  }\\n\\n  async function resolveForLaunch(\\n    launch: LaunchFlags,\\n    ctx: ExtensionContext,\\n  ): Promise<OpResult<ThreadRef | null>> {\\n    if (launch.resume === true) {\\n      const pickerDeps: {\\n        registryPath?: string;\\n        select: (choices: readonly ThreadChoice[]) => Promise<string | null>;\\n      } = { select: (choices) => selectThread(choices, ctx) };\\n      if (deps.registryPath !== undefined) pickerDeps.registryPath = deps.registryPath;\\n      return pickThread(ctx.cwd, pickerDeps);\\n    }\\n    const resolveDeps: { cwd: string; registryPath?: string; newThreadFilePath: () => string } = {\\n      cwd: ctx.cwd,\\n      newThreadFilePath,\\n    };\\n    if (deps.registryPath !== undefined) resolveDeps.registryPath = deps.registryPath;\\n    return resolveThread(launch, resolveDeps);\\n  }\\n\\n  // session_start: resolve the recording thread from the launch and construct\\n  // one background instance against it. Config is gated first so a session with\\n  // no usable inference config resolves/creates nothing (no orphan thread).\\n  const onSessionStart: PiHookHandler<\\\"session_start\\\"> = async (ctx, event) => {\\n    const config = buildSdkConfig(ctx);\\n    if (!config.ok) {\\n      lastDiagnostic = diag(\\\"instance_not_configured\\\", config.error.reason);\\n      return;\\n    }\\n    const resolved = await resolveForLaunch(launchFor(event), ctx);\\n    if (!resolved.ok) {\\n      lastDiagnostic = diag(\\\"thread_resolution_failed\\\", resolved.error.reason);\\n      return;\\n    }\\n    if (resolved.value === null) {\\n      lastDiagnostic = diag(\\\"no_thread_selected\\\", \\\"--resume resolved no thread\\\");\\n      return;\\n    }\\n    const built = await initInstance(resolved.value, config.value);\\n    if (!built.ok) {\\n      lastDiagnostic = diag(\\\"instance_init_failed\\\", built.error.reason);\\n      return;\\n    }\\n    instance = built.value;\\n    state = createSessionState(resolved.value);\\n    rememberedThreadId = threadIdOf(resolved.value);\\n  };\\n\\n  // Dispose with flush before any session swap (session_before_switch fires\\n  // pre-new/resume) and on shutdown/reload. Idempotent: disposing twice or with\\n  // no live instance is a no-op.\\n  const onDispose: PiHookHandler<Epic1Hook> = async () => {\\n    const result = await disposeInstance(instance);\\n    if (!result.ok) lastDiagnostic = diag(\\\"dispose_failed\\\", result.error.reason);\\n    instance = null;\\n  };\\n\\n  // Contain every handler: an observe-only hook must never throw back into PI\\n  // (a thrown hook breaks the user's session). A caught error becomes a\\n  // plain-data diagnostic; it is never rethrown.\\n  const guard = (\\n    name: Epic1Hook,\\n    body: PiHookHandler<Epic1Hook>,\\n  ): PiHookHandler<Epic1Hook> => {\\n    return async (ctx, event): Promise<void> => {\\n      try {\\n        await body(ctx, event);\\n      } catch (err) {\\n        lastDiagnostic = {\\n          code: \\\"hook_handler_error\\\",\\n          message: `${name}: ${err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err)}`,\\n          recordedGap: false,\\n        };\\n      }\\n    };\\n  };\\n\\n  // Observe-only foundation: capture-bearing hooks (message_end, turn_end,\\n  // agent_end, runtime changes, fork) stay no-ops in Story 1 — capture and fork\\n  // land in later stories. Each receives a FRESH ctx and retains none of it.\\n  const noop: PiHookHandler<Epic1Hook> = () => {\\n    // Intentionally empty — later stories replace this with real routing.\\n  };\\n\\n  // Bodies per hook: session lifecycle is live in Story 1; the rest are no-ops.\\n  const bodies: Record<Epic1Hook, PiHookHandler<Epic1Hook>> = {\\n    session_start: onSessionStart as PiHookHandler<Epic1Hook>,\\n    session_before_switch: onDispose,\\n    session_shutdown: onDispose,\\n    message_end: noop,\\n    turn_end: noop,\\n    agent_end: noop,\\n    model_select: noop,\\n    thinking_level_select: noop,\\n    session_before_fork: noop,\\n  };\\n\\n  const handlers = {} as Record<Epic1Hook, PiHookHandler<Epic1Hook>>;\\n  for (const name of EPIC_1_HOOKS) handlers[name] = guard(name, bodies[name]);\\n\\n  return {\\n    handlers,\\n    register(pi: ExtensionAPI): void {\\n      for (const name of EPIC_1_HOOKS) pi.registerHook(name, handlers[name]);\\n    },\\n    getState(): SessionState | null {\\n      return state;\\n    },\\n    getInstance(): LhcInstance | null {\\n      return instance;\\n    },\\n    snapshot(): ConnectorSnapshot {\\n      return { state, lastDiagnostic };\\n    },\\n  };\\n}\\n\\n/** PI entry point: PI calls this with the ExtensionAPI when the extension\\n *  loads (and again on `/reload`). Registers the observe-only hook rail with\\n *  the production defaults (the `~/.lhc` registry, `process.argv` launch flags).\\n *  The inference-backed `buildSdkConfig` is wired in Stories 5/6; until then a\\n *  live session resolves nothing and records a diagnostic rather than faking. */\\nexport function activate(pi: ExtensionAPI): void {\\n  createConnector().register(pi);\\n}\\n\\nexport default activate;\\n\"},\"caller\":{\"type\":\"direct\"}}],\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"stop_details\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":2,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":5940,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":266166,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":5940},\"output_tokens\":35,\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"inference_geo\":\"not_available\"},\"diagnostics\":null,\"context_management\":null},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"3bd99b80-3187-4d52-9c3e-b6b2c0cfc11e\",\"uuid\":\"71820be1-0492-4542-897b-b4786f11387d\",\"request_id\":\"req_011Cc2XLXTZbhz97M76SSSee\"}\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/artifacts/01-session-lifecycle-and-thread-resolution/streams/003-implementor.stdout.log:3172:{\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_016CwHShWBADCx8WWmqCHpU1\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"The file /Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/pi-lhc/src/index.ts has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)\"}]},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"3bd99b80-3187-4d52-9c3e-b6b2c0cfc11e\",\"uuid\":\"e0894166-133f-4a72-83ce-e492812f1978\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-06-14T04:31:15.401Z\",\"tool_use_result\":{\"type\":\"update\",\"filePath\":\"/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/pi-lhc/src/index.ts\",\"content\":\"// Extension entry. PI loads this module and calls `activate(pi)`; it registers\\n// the Epic 1 observe-only hook rail and routes each hook to a fail-closed\\n// handler. The connector owns only plain-data `SessionState` plus the live\\n// `LhcInstance` it constructs per session — never a PI `ctx`/session object,\\n// which PI replaces on new/resume/fork (tech design Flow 1, plain-data rule).\\nimport { mkdirSync } from \\\"node:fs\\\";\\nimport { homedir } from \\\"node:os\\\";\\nimport { join } from \\\"node:path\\\";\\nimport { randomUUID } from \\\"node:crypto\\\";\\nimport type { OpResult, SdkConfig, ThreadRef } from \\\"lhc\\\";\\nimport type {\\n  ExtensionAPI,\\n  ExtensionContext,\\n  PiHookHandler,\\n  PiHookName,\\n} from \\\"./pi/types.js\\\";\\nimport type { CaptureFailureDiagnostic, SessionState } from \\\"./lifecycle/state.js\\\";\\nimport { createSessionState } from \\\"./lifecycle/state.js\\\";\\nimport { disposeInstance, initInstance } from \\\"./lifecycle/instance.js\\\";\\nimport {\\n  parseLaunchFlags,\\n  resolveThread,\\n  type LaunchFlags,\\n} from \\\"./lifecycle/thread-resolution.js\\\";\\nimport { pickThread, type ThreadChoice } from \\\"./lifecycle/picker.js\\\";\\nimport type { LhcInstance } from \\\"./shared/instance.js\\\";\\n\\nexport { disposeInstance, initInstance, initLhc } from \\\"./lifecycle/instance.js\\\";\\nexport type { LhcInstance } from \\\"./shared/instance.js\\\";\\n\\n/** The Epic 1 observe-only hook rail: every PI hook the connector consumes.\\n *  Deliberately NOT `context` — context serving is Epic 2 and this epic\\n *  registers no handler for it (story Scope Out). */\\nexport const EPIC_1_HOOKS = [\\n  \\\"session_start\\\",\\n  \\\"message_end\\\",\\n  \\\"turn_end\\\",\\n  \\\"agent_end\\\",\\n  \\\"model_select\\\",\\n  \\\"thinking_level_select\\\",\\n  \\\"session_before_fork\\\",\\n  \\\"session_before_switch\\\",\\n  \\\"session_shutdown\\\",\\n] as const satisfies readonly PiHookName[];\\n\\nexport type Epic1Hook = (typeof EPIC_1_HOOKS)[number];\\n\\n/** What the connector needs from the host to run a session. All injectable so\\n *  the lifecycle is exercised against a real temp registry/thread in tests and\\n *  the default `~/.lhc` registry in production.\\n *\\n *  `buildSdkConfig` is the inference seam: it returns the SdkConfig (provider /\\n *  inference + policy) to construct the instance from. In Story 1 the real\\n *  config — the PI-model-registry-backed `ModelCall` and assignments — is not\\n *  wired yet (that is Stories 5/6), so the default fails closed; the connector\\n *  then resolves nothing and records a diagnostic rather than faking a session.\\n */\\nexport interface ConnectorDeps {\\n  buildSdkConfig?: (ctx: ExtensionContext) => OpResult<SdkConfig>;\\n  registryPath?: string;\\n  newThreadFilePath?: () => string;\\n  parseLaunch?: () => LaunchFlags;\\n  selectThread?: (\\n    choices: readonly ThreadChoice[],\\n    ctx: ExtensionContext,\\n  ) => Promise<string | null>;\\n}\\n\\n/** Everything the connector retains across hooks as PLAIN data — by\\n *  construction structuredClone-able. The live `LhcInstance` is held separately\\n *  in the closure (it is the connector's own engine, rebuilt on reload) and is\\n *  deliberately not part of the snapshot. */\\nexport interface ConnectorSnapshot {\\n  state: SessionState | null;\\n  lastDiagnostic: CaptureFailureDiagnostic | null;\\n}\\n\\nexport interface Connector {\\n  /** Register the observe-only hook rail on the PI extension API. */\\n  register(pi: ExtensionAPI): void;\\n  /** The plain-data state retained across hooks. `null` until a session\\n   *  resolves a thread; never holds a PI ctx. */\\n  getState(): SessionState | null;\\n  /** The live LHC instance for the current session, or `null`. A test/inspection\\n   *  seam — held by the connector but never part of the plain-data snapshot. */\\n  getInstance(): LhcInstance | null;\\n  /** Plain-data snapshot of all retained state (inspection/test seam). */\\n  snapshot(): ConnectorSnapshot;\\n  /** The hook handlers, keyed by event — exposed so tests can drive them with\\n   *  synthetic ctx/events without a live PI. */\\n  readonly handlers: Readonly<Record<Epic1Hook, PiHookHandler<Epic1Hook>>>;\\n}\\n\\nconst DEFAULT_LHC_DIR = join(homedir(), \\\".lhc\\\");\\n\\n/** Inference host not wired yet (Story 5/6): fail closed so a session resolves\\n *  nothing and records a diagnostic instead of constructing a faked instance. */\\nfunction inferenceNotConfigured(): OpResult<SdkConfig> {\\n  return {\\n    ok: false,\\n    error: {\\n      errorClass: \\\"system_error\\\",\\n      code: \\\"not_implemented\\\",\\n      reason:\\n        \\\"pi-lhc inference host not wired yet (Epic 1 Story 5); SDK construction deferred\\\",\\n    },\\n  };\\n}\\n\\n/** Default new-thread file location: `~/.lhc/threads/<uuid>.sqlite`. The id is\\n *  generated by the registry at creation, so the file name is just a unique\\n *  handle; the directory is ensured up front (DatabaseSync does not create\\n *  parents). */\\nfunction defaultNewThreadFilePath(): string {\\n  const dir = join(DEFAULT_LHC_DIR, \\\"threads\\\");\\n  mkdirSync(dir, { recursive: true });\\n  return join(dir, `${randomUUID()}.sqlite`);\\n}\\n\\nfunction threadIdOf(ref: ThreadRef): string | null {\\n  return \\\"threadId\\\" in ref ? ref.threadId : null;\\n}\\n\\n/** Build a connector instance. Its mutable state lives in this closure; the\\n *  registered handlers capture it, so the instance stays live for the session\\n *  even when `activate` discards the returned object. */\\nexport function createConnector(deps: ConnectorDeps = {}): Connector {\\n  // The connector's retained state. `state` is plain data (the durable thread\\n  // reference + flags); `instance` is the live SDK engine (rebuilt on reload);\\n  // `rememberedThreadId` is the durable id the reload path reattaches by; and\\n  // `lastDiagnostic` is the plain-data health signal. None is a PI object.\\n  let state: SessionState | null = null;\\n  let instance: LhcInstance | null = null;\\n  let rememberedThreadId: string | null = null;\\n  let lastDiagnostic: CaptureFailureDiagnostic | null = null;\\n\\n  const buildSdkConfig = deps.buildSdkConfig ?? (() => inferenceNotConfigured());\\n  const newThreadFilePath = deps.newThreadFilePath ?? defaultNewThreadFilePath;\\n  const parseLaunch = deps.parseLaunch ?? (() => parseLaunchFlags(process.argv));\\n  const selectThread =\\n    deps.selectThread ?? ((): Promise<string | null> => Promise.resolve(null));\\n\\n  function diag(code: string, message: string): CaptureFailureDiagnostic {\\n    return { code, message, recordedGap: false };\\n  }\\n\\n  // On reload (same session continues, in-memory state wiped) reconstruct the\\n  // SAME thread from its durable id rather than re-running the launch — a\\n  // no-flag session would otherwise create a duplicate (AC-1.5). The launch\\n  // (process.argv) is the fallback when no id is remembered, which covers\\n  // --session/--continue/--resume reloads. The permanent home for reload\\n  // lineage is LHC thread metadata (Feature 2+).\\n  function launchFor(event: { reason: string }): LaunchFlags {\\n    if (event.reason === \\\"reload\\\" && rememberedThreadId !== null) {\\n      return { session: rememberedThreadId };\\n    }\\n    return parseLaunch();\\n  }\\n\\n  async function resolveForLaunch(\\n    launch: LaunchFlags,\\n    ctx: ExtensionContext,\\n  ): Promise<OpResult<ThreadRef | null>> {\\n    if (launch.resume === true) {\\n      const pickerDeps: {\\n        registryPath?: string;\\n        select: (choices: readonly ThreadChoice[]) => Promise<string | null>;\\n      } = { select: (choices) => selectThread(choices, ctx) };\\n      if (deps.registryPath !== undefined) pickerDeps.registryPath = deps.registryPath;\\n      return pickThread(ctx.cwd, pickerDeps);\\n    }\\n    const resolveDeps: { cwd: string; registryPath?: string; newThreadFilePath: () => string } = {\\n      cwd: ctx.cwd,\\n      newThreadFilePath,\\n    };\\n    if (deps.registryPath !== undefined) resolveDeps.registryPath = deps.registryPath;\\n    return resolveThread(launch, resolveDeps);\\n  }\\n\\n  // session_start: resolve the recording thread from the launch and construct\\n  // one background instance against it. Config is gated first so a session with\\n  // no usable inference config resolves/creates nothing (no orphan thread).\\n  const onSessionStart: PiHookHandler<\\\"session_start\\\"> = async (ctx, event) => {\\n    const config = buildSdkConfig(ctx);\\n    if (!config.ok) {\\n      lastDiagnostic = diag(\\\"instance_not_configured\\\", config.error.reason);\\n      return;\\n    }\\n    const resolved = await resolveForLaunch(launchFor(event), ctx);\\n    if (!resolved.ok) {\\n      lastDiagnostic = diag(\\\"thread_resolution_failed\\\", resolved.error.reason);\\n      return;\\n    }\\n    if (resolved.value === null) {\\n      lastDiagnostic = diag(\\\"no_thread_selected\\\", \\\"--resume resolved no thread\\\");\\n      return;\\n    }\\n    const built = await initInstance(resolved.value, config.value);\\n    if (!built.ok) {\\n      lastDiagnostic = diag(\\\"instance_init_failed\\\", built.error.reason);\\n      return;\\n    }\\n    instance = built.value;\\n    state = createSessionState(resolved.value);\\n    rememberedThreadId = threadIdOf(resolved.value);\\n  };\\n\\n  // Dispose with flush before any session swap (session_before_switch fires\\n  // pre-new/resume) and on shutdown/reload. Idempotent: disposing twice or with\\n  // no live instance is a no-op.\\n  const onDispose: PiHookHandler<Epic1Hook> = async () => {\\n    const result = await disposeInstance(instance);\\n    if (!result.ok) lastDiagnostic = diag(\\\"dispose_failed\\\", result.error.reason);\\n    instance = null;\\n  };\\n\\n  // Contain every handler: an observe-only hook must never throw back into PI\\n  // (a thrown hook breaks the user's session). A caught error becomes a\\n  // plain-data diagnostic; it is never rethrown.\\n  const guard = (\\n    name: Epic1Hook,\\n    body: PiHookHandler<Epic1Hook>,\\n  ): PiHookHandler<Epic1Hook> => {\\n    return async (ctx, event): Promise<void> => {\\n      try {\\n        await body(ctx, event);\\n      } catch (err) {\\n        lastDiagnostic = {\\n          code: \\\"hook_handler_error\\\",\\n          message: `${name}: ${err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err)}`,\\n          recordedGap: false,\\n        };\\n      }\\n    };\\n  };\\n\\n  // Observe-only foundation: capture-bearing hooks (message_end, turn_end,\\n  // agent_end, runtime changes, fork) stay no-ops in Story 1 — capture and fork\\n  // land in later stories. Each receives a FRESH ctx and retains none of it.\\n  const noop: PiHookHandler<Epic1Hook> = () => {\\n    // Intentionally empty — later stories replace this with real routing.\\n  };\\n\\n  // Bodies per hook: session lifecycle is live in Story 1; the rest are no-ops.\\n  const bodies: Record<Epic1Hook, PiHookHandler<Epic1Hook>> = {\\n    session_start: onSessionStart as PiHookHandler<Epic1Hook>,\\n    session_before_switch: onDispose,\\n    session_shutdown: onDispose,\\n    message_end: noop,\\n    turn_end: noop,\\n    agent_end: noop,\\n    model_select: noop,\\n    thinking_level_select: noop,\\n    session_before_fork: noop,\\n  };\\n\\n  const handlers = {} as Record<Epic1Hook, PiHookHandler<Epic1Hook>>;\\n  for (const name of EPIC_1_HOOKS) handlers[name] = guard(name, bodies[name]);\\n\\n  return {\\n    handlers,\\n    register(pi: ExtensionAPI): void {\\n      for (const name of EPIC_1_HOOKS) pi.registerHook(name, handlers[name]);\\n    },\\n    getState(): SessionState | null {\\n      return state;\\n    },\\n    getInstance(): LhcInstance | null {\\n      return instance;\\n    },\\n    snapshot(): ConnectorSnapshot {\\n      return { state, lastDiagnostic };\\n    },\\n  };\\n}\\n\\n/** PI entry point: PI calls this with the ExtensionAPI when the extension\\n *  loads (and again on `/reload`). Registers the observe-only hook rail with\\n *  the production defaults (the `~/.lhc` registry, `process.argv` launch flags).\\n *  The inference-backed `buildSdkConfig` is wired in Stories 5/6; until then a\\n *  live session resolves nothing and records a diagnostic rather than faking. */\\nexport function activate(pi: ExtensionAPI): void {\\n  createConnector().register(pi);\\n}\\n\\nexport default activate;\\n\",\"structuredPatch\":[{\"oldStart\":1,\"oldLines\":14,\"newStart\":1,\"newLines\":29,\"lines\":[\" // Extension entry. PI loads this module and calls `activate(pi)`; it registe